window.GENERA_INDEX = {"aardonyx":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-23","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"about 200–190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Anchisauria","Aardonyx"],"diet":"Herbivore","locomotion":"Habitually bipedal; capable of quadrupedal walking","description":"Aardonyx was a large early sauropodomorph from Early Jurassic South Africa. Its jaws and limbs preserve a combination of features associated with mostly bipedal ancestors and the bulk-feeding, quadrupedal sauropods that evolved later.","facts":["The holotype BP/1/6254 is the rostral half of a left maxilla.","A nearby caudal left-maxilla fragment, BP/1/6505, may be part of the same bone as the holotype.","Referred material from Marc’s Quarry includes cranial and mandibular elements, cervical, dorsal, sacral and caudal vertebrae, ribs, gastralia, chevrons, girdle elements, and bones of the forelimbs, hindlimbs, hands and feet.","The quarry assemblage appears to represent two immature individuals; the smaller individual is about 85 per cent of the larger in linear postcranial dimensions.","Histology of a rib and scapular fragment showed continuing growth and suggested that the sampled individual or individuals may have been younger than ten years at death.","The disarticulated material supports a broad anatomical reconstruction, but specimen association, adult proportions and soft tissues remain uncertain."]},"ageReviewNote":"The profile follows the Early Jurassic upper Elliot Formation assignment of the Marc’s Quarry type assemblage. The original description did not provide a narrow numerical radiometric age for the quarry.","residualUncertainty":["The referred bones are disarticulated rather than preserved as two associated skeletons.","Both reconstructed individuals were immature, so adult body proportions and maximum size are not directly known.","BP/1/6505 may belong to the holotype maxilla, but the two fragments do not overlap.","No direct skin, colour, gut-content or other soft-tissue evidence is known.","The exact phylogenetic position of Aardonyx near the origin of Sauropoda varies among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2009.1440","https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC2842739","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/aardonyx.html"]},"abelisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 83-80 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauridae"],"description":"Abelisaurus comahuensis was a large abelisaurid theropod from the Anacleto Formation of Patagonia. The genus is based on a single incomplete skull; no confidently associated postcranial skeleton is known.","facts":["The holotype is a partial skull lacking much of the snout and lower jaws.","Its body proportions, size and external covering must largely be inferred from other abelisaurids."],"length":null,"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Anacleto Formation is generally dated to the early Campanian.","residualUncertainty":["Only one specimen is securely known.","Its detailed position within Abelisauridae is unstable."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Bonaparte%20%26amp%3B%20Novas%201985.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2011.01055.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/abelisaurus.html"]},"achelousaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 75-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae"],"description":"Achelousaurus horneri was a centrosaurine ceratopsid from the upper Two Medicine Formation of Montana. Several skulls and less complete postcranial remains show adults with roughened nasal and brow bosses rather than long horns.","facts":["The sample includes three relatively complete adult skulls plus younger individuals.","The proposed evolutionary sequence involving Einiosaurus and Pachyrhinosaurus remains a phylogenetic interpretation, not a directly observed lineage."]},"ageReviewNote":"Type-locality evidence places the genus in the late Campanian upper Two Medicine Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred juvenile material is less diagnostic.","Relationships among derived centrosaurines differ among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.jstor.org/stable/1306481","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/achelousaurus.html"]},"achillobator":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 98-83 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae"],"description":"Achillobator giganticus was a large dromaeosaurid from the Bayan Shireh Formation of Mongolia. It is based on one disarticulated partial skeleton; the association of all elements and its exact age within the formation remain uncertain.","facts":["Preserved material includes jaws, vertebrae, pelvis and limb bones, but no complete articulated skeleton.","A large sickle-claw-bearing second toe is supported by its dromaeosaurid anatomy; hunting behaviour is not preserved."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Bayan Shireh Formation spans a broad mid- to Late Cretaceous interval and is not dated narrowly at the type locality.","residualUncertainty":["The association of every listed element has been questioned.","The formation age and exact dromaeosaurid position remain broad."],"sourceUrls":["https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/5e81f126-0bc9-4a56-81f6-675a3c027f9c","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/achillobator.html"]},"acrocanthosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 115-108 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Carcharodontosauria"],"description":"Acrocanthosaurus atokensis was a very large carcharodontosaurian theropod from Aptian-Albian rocks of the south-central United States. Several partial skeletons, including one with much of the skull and postcranium, make its tall neural spines and general anatomy well documented.","facts":["No specimen is literally complete, but NCSM 14345 preserves much of the skull and skeleton.","The elongated neural spines supported a raised ridge of soft tissue; its display or physiological role is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure records come chiefly from the Antlers and Twin Mountains formations, around the Aptian-Albian boundary.","residualUncertainty":["The function and external form of the dorsal ridge are unknown.","Some isolated eastern North American referrals are uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://nmdigital.unm.edu/digital/collection/bulletins/id/1053/","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3061882/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/acrocanthosaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2012.03.003"]},"aegyptosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Titanosauria"],"description":"Aegyptosaurus baharijensis was a titanosaur from Cenomanian rocks of Egypt. Its name-bearing bones were fragmentary and were destroyed during the Second World War, so much of its diagnosis now depends on Stromer's descriptions and illustrations.","facts":["The type series included vertebrae and limb bones, not a complete skeleton.","Material from elsewhere in North Africa has been referred historically, but not all referrals can be demonstrated to belong to the type species."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type material came from the Cenomanian Bahariya Formation.","residualUncertainty":["The type material is destroyed.","The taxon needs modern specimen-based reassessment.","Many historical referrals are insecure."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/28682600","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/aegyptosaurus.html"]},"afrovenator":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"about 168–161 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Megalosauridae","Afrovenatorinae"],"description":"Afrovenator was a large megalosaurid theropod from the Tiourarén Formation of Niger. It is represented by one relatively complete partial skeleton, including substantial cranial and postcranial material. The formation was initially described as Early Cretaceous but is now most plausibly regarded as late Middle Jurassic.","facts":["Known primarily from the holotype MNN TIG1 (formerly UC OBA 1), a relatively complete partial skull and skeleton.","The preserved material includes much of the skull, parts of the vertebral column, forelimbs and hands, much of the pelvis, and most of the hind limbs.","A 2009 reassessment concluded that the Tiourarén dinosaur fauna is probably late Middle Jurassic rather than Early Cretaceous.","Afrovenator is placed within Megalosauridae; its exact position within that family varies among phylogenetic analyses."]},"ageReviewNote":"The profile follows the late Middle Jurassic interpretation of the Tiourarén Formation. PBDB spans a broader interval because its occurrence set includes records and formation assignments with different age resolutions.","residualUncertainty":["The formation lacks a direct radiometric date.","Only one principal skeleton is known.","Exact placement within Megalosauridae varies among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.266.5183.267","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2008.10.019","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927"]},"agilisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"about 168-163 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia"],"description":"Agilisaurus louderbacki was a small neornithischian from the lower Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan, China. Its holotype is a largely complete articulated skeleton with a skull, although parts of the forelimbs and other elements are incomplete.","facts":["The single principal skeleton provides unusually good anatomical evidence for a small Middle Jurassic ornithischian.","It was bipedal, but diet beyond broad herbivory or omnivory cannot be demonstrated directly."],"diet":"Herbivore or omnivore (uncertain)","locomotion":"on 2 legs"},"ageReviewNote":"The lower Shaximiao Formation is Middle Jurassic, commonly Bathonian-Callovian, rather than Late Jurassic.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal individual is known.","Its position near the base of Neornithischia differs among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2005)025%5B0823:SODFTM%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/agilisaurus.html"]},"alamosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72–66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Titanosauria","Lithostrotia"],"description":"Alamosaurus sanjuanensis was a titanosaurian sauropod from Maastrichtian rocks of the south-western United States. The name is founded on an isolated shoulder blade. Many partial skeletons, vertebrae and limb bones have historically been referred to it, but the extent of that referred material is now under renewed taxonomic debate. It is one of the youngest named sauropods from North America, not the continent's only Late Cretaceous sauropod occurrence.","facts":["The holotype USNM 10486 is a left scapula; an ischium found nearby, USNM 10487, is the paratype.","USNM 15560 from Utah was long treated as an incomplete referred Alamosaurus skeleton; a 2025 paper proposed separating it as Utetitan zellaguymondeweyae, a proposal not yet established as broad consensus.","BIBE 45854 from Texas preserves an articulated cervical series historically referred to Alamosaurus; the same 2025 revision questioned how much of the Texas material belongs to the type species.","Some fragmentary bones indicate very large adults, but precise maximum length and mass remain uncertain because no giant individual is represented by a complete skeleton.","Osteoderm fragments associated with referred material show that at least some individuals carried bony skin deposits; their arrangement over the body is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure and widely accepted records are Maastrichtian. Some older south-western North American sauropod material has been referred to Alamosaurus, but those referrals should not extend the headline range without specimen-level review.","residualUncertainty":["Many referrals are isolated and cannot all be compared directly with the type specimen.","The proposed separation of Utetitan is recent and has not yet been tested widely in subsequent phylogenetic work.","Maximum adult size is extrapolated from incomplete individuals.","The distribution and external appearance of the osteoderms are unknown.","Precise relationships within derived Titanosauria vary among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2011.595856","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2016.1183150","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2014.901334","https://giw.utahgeology.org/giw/index.php/GIW/article/view/156"]},"albertaceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 78 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae"],"description":"Albertaceratops nesmoi was a centrosaurine ceratopsid from the Oldman Formation of Alberta. It is securely represented by a nearly complete skull; much of the postcranial material first associated with it came from a different taxon later named Medusaceratops.","facts":["The long brow horns and low nasal ornament are documented by the holotype skull.","The genus should not be described as known from a complete skeleton or extensive monospecific bonebed."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality lies in the middle Campanian Oldman Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Postcranial referrals are limited.","Some cranial referrals and centrosaurine relationships remain under study."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1666/0022-3360(2007)81%5B376:ANBCCF%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.scotthartman.info/publication/ryan-2010-new/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/albertaceratops.html"]},"albertosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 71-68 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Tyrannosauridae","Albertosaurinae"],"description":"Albertosaurus sarcophagus was an albertosaurine tyrannosaurid from the lower Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta. Numerous skulls and skeletons spanning growth stages, including a multi-individual bonebed, make it one of the better represented large theropods.","facts":["The Dry Island bonebed preserves many individuals but does not by itself prove coordinated pack hunting.","Its lighter build and long hind limbs relative to Tyrannosaurus are anatomically supported; precise speed is not."]},"ageReviewNote":"Well-supported A. sarcophagus occurrences are early Maastrichtian in the lower Horseshoe Canyon Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Bonebed formation and social behaviour are not equivalent.","Some historical records outside Alberta belong to other tyrannosaurids."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3737824","https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02699","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/albertosaurus.html","https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/a02fc8e6-2c29-45cd-a95a-271d17d1786f","https://doi.org/10.1139/E10-035"]},"alectrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 96-83 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea"],"description":"Alectrosaurus olseni was a medium-sized tyrannosauroid from the Iren Dabasu Formation of Inner Mongolia. The name is anchored by an associated hind limb; much cranial and forelimb material once assigned to it belongs to other theropods.","facts":["The secure type material is chiefly a partial hind limb and foot.","Recent work supports tyrannosauroid affinities but cannot recover a complete body from the limited diagnostic material."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Iren Dabasu Formation has a debated, broad Late Cretaceous age, probably within the Cenomanian-Campanian interval.","residualUncertainty":["Formation age is poorly constrained.","Historical cranial and forelimb referrals are excluded.","Its exact tyrannosauroid position is unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1989.10011737","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2023.2199817","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/alectrosaurus.html"]},"alioramus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Tyrannosauridae","Alioramini"],"description":"Alioramus was a long-snouted tyrannosaurid from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. A. remotus is based on a fragmentary skull and foot bones, while the better-preserved A. altai provides most detailed anatomical information.","facts":["A. altai preserves a substantially complete skull and partial skeleton of a subadult.","The distinctive row of low nasal rugosities is real; their display role is an inference."]},"ageReviewNote":"Both named species come from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level differences may include ontogenetic effects.","Adult body size is not securely known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/770.1","https://doi.org/10.1206/810.1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/alioramus.html"]},"allosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 155-148 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Allosauridae"],"description":"Allosaurus was a large allosaurid theropod abundant in the Morrison Formation of western North America. Many skulls and skeletons document its anatomy and growth, although the boundaries and diagnoses of named species remain under active revision.","facts":["A. fragilis and A. jimmadseni are widely recognised North American species.","Bonebeds and healed injuries provide population-level evidence but do not establish pack hunting or a single feeding strategy."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure North American material is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian in the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Species taxonomy and historical referrals require specimen-level treatment.","Social and hunting behaviour are not directly established."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.7803","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.8493","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/allosaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.5479/si.03629236.110.i"]},"alvarezsaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 86-83 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Alvarezsauria"],"description":"Alvarezsaurus calvoi was a small alvarezsaurian theropod from the Bajo de la Carpa Formation of Patagonia. It is known from one incomplete, mostly postcranial skeleton; better-known anatomy of later alvarezsaurids should not be copied directly onto it.","facts":["The holotype includes vertebrae, pelvis and hind-limb material but no diagnostic complete skull or forelimb.","Its bipedal stance is secure, whereas diet and specialised digging behaviour are inferred from the wider clade."],"diet":null,"locomotion":"on 2 legs"},"ageReviewNote":"The Bajo de la Carpa Formation is usually regarded as Santonian.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is known.","Its position within early Alvarezsauria varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2021.104830","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/alvarezsaurus.html"]},"amargasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 130-120 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Dicraeosauridae"],"description":"Amargasaurus cazaui was a dicraeosaurid sauropod from the La Amarga Formation of Argentina. One partial skeleton preserves much of the vertebral column, including paired, extremely tall cervical neural spines, plus skull and limb elements.","facts":["The holotype is relatively complete for a dicraeosaurid but is not a full articulated skeleton.","The neural spines supported soft tissue, but competing sail, sheath and display reconstructions are not decisively resolved."],"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Puesto Antigual Member of the La Amarga Formation is Barremian-early Aptian.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal skeleton is known.","The soft-tissue covering and function of the neck spines are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/joa.13659","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6362061/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/amargasaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2014.838174"]},"ammosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"about 201–190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Anchisauria","Anchisaurus"],"taxonomicStatus":"junior synonym of Anchisaurus polyzelus","acceptedName":false,"description":"Ammosaurus major is a historical name for Early Jurassic sauropodomorph material from the Portland Formation of Connecticut. Detailed revision found its type and the other Manchester quarry skeletons to belong to Anchisaurus polyzelus, making Ammosaurus a junior subjective synonym rather than a separate accepted genus.","facts":["Marsh named the material Anchisaurus major in 1889 and erected Ammosaurus for it in 1891.","Yates's 2010 revision found the Manchester skeletons conspecific and referred them to the older name Anchisaurus polyzelus.","The International Commission on Zoological Nomenclature designated the almost complete YPM 1883 skeleton as the neotype of Anchisaurus polyzelus in 2015, stabilising that name.","This page is retained to explain the historical name and should not be counted as a separate valid dinosaur genus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed range follows the Early Jurassic Portland Formation material on which Ammosaurus and Anchisaurus were based. Broader records assigned to the historical name should not be treated as proof of a longer-lived separate genus.","residualUncertainty":["Junior synonymy is a taxonomic conclusion rather than an objective identity between the original name-bearing specimens.","The exact position of Anchisaurus near the origin of Sauropoda differs among phylogenetic analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://elischolar.library.yale.edu/peabody_museum_natural_history_postilla/230/","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2010.00952.x","https://www.biotaxa.org/bzn/article/view/37953"]},"ampelosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Maastrichtian, about 71.5 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Titanosauria","Lithostrotia","Lirainosaurinae"],"description":"Ampelosaurus atacis was an early Maastrichtian titanosaur from the Bellevue bonebed in southern France. The holotype is three connected dorsal vertebrae, while abundant referred material includes other vertebrae, girdle and limb bones, teeth and several forms of osteoderm. The bonebed contains multiple individuals, but not every titanosaur bone from Bellevue can automatically be assigned to Ampelosaurus.","facts":["The holotype MDE C3-247 consists of three connected middle dorsal vertebrae from the Bellevue locality.","The original diagnosis also drew on abundant disarticulated postcranial bones and plate-, bulb- and spine-shaped osteoderms.","The site preserves many individuals rather than one complete skeleton; later work has cautioned that more than one titanosaur morphotype may be present.","Bone histology shows prolonged growth and extensive remodelling, while exact adult size estimates remain dependent on associating isolated limb bones correctly.","Ampelosaurus is usually recovered among European lithostrotian titanosaurs, commonly within Lirainosaurinae."]},"ageReviewNote":"The headline age is restricted to the early Maastrichtian Bellevue type locality. Wider French and Spanish referrals need specimen-level taxonomic review and should not silently extend the range of the genus.","residualUncertainty":["The Bellevue accumulation may include more than one titanosaur taxon.","Many bones are disarticulated, complicating individual and species association.","The full arrangement of osteoderms is unknown.","Precise placement within Lithostrotia varies among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/LeLoeuff_95.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0036907","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2015.08.009"]},"amygdalodon":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"late Toarcian–early Aalenian, about 182–173 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda"],"length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Amygdalodon patagonicus is an early sauropod from the Cerro Carnerero Formation of Patagonia, dated from the late Toarcian to early Aalenian across the Early–Middle Jurassic boundary. Its type assemblage is fragmentary and mixed: the sauropod bones represent at least two individuals, and one included element belongs to an indeterminate vertebrate. A posterior dorsal vertebra was selected as the lectotype.","facts":["Rauhut's 2003 revision showed that the original type material is not a single partial skeleton.","The assemblage contains sauropod bones from at least two individuals plus one bone from another, indeterminate vertebrate.","A posterior dorsal vertebra was designated as the lectotype to anchor the name Amygdalodon patagonicus.","Its position is close to the origin of Eusauropoda: Rauhut placed it within basal Eusauropoda, while the dental phylogeny of Carballido and Pol recovered it just outside that clade.","Amygdalodon is important evidence that early sauropods had reached South America by the Early to early Middle Jurassic."]},"ageReviewNote":"Updated Museo de La Plata catalogue data report a late Toarcian–early Aalenian age. This crosses the Early–Middle Jurassic boundary, so the catalogue period label is necessarily simplified.","residualUncertainty":["The type assemblage is disarticulated and contains more than one individual.","The formation age is broad and not resolved to a narrow numerical interval.","The lectotype is incomplete, limiting the genus-level diagnosis.","A published full-body length is necessarily a loose extrapolation."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1002/mmng.20030060110","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crpv.2010.01.003","https://palaeo-electronica.org/content/2013-technical/410-museo-de-la-plata-catalogue"]},"anchiceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae"],"description":"Anchiceratops ornatus was a chasmosaurine ceratopsid from the Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta. Multiple skulls and some partial skeletons document a short, ornamented frill, but proposed additional species and sexual differences are not securely supported.","facts":["The genus is represented by numerous skulls with variable frill shape.","Variation may reflect age, individual differences or stratigraphy rather than separate sexes."]},"ageReviewNote":"Most diagnostic material is upper Campanian to lower Maastrichtian within the Horseshoe Canyon Formation.","residualUncertainty":["The interpretation of skull variation remains debated.","Some referred material lacks precise stratigraphic control."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2011.601484","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/anchiceratops.html"]},"anchiornis":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 161 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Paraves","Anchiornithidae"],"description":"Anchiornis huxleyi was a small feathered paravian from the Oxfordian Tiaojishan Formation of northeastern China. Hundreds of articulated specimens preserve bones and extensive plumage, including long feathers on all four limbs.","facts":["Exceptional soft-tissue preservation supports detailed plumage descriptions.","Published colour reconstructions infer melanosome-based colours, but individual and body-region sampling limit how universal a single reconstruction is."],"locomotion":"bipedal; aerial capability debated"},"ageReviewNote":"Radiometric work places the relevant Tiaojishan beds near 160.9-160.3 million years ago.","residualUncertainty":["Its exact position among early paravians varies.","Flight capability and colour reconstruction are model-dependent."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1186290","https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0090-411.1.1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/anchiornis.html"]},"anchisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"about 201-190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Anchisauria"],"description":"Anchisaurus polyzelus was a small early sauropodomorph from the Portland Formation of Connecticut and Massachusetts. Its anatomy is based on several incomplete skeletons, including the neotype YPM 1883; Ammosaurus is a junior synonym.","facts":["The accepted species spelling is polyzelus, not polyzelous.","The Manchester quarry material formerly named Ammosaurus major belongs to Anchisaurus in the modern revision."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Portland Formation material is Early Jurassic, broadly Hettangian-Sinemurian.","residualUncertainty":["Its exact phylogenetic position near Sauropoda varies.","Some historical referrals require renewed comparison."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2010.00952.x","https://www.biotaxa.org/bzn/article/view/37953","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/anchisaurus.html"]},"ankylosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 68-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae","Ankylosaurinae"],"description":"Ankylosaurus magniventris was a very large ankylosaurid from latest Cretaceous rocks of western North America. Several incomplete skeletons and skulls collectively document its broad body, armour and tail club, but no single complete skeleton is known.","facts":["The tail club is known from referred material and was capable of powerful lateral motion.","Much familiar full-body restoration combines non-overlapping specimens and comparison with relatives."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure specimens are late Maastrichtian, principally from the Hell Creek, Lance and Scollard formations.","residualUncertainty":["No complete articulated skeleton is known.","Exact armour placement and maximum size remain estimates."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/facets-2017-0063","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/ankylosaurus.html","https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/handle/2246/1435","https://doi.org/10.1139/e04-043"]},"anserimimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria","Ornithomimidae"],"description":"Anserimimus planinychus was an ornithomimid from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. Its type is an incomplete skeleton with a distinctive, robust forelimb; no complete skull is securely known.","facts":["The broad hand bones inspired the species name but do not reveal a specific feeding behaviour.","A bipedal, cursorial body plan is supported by ornithomimid anatomy; exact diet is uncertain."],"diet":"diet uncertain; omnivory or herbivory plausible"},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality is in the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Only one diagnostic partial skeleton is securely established.","Some referred material is not fully described."],"sourceUrls":["https://artscimedia.case.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/108/2017/05/17211204/JPSK-2006-Kobayashi-Barsbold-ornithomimids-from-nemegt-Fm-LK-MONG.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/anserimimus.html"]},"antarctosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 83-78 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauria"],"description":"Antarctosaurus wichmannianus was a titanosaur from the Anacleto Formation of Patagonia. Its holotype is a disarticulated collection of cranial and postcranial bones whose association is uncertain, so it cannot support a confident complete-body reconstruction.","facts":["The type includes braincase, jaw, vertebral and limb elements found in association but not articulation.","Other species historically placed in Antarctosaurus are not automatically part of A. wichmannianus and several have been reassigned or regarded as dubious."],"taxonomicStatus":"validity disputed because the fragmentary type assemblage may not be a single individual","length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Anacleto Formation is early Campanian.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype may combine more than one individual.","The genus-level diagnosis and phylogenetic position require revision.","Historical species referrals are heterogeneous."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2010.0011","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/antarctosaurus.html"]},"apatosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 154-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Diplodocidae","Apatosaurinae"],"description":"Apatosaurus was a robust diplodocid sauropod from the Morrison Formation of western North America. Multiple partial skeletons document most of the body, but skulls are rare and species-level referrals have been repeatedly revised.","facts":["A. ajax is the type species; A. louisae is also widely accepted.","Brontosaurus was synonymised with Apatosaurus for much of the twentieth century, but a 2015 specimen-level analysis revived it as a separate genus; that result is influential but still tested in later work."]},"ageReviewNote":"Diagnostic material is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian within the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Species and genus boundaries among apatosaurines remain debated.","Some historical skull and skeleton associations are uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.857","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/apatosaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.5962/p.228587"]},"aquilops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 109-104 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia"],"description":"Aquilops americanus was a small early neoceratopsian from the Cloverly Formation of Montana. It is known from one partial skull, important for ceratopsian biogeography but insufficient for a detailed full-body reconstruction.","facts":["The hooked rostral bone and associated skull elements diagnose the genus.","Body size and posture are inferred from relatives because no postcranial bones are known."],"length":null,"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant Cloverly beds are late Aptian to early Albian.","residualUncertainty":["Only one partial skull is known.","The individual was not fully mature.","Its exact position among early neoceratopsians is weakly resolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0112055","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/aquilops.html"]},"aragosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 145-140 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes"],"description":"Aragosaurus ischiaticus was an early titanosauriform sauropod from the Villar del Arzobispo Formation of Spain, close to the Jurassic-Cretaceous boundary. Its holotype is an associated but incomplete postcranial skeleton, not an Early Cretaceous animal securely dated to 130-120 million years ago.","facts":["The known skeleton includes vertebrae and limb-girdle elements but no skull.","Revised stratigraphy moved the type locality from a traditional Barremian assignment to the Tithonian-Berriasian interval."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality is now placed in the Tithonian-Berriasian Villar del Arzobispo Formation.","residualUncertainty":["The type locality is not directly radiometrically dated.","Its exact position within early Titanosauriformes varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/zoj.12144","https://doi.org/10.1017/S0016756811000732","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/aragosaurus.html"]},"aralosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 86-83 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae","Aralosaurini"],"description":"Aralosaurus tuberiferus was a lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from the Bostobe Formation of Kazakhstan. It is based mainly on a fragmentary posterior skull and jaws; the rest of its body is poorly documented.","facts":["Modern revision identified a hollow-crested lambeosaurine affinity from the preserved skull anatomy.","A complete crest, skeleton and precise body dimensions are not known."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Bostobe Formation is generally Santonian to early Campanian, but the type horizon is not narrowly dated.","residualUncertainty":["The formation age is broad.","Its detailed relationship to Canardia and other aralosaurins remains analysis-dependent."],"sourceUrls":["https://biblio.naturalsciences.be/rbins-publications/bulletin-of-the-royal-belgian-institute-of-natural-sciences-earth-sciences/74-sup-2004/irscnb_p4087_022b9dx_74-2_bulletin-10.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0069835","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/aralosaurus.html"]},"archaeoceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 125-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia"],"description":"Archaeoceratops was a small early neoceratopsian from the Xinminpu Group of Gansu, China. Several partial skulls and skeletons represent two named species and document a largely bipedal animal without the elaborate horns of later ceratopsids.","facts":["A. oshimai is represented by multiple partial individuals; A. yujingziensis is a second species described from the same regional unit.","Broad herbivory is supported by dental anatomy, but exact diet and social behaviour are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Xinminpu Group localities are Aptian-Albian but their individual horizons are not all dated precisely.","residualUncertainty":["Local stratigraphic ages are broad.","Basal neoceratopsian relationships vary among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://app.pan.pl/archive/published/app48/app48-261.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2025.2568096","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/archaeoceratops.html"]},"archaeopteryx":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 151-148 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Avialae","Archaeopterygidae"],"description":"Archaeopteryx was a feathered early avialan from the Solnhofen Archipelago limestones of Germany. More than a dozen skeletal specimens, many with flight-feather impressions, document a mosaic of avian and non-avian theropod features.","facts":["Asymmetrical wing feathers support aerodynamic function, while flight style remains debated.","The number and limits of Archaeopteryx species are contested; not every specimen is necessarily conspecific."],"locomotion":"bipedal; capable of active flight, exact flight style debated"},"ageReviewNote":"The principal Solnhofen and Altmuehltal specimens are late Kimmeridgian to early Tithonian.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level taxonomy is actively debated.","Powered-flight ability and ecology depend on biomechanical interpretation."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.4191","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-03296-8","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/archaeopteryx.html","https://doi.org/10.1098/rstl.1863.0003"]},"archaeornithomimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 96-83 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria","Ornithomimidae"],"description":"Archaeornithomimus asiaticus was an ornithomimid from the Iren Dabasu Formation of Inner Mongolia. It is known from disarticulated bones of several individuals; Uzbek material formerly called A. bissektensis is now placed in the separate genus Dzharacursor.","facts":["The type assemblage contains mainly postcranial bones rather than a complete individual.","A toothless, cursorial ornithomimid body is supported, but diet is not directly known."],"diet":"diet uncertain; omnivory or herbivory plausible"},"ageReviewNote":"The Iren Dabasu type horizon has a broad debated Late Cretaceous age; the younger Uzbek species should not extend the range.","residualUncertainty":["The Iren Dabasu age is imprecise.","The type assemblage requires modern comprehensive revision."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2015.07.012","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2024.2433759","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/archaeornithomimus.html"]},"argentinosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 96-93 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauria","Colossosauria"],"description":"Argentinosaurus huinculensis was a giant titanosaur from the Huincul Formation of Patagonia. Despite its fame, it is known from a small set of enormous vertebrae, ribs and limb-girdle elements; precise maximum length and mass are highly extrapolative.","facts":["The holotype includes several dorsal vertebrae, part of the sacrum, an anterior caudal, ribs and a right tibia.","A giant femoral shaft often discussed with it is not securely associated with the holotype."],"length":null,"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The lower Huincul Formation is late Cenomanian to early Turonian.","residualUncertainty":["No skull or complete limb is known.","Some referred elements may not belong to the genus.","Its exact titanosaurian position varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3864407/","https://www.app.pan.pl/archive/published/app68/app010862023.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/argentinosaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0078733"]},"arrhinoceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 71-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae"],"description":"Arrhinoceratops brachyops was a chasmosaurine ceratopsid from the Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta. It is known chiefly from a large, nearly complete but crushed skull; postcranial anatomy is poorly established.","facts":["The name, meaning no nose horn, is misleading because a nasal horn core is present.","A juvenile skull adds growth information, but whole-body reconstructions rely on related ceratopsids."]},"ageReviewNote":"The diagnostic material is lower Maastrichtian within the Horseshoe Canyon Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Very little diagnostic postcranial material is known.","Distortion complicates details of the holotype skull."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/zoj.12294","https://pubs.usgs.gov/pp/0210a/report.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/arrhinoceratops.html"]},"atlascopcosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 115-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ornithopoda","Elasmaria"],"description":"Atlascopcosaurus loadsi was a small ornithopod from the Albian Eumeralla Formation of Victoria, Australia. It is based on a partial upper jaw and isolated jaw and tooth material, so nearly all postcranial and body-size details are inferred.","facts":["The holotype is a partial maxilla with teeth.","Modern Australian reviews retain it provisionally as valid while recognising weak phylogenetic resolution."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Eumeralla Formation material is Albian rather than spanning most of the Early Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["The diagnosis relies on limited dental characters.","Its exact position within Gondwanan ornithopods is unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/03115518.2023.2228367","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/atlascopcosaurus.html"]},"atrociraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae"],"description":"Atrociraptor marshalli was a small dromaeosaurid from the lower Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta. The holotype consists of a partial snout, jaws and teeth; isolated teeth referred later do not provide a complete skeleton.","facts":["The short, deep snout is reconstructed from incomplete and somewhat distorted cranial material.","Bipedal locomotion and carnivory are secure at broad level, but precise prey and hunting behaviour are unknown."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type comes from the early Maastrichtian lower Horseshoe Canyon Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Only one diagnostic partial skull is established.","Its position within Dromaeosauridae varies among recent analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/40662065_A_new_dromaeosaurid_from_the_Horseshoe_Canyon_Formation_Upper_Cretaceous_of_Alberta_Canada","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2021.2010087","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/atrociraptor.html"]},"aucasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 83-78 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauridae","Carnotaurinae"],"description":"Aucasaurus garridoi was an abelisaurid theropod from the Anacleto Formation of Patagonia. Its single holotype preserves a nearly complete skull and much of the articulated skeleton, making it one of the anatomically best known abelisaurids.","facts":["The forelimbs are extremely reduced and anatomically specialised.","Damage around the skull has inspired death-scenario proposals, but the cause is not established."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Anacleto Formation is early Campanian.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal individual is known.","The cause of cranial damage and details of soft tissue are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.16236","https://www.app.pan.pl/article/item/app20130037.html","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/aucasaurus.html"]},"austrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 105-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Somphospondyli"],"description":"Austrosaurus mckillopi was a somphospondylan sauropod from the upper Albian Allaru Mudstone of Queensland, Australia. The type is a short series of vertebrae with ribs; many later referrals cannot be assumed to belong to it.","facts":["The holotype comprises six presacral vertebrae and associated ribs.","Modern reassessment places it near, but not securely within, Titanosauria."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Allaru Mudstone type horizon is late Albian.","residualUncertainty":["The taxon is very incomplete.","Historical referred material is not securely attributable.","Its precise somphospondylan position varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/03115518.2017.1334826","https://doi.org/10.1080/03115518.2023.2228367","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/austrosaurus.html"]},"avaceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 78-76 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae"],"description":"Avaceratops lammersi was a small centrosaurine ceratopsid from the Judith River Formation of Montana. Its holotype is a partial skull and skeleton of an immature individual; additional skulls have been referred, but their assignment and adult morphology remain debated.","facts":["The holotype is not a complete adult skeleton.","Its short frill lacks the elaborate ornament of many centrosaurines, although ontogeny complicates comparison."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality is late Campanian in the Judith River Formation.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype is immature.","The referral of larger skulls and the genus's phylogenetic position remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.jstor.org/stable/4064910","https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2013.1186","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/avaceratops.html"]},"avimimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Oviraptorosauria","Avimimidae"],"description":"Avimimus portentosus was a small, long-legged oviraptorosaur from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. It is represented by several partial skeletons, while a separate species, A. nemegtensis, is known from an older bonebed.","facts":["The type species combines toothless jaws, a fused hand and cursorial hind limbs.","Feathering is strongly expected from its oviraptorosaur relationships, but detailed plumage is not directly preserved in the principal specimens."]},"ageReviewNote":"A. portentosus is from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation; A. nemegtensis should be treated separately.","residualUncertainty":["Some historical referrals may belong to A. nemegtensis.","Diet and plumage details are inferred rather than directly preserved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2017.1347177","https://doi.org/10.1038/srep35782","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/avimimus.html"]},"bactrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 96-83 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Bactrosaurus johnsoni was a non-hadrosaurid hadrosauroid from the Iren Dabasu Formation of Inner Mongolia. It is represented by disarticulated cranial and postcranial bones from numerous individuals, not by a single nearly complete skeleton.","facts":["At least a dozen individuals are represented at the principal locality.","Its broad herbivorous and primarily bipedal-to-facultatively quadrupedal interpretation is based on hadrosauroid anatomy; exact gait and diet are not directly observed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Iren Dabasu Formation has a debated broad Late Cretaceous age, probably within the Cenomanian-Campanian interval.","residualUncertainty":["The formation age is imprecise.","Some historical referrals and named species are not secure.","Its position close to Hadrosauridae varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2011.01053.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/bactrosaurus.html"]},"bagaceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 84-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia","Bagaceratopsidae"],"description":"Bagaceratops rozhdestvenskyi was a small neoceratopsian from the Campanian Barun Goyot Formation of Mongolia. Numerous skulls and some postcranial skeletons document its short face, small frill and incipient nasal ornament.","facts":["Multiple growth stages are known, making the skull better sampled than those of many early ceratopsians.","Several genera named from similar Mongolian skulls have been proposed as synonyms of Bagaceratops, but taxonomic boundaries still require specimen-level care."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Barun Goyot Formation is Campanian, but lacks a narrow universally agreed numerical range.","residualUncertainty":["Synonymy of Gobiceratops, Lamaceratops, Platyceratops and Magnirostris is influential but not universally adopted.","Formation age is broad."],"sourceUrls":["https://d3qi0qp55mx5f5.cloudfront.net/paulsereno/i/docs/00-Marginocephalia.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2019.1593404","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/bagaceratops.html"]},"bambiraptor":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae"],"description":"Bambiraptor feinbergi is the accepted original spelling for a small dromaeosaurid from the upper Two Medicine Formation of Montana. The holotype, AMNH FR 30556, is a largely complete, partially articulated juvenile skeleton. Duplicate elements belong to a separate paratype assemblage representing at least two larger individuals and do not make the holotype a mixed specimen.","facts":["The species was originally named feinbergi; the later spelling feinbergorum is not a mandatory correction.","The holotype and multi-individual paratype assemblage must be kept distinct.","Adult size, proportions and ecology cannot be derived securely from the juvenile holotype."],"taxonomicStatus":"provisionally valid; distinction from juvenile Saurornitholestes requires specialist reassessment","length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The holotype comes from the late Campanian upper Two Medicine Formation.","residualUncertainty":["The paratype combines at least two larger individuals and cannot define one adult skeleton.","The holotype may represent a juvenile of another dromaeosaurid.","A modern monographic redescription is needed.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://kuscholarworks.ku.edu/bitstream/handle/1808/3761/Burnham_2000_LR.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0054329","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/bambiraptor.html"]},"barapasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"about 199-184 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Barapasaurus tagorei was an early sauropod from the lower Kota Formation of India. Roughly three hundred disarticulated bones represent at least six individuals and collectively document much of the postcranial skeleton, but the skull is known only from teeth and very limited elements.","facts":["The quarry is a multi-individual assemblage, not one complete giant skeleton.","Its columnar limbs support obligate quadrupedal locomotion; precise feeding height and mass remain reconstructed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The lower Kota Formation is generally Sinemurian-Pliensbachian, although its upper boundary is not tightly dated.","residualUncertainty":["The assemblage is disarticulated.","The formation age is not radiometrically narrow.","Its position around the base of Eusauropoda varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2010.00933.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/barapasaurus.html"]},"barosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 155-149 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Diplodocidae","Diplodocinae"],"description":"Barosaurus lentus was a long-necked diplodocid from the Morrison Formation of western North America. Several incomplete skeletons collectively document its elongated neck and relatively short tail compared with Diplodocus, but skull material is scarce and referrals need revision.","facts":["The type specimen was founded on a series of tail vertebrae; better skeletons were referred later.","The famous rearing museum mount is a biomechanical reconstruction, not a preserved pose or directly observed behaviour."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure material is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian within the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred specimens may not belong to the type species.","The largest reported cervical vertebrae have uncertain identification."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.nps.gov/dino/learn/nature/barosaurus-lentus.htm","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.857","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/barosaurus.html"]},"baryonyx":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 130-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Spinosauridae","Baryonychinae"],"description":"Baryonyx walkeri was a baryonychine spinosaurid from the Barremian Weald Clay Formation of England. The holotype is a substantial partial skull and skeleton, and associated gut-region remains provide rare direct evidence of both fish and ornithopod consumption.","facts":["The holotype includes much of the skull, vertebral column and limbs, including the enlarged first manual claw.","Fish scales and partly digested iguanodontian bones show a broad carnivorous diet, not exclusive fish-eating."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality is Barremian in the Weald Clay Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Some British and Iberian referrals are taxonomically uncertain.","The exact relationship to Suchomimus is debated but they are not established synonyms."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/324359a0","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8481559/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/how-did-baryonyx-change-what-we-knew-about-spinosaurs.html","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/baryonyx.html"]},"becklespinax":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 140-133 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae"],"description":"Becklespinax altispinax is a historical name based on three tall-spined dorsal vertebrae from the Hastings Beds Group of England. The bones are too limited for a secure genus-level diagnosis or full-body reconstruction, and modern reviews generally regard the name as dubious.","facts":["The name-bearing specimen NHMUK R1828 comprises three associated dorsal vertebrae.","The better-known name Altispinax dunkeri is also problematic and should not be used to supply a skull, limbs or a sail."],"taxonomicStatus":"dubious name, commonly treated as a nomen dubium","acceptedName":false,"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The specimen is from the Valanginian Wadhurst Clay/Hastings Beds succession.","residualUncertainty":["The specimen may be indeterminate Tetanurae.","Diagnostic validity and correct nomenclature need specialist review.","The neural-spine soft tissue is unknown.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://doi.org/10.1144/SP343.13","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/becklespinax.html"]},"beipiaosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Therizinosauria"],"description":"Beipiaosaurus inexpectus was a feathered therizinosaur from the Yixian Formation of Liaoning, China. Several partial skeletons preserve extensive filamentous plumage and document a relatively basal member of the long-necked, large-gutted therizinosaur lineage.","facts":["Both ordinary filamentous feathers and unusually long broad filaments are directly preserved.","Herbivory or omnivory is plausible from therizinosaur anatomy, but exact food items are not preserved."],"diet":"diet uncertain; herbivory or omnivory plausible"},"ageReviewNote":"The Jianshangou beds of the Yixian Formation are early Aptian, close to 125 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["Not every specimen preserves the same anatomy.","The function of the elongated broad filaments is unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/20670","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8483305/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/beipiaosaurus.html"]},"bellusaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"about 168-163 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda","Neosauropoda"],"description":"Bellusaurus sui was a small-bodied sauropod from the lower Shishugou Formation of Xinjiang, China. A bonebed preserves disarticulated remains of at least a dozen juveniles; adult anatomy and final body size are unknown.","facts":["Cranial and postcranial remains provide broad coverage across the juvenile skeleton.","Its small size is primarily an ontogenetic feature and should not be presented as a known adult maximum."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The lower Shishugou Formation is Middle Jurassic, usually Bathonian-Callovian.","residualUncertainty":["No confirmed adult is known.","Ontogeny affects its phylogenetic placement.","The bonebed accumulation process is not fully resolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5985764/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/bellusaurus.html"]},"borogovia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Troodontidae"],"description":"Borogovia gracilicrus was a small troodontid theropod from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. It is based on one fragmentary lower hind limb, so its skull, trunk, feathers and total body size are inferred from relatives.","facts":["The distinctive foot lacks an enlarged predatory second-toe claw of the proportions seen in many other paravians.","Its name remains valid in recent troodontid analyses, but evidence is sparse."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type comes from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Only one fragmentary specimen is known.","Its exact troodontid relationships are weakly constrained."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.12640","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/borogovia.html"]},"brachiosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian–Tithonian, about 154–150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Brachiosauridae","Brachiosaurus"],"diet":"Herbivore","locomotion":"Quadrupedal","description":"Brachiosaurus altithorax was a brachiosaurid sauropod from the Morrison Formation of Colorado. Its holotype is a partial postcranial skeleton; the well-known African skeletons once called Brachiosaurus brancai belong to the separate genus Giraffatitan.","facts":["The holotype FMNH P 25107 was found by Elmer Riggs's team in 1900 near Fruita, Colorado, and described in 1903; it comprises dorsal and sacral vertebrae, ribs, a coracoid, humerus, ilium and femur.","No skull is confidently associated with B. altithorax; a Morrison skull (USNM 5730) once assigned to Camarasaurus may belong to Brachiosaurus but is not from the holotype.","Long forelimbs and a high shoulder are supported by the holotype, while many popular details derive from Giraffatitan and must not be transferred uncritically.","Taylor's 2009 re-evaluation formalised the separation of the Tanzanian material as Giraffatitan brancai.","Referred Morrison material is limited and some referrals are debated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The holotype is Kimmeridgian within the Brushy Basin Member of the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Few specimens are securely referable to B. altithorax.","African Giraffatitan material must be excluded from descriptions of the genus.","No skull or neck is associated with the holotype.","Maximum size and mass are extrapolations from one partial skeleton."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.2475/ajs.s4-15.88.299","https://doi.org/10.1671/039.029.0309","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2011.557115","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/brachiosaurus.html"]},"brachylophosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 78-76 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae"],"description":"Brachylophosaurus canadensis was a flat-crested hadrosaurid from the Judith River Formation of Montana and Oldman Formation of Alberta. Numerous skulls and skeletons, including specimens with extensive skin impressions, make its anatomy and growth well documented.","facts":["Several so-called mummies preserve scales and body outlines, not an intact living appearance.","Plant fragments in one body cavity are potentially gut contents, but contamination and taphonomic mixing limit fine dietary conclusions."]},"ageReviewNote":"Diagnostic occurrences are late Campanian, principally about 78-76 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level referral of some material requires stratigraphic context.","Gut-content and molecular preservation claims need cautious interpretation."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x","https://doi.org/10.2110/palo.2007.p07-044r","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/brachylophosaurus.html"]},"brachytrachelopan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"approximately 160-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Dicraeosauridae"],"description":"Brachytrachelopan mesai was a short-necked dicraeosaurid from the Canadon Calcareo Formation of Patagonia. It is known from one incomplete, partly articulated postcranial skeleton that preserves much of the neck and trunk but no skull.","facts":["The unusually short cervical vertebrae directly support a proportionally short neck.","Selective low browsing is plausible, but exact feeding height and vegetation are not preserved."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Canadon Calcareo Formation is Late Jurassic, but its precise Oxfordian-Tithonian placement remains debated.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is known.","The formation age is broad.","Its exact relationships among dicraeosaurids vary."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03623","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/brachytrachelopan.html"]},"buitreraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 99-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae","Unenlagiinae"],"description":"Buitreraptor gonzalezorum was a small long-snouted unenlagiine dromaeosaurid from the Cenomanian Candeleros Formation of Patagonia. The holotype is a near-complete skeleton with a skull, and additional individuals clarify much of its postcranial anatomy.","facts":["Its numerous small, unserrated teeth differ from the broad serrated teeth of many northern dromaeosaurids.","The teeth support small-prey feeding but do not identify a single specialised diet."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Candeleros Formation is Cenomanian.","residualUncertainty":["Its exact position among unenlagiines and other paravians varies.","No direct feather impressions or gut contents are known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03996","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5875404/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/buitreraptor.html"]},"camarasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 155-149 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Camarasauridae"],"description":"Camarasaurus was an abundant macronarian sauropod from the Morrison Formation of western North America. Many skulls and skeletons across several growth stages document almost the entire anatomy, although the limits and validity of its named species still require revision.","facts":["Camarasaurus had a relatively short, deep skull and robust teeth suited to cropping vegetation.","Most mounted skeletons combine material; abundance does not mean every display is one complete individual."],"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"Diagnostic material spans much of the Kimmeridgian-Tithonian Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level taxonomy needs modern specimen-level revision.","Feeding height and social behaviour are inferred.","Some historical referrals are non-diagnostic."],"sourceUrls":["https://docentes.fct.unl.pt/omateus/publications/specimen-level-cladistic-analysis-camarasaurus-dinosauria-sauropoda-and-revisio","https://doi.org/10.26879/559","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/camarasaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.5962/p.217807"]},"camptosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 155-149 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ornithopoda","Ankylopollexia"],"description":"Camptosaurus dispar was a medium-sized ankylopollexian ornithopod from the Morrison Formation of western North America. Several partial skeletons and skulls document its anatomy, but many species and specimens historically assigned to Camptosaurus have been moved to other genera.","facts":["The accepted genus concept is substantially narrower than in older books.","It was primarily bipedal and may have used the forelimbs in slow locomotion, but habitual gait is a functional inference."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure C. dispar material is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian in the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Historical referrals from Europe and elsewhere largely represent other taxa.","The genus-level sample still needs specimen-by-specimen revision."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.mapress.com/zt/article/view/zootaxa.2783.1.4","https://doi.org/10.2992/0097-4463(2008)76%5B227:ANSOCO%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/camptosaurus.html"]},"carcharodontosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Carcharodontosauria","Carcharodontosauridae"],"description":"Carcharodontosaurus saharicus was a giant carcharodontosaurid from Cenomanian North Africa. The name originated with now-lost Algerian teeth and is stabilised by a large partial Moroccan neotype skull; the destroyed Egyptian skeleton long used to reconstruct it was separated in 2025 as Tameryraptor markgrafi.","facts":["The Moroccan neotype SGM-Din 1 preserves a large but incomplete skull, not a full skeleton.","Postcranial and crest details from the Bahariya specimen named Tameryraptor must no longer be presented as C. saharicus anatomy."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure C. saharicus evidence is Cenomanian, chiefly the historical Algerian type record and the Kem Kem Group neotype of Morocco.","residualUncertainty":["C. iguidensis may not remain in the same genus.","Many isolated North African teeth and bones are not diagnostic.","The 2025 Tameryraptor revision is recent and should be monitored."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.272.5264.986","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0311096","https://d3qi0qp55mx5f5.cloudfront.net/paulsereno/i/docs/07-JVP-Carch.iguidensis.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/carcharodontosaurus.html"]},"carnotaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 72-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauridae","Carnotaurinae"],"description":"Carnotaurus sastrei was a horned abelisaurid from the La Colonia Formation of Patagonia. Its single holotype is a nearly complete articulated skeleton with a skull and extensive skin impressions, providing exceptional anatomical evidence from one individual.","facts":["Paired frontal horns, a deep skull and extremely reduced forelimbs are directly preserved.","Skin impressions show varied non-overlapping scales; an osteoderm-studded body or a specific colour pattern is not supported."]},"ageReviewNote":"The La Colonia Formation is latest Cretaceous, but the exact late Campanian-Maastrichtian age of the type horizon remains uncertain.","residualUncertainty":["Only one specimen is known.","The exact formation age remains debated.","Horn and tail functions are biomechanical hypotheses."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/226819","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2021.104994","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/carnotaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2020.1802445"]},"caudipteryx":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Oviraptorosauria","Caudipterygidae"],"description":"Caudipteryx was a small feathered oviraptorosaur from the Yixian Formation of Liaoning, China. Multiple nearly complete skeletons preserve pennaceous feathers on the arms and tail as well as gastroliths in the abdominal region.","facts":["Its short, symmetrical arm feathers were not a powered-flight wing.","Gastroliths and tooth reduction are consistent with plant consumption or omnivory but do not prove a strictly herbivorous diet."]},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant Yixian beds are early Aptian, close to 125 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["C. zoui and C. dongi distinctions have been debated.","Gastrolith function and exact diet are not uniquely determined."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/31635","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/caudipteryx.html"]},"cedarpelta":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 98-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Cedarpelta bilbeyhallorum was an ankylosaurid from the Mussentuchit Member of the Cedar Mountain Formation of Utah. Disarticulated skulls, armour and postcranial bones from more than one individual collectively document the animal; no single complete skeleton is known.","facts":["The skull material is unusually informative for an early ankylosaurid.","No diagnostic tail club is preserved, so a clubbed tail should not be asserted for this genus."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Mussentuchit Member is Cenomanian, not Barremian or earliest Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["Material represents multiple individuals.","Its placement at or just outside Ankylosauridae varies.","The arrangement of osteoderms is incompletely known."],"sourceUrls":["https://books.google.com/books?id=04UqgW3Lo3QC&pg=PA201","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2015.1059985","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/cedarpelta.html"]},"centrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 76.5-75.5 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae"],"description":"Centrosaurus apertus was a centrosaurine ceratopsid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. Thousands of bones from many individuals, including articulated skeletons, skulls and extensive bonebeds, make it among the best sampled non-avian dinosaurs.","facts":["A large nasal horn and hooked frill ornament are documented across growth stages.","Mass-death bonebeds support group aggregation at death but do not alone reveal permanent herd structure or migration."]},"ageReviewNote":"C. apertus is concentrated in the lower-middle Dinosaur Park Formation, late Campanian.","residualUncertainty":["Bonebed processes and social interpretation are debated.","Some named centrosaur species may reflect variation or different horizons."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.2110/palo.2014.084","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933270/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/centrosaurus.html"]},"ceratosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 153-148 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Ceratosauridae"],"description":"Ceratosaurus nasicornis was a horned ceratosaurian theropod from the Morrison Formation of western North America. The holotype preserves a skull and much of the skeleton; additional specimens improve coverage, while several proposed species and referrals remain disputed.","facts":["The midline nasal horn core and paired orbital rugosities are directly preserved.","A midline row of small osteoderms is associated with the holotype, but their complete arrangement is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure C. nasicornis material is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian in the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["C. dentisulcatus and C. magnicornis may not be distinct species.","European and African referrals are debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://repository.si.edu/handle/10088/10108","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/ceratosaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.2475/ajs.s3-27.160.329","https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201907002246"]},"cetiosauriscus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"about 166-164 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda","Neosauropoda"],"description":"Cetiosauriscus stewarti was a sauropod from the Callovian Oxford Clay Formation of England. Its only confident skeleton preserves the rear trunk, pelvis, much of the tail and limbs but no skull or neck; its exact relationships remain unresolved.","facts":["The name C. stewarti has been conserved for specimen NHMUK PV R3078.","Swiss material once called Cetiosauriscus greppini belongs to the separate genus Amanzia."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type skeleton is Callovian in the Oxford Clay Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Phylogenetic analyses disagree between non-neosauropod eusauropod and diplodocoid affinities.","Some elements are incomplete or restored."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2025.2550760","https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10086222/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/cetiosauriscus.html"]},"cetiosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"about 168-166 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Cetiosaurus oxoniensis was a basal eusauropod from the Great Oolite Group of England. Several partial postcranial skeletons collectively document a large, robust quadruped, but skull remains are extremely limited and older species assignments are taxonomically confused.","facts":["C. oxoniensis was conserved as the type species to stabilise the long-used genus name.","Most historical Cetiosaurus species are not necessarily congeneric with C. oxoniensis."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure C. oxoniensis material is Bathonian in the Great Oolite Group.","residualUncertainty":["Historical referrals are taxonomically heterogeneous.","Its exact position among basal eusauropods varies.","No complete skull is known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-4983.00275","https://www.biotaxa.org/bzn/issue/view/5182","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/cetiosaurus.html"]},"chaoyangsaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"approximately 150-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Chaoyangsauridae"],"description":"Chaoyangsaurus youngi was a small early ceratopsian from the Tuchengzi Formation of Liaoning, China. It is known from a single partial skull with associated postcranial fragments, making detailed body proportions and ecology uncertain.","facts":["Cranial anatomy supports a position near the base of Ceratopsia.","It lacked the elaborate horns and frill of later ceratopsids; the rest of its external appearance is reconstructed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The upper Tuchengzi Formation is usually considered latest Jurassic, probably Tithonian, but dating remains debated.","residualUncertainty":["The formation age is debated.","Only one fragmentary individual is known.","Relationships among earliest ceratopsians vary."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1999.10011181","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chaoyangsaurus.html"]},"chasmosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 76.5-75.5 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae"],"description":"Chasmosaurus was a long-frilled chasmosaurine ceratopsid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. Numerous skulls and skeletons, including juveniles and skin impressions, document its anatomy, while species limits and the significance of frill variation remain debated.","facts":["C. belli is securely recognised; C. russelli is also commonly retained, whereas Vagaceratops is generally separate.","Large frill openings and marginal ornaments are preserved, but display, species recognition and sexual selection are hypotheses."]},"ageReviewNote":"Diagnostic Chasmosaurus material is late Campanian in the Dinosaur Park Formation.","residualUncertainty":["C. belli and C. russelli may overlap in variable characters.","Some specimens historically assigned to Chasmosaurus are Vagaceratops or indeterminate."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.11646/zootaxa.2963.1.1","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0145805","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chasmosaurus.html"]},"chindesaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"about 213-210 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia"],"description":"Chindesaurus bryansmalli was an early saurischian from the Petrified Forest Member of the Chinle Formation in Arizona. It is based on one incomplete postcranial skeleton without a skull; whether it is a herrerasaurid or another early theropod-line saurischian remains unresolved.","facts":["The holotype preserves vertebral, pelvic and hind-limb elements but no diagnostic complete skull or forelimb.","Carnivory and bipedalism are reasonable broad inferences; detailed feeding ecology is unknown."],"diet":null,"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type horizon is late Norian in the Petrified Forest Member of the Chinle Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Its position as a herrerasaurid or early theropod differs among analyses.","Several referred specimens may not belong to the genus."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2019.1645682","https://www.nps.gov/pefo/learn/nature/dinosaurs.htm","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chindesaurus.html"]},"chinshakiangosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"approximately 201-190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropodiformes","Sauropoda"],"description":"Chinshakiangosaurus chunghoensis was an early sauropod or near-sauropod from the Fengjiahe Formation of Yunnan, China. The accepted species spelling is chunghoensis, and the fragmentary skeleton is Early Jurassic rather than Late Jurassic.","facts":["The best-known element is a partial lower jaw with teeth, accompanied by fragmentary postcranial bones.","Its jaw suggests a fleshy cheek is possible, but soft tissue and exact feeding mechanics are not preserved."],"taxonomicStatus":"provisionally valid but fragmentary","length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Fengjiahe Formation is Early Jurassic, broadly Hettangian-Sinemurian.","residualUncertainty":["The diagnosis and association of the material need renewed study.","Its position as basal sauropod or close sauropodiform varies.","The formation is not narrowly dated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S0016756806003062","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8929930/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chinshakiangosaurus.html"]},"chirostenotes":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 77-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Oviraptorosauria","Caenagnathidae"],"description":"Chirostenotes pergracilis was a caenagnathid oviraptorosaur from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. The name is based on a pair of hands; referred jaws and partial skeletons clarify the body plan, but associations among historically named caenagnathid parts remain complex.","facts":["Modern studies generally distinguish Chirostenotes from the jaw-based genus Caenagnathus.","A toothless beak and bipedal stance are supported at clade level; exact diet is unresolved."],"diet":"diet uncertain; omnivory or herbivory plausible"},"ageReviewNote":"Secure C. pergracilis material is late Campanian in the Dinosaur Park Formation, not Maastrichtian.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred skeletons may represent other caenagnathids.","The relationship to Caenagnathus and Macrophalangia has a complex nomenclatural history."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1997.10011018","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2020.1726908","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chirostenotes.html"]},"chubutisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 125-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Somphospondyli"],"description":"Chubutisaurus insignis was a large somphospondylan sauropod from the Cerro Barcino Formation of Patagonia. Its quarry produced a fragmentary postcranial skeleton including vertebrae and limb bones but no skull; older, very narrow age claims are not secure.","facts":["The recovered material provides useful limb and vertebral anatomy but not a complete skeleton.","It is usually placed just outside or close to Titanosauria; the precise node varies among analyses."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant Cerro Barcino beds are broadly late Early to early Late Cretaceous, with member correlation and numerical age still debated.","residualUncertainty":["The exact stratigraphic member and age have been reported inconsistently.","Only one principal individual is known.","Phylogenetic placement varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2011.539651","https://staff.mef.org.ar/images/investigadores/jose_luis_carballido/Papers/Carballido_et_al._2011_Chubutisaurus.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chubutisaurus.html"]},"chungkingosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"approximately 163-157 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae"],"description":"Chungkingosaurus jiangbeiensis was a small stegosaur from the upper Shaximiao Formation of Chongqing, China. Its holotype is a partial skull and incomplete skeleton with plates and spikes; extensive restoration in the mount means exact armour arrangement requires caution.","facts":["The taxon remains recognised in recent reassessments, but its anatomy and position among stegosaurs are incompletely resolved.","Tail spikes are preserved in the assemblage, although their exact number and arrangement on the living animal are uncertain."]},"ageReviewNote":"The upper Shaximiao Formation is Late Jurassic, commonly Oxfordian.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype mount contains substantial restoration.","Some referred specimens may represent other stegosaurs.","A fully peer-reviewed modern monograph is lacking."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Dong%26amp%3B_83.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.09.29.615678","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/chungkingosaurus.html"]},"citipati":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 75-71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Oviraptorosauria","Oviraptoridae"],"description":"Citipati osmolskae was a large oviraptorid from the Djadokhta Formation of Mongolia. Several skulls and skeletons, embryos and adult-on-nest specimens document its toothless beak and direct association with eggs.","facts":["Adult specimens preserved over nests support brooding posture, though fine behavioural details remain inferred.","The famous specimen called Big Mama is referred to Citipati; not every crest-bearing Djadokhta oviraptorid belongs to this species."],"diet":"diet uncertain; omnivory or herbivory plausible"},"ageReviewNote":"The Djadokhta Formation is late Campanian, broadly about 75-71 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["Several Djadokhta oviraptorid morphs remain unnamed or differently assigned.","Nest posture does not reveal the complete incubation strategy.","Diet is unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0082(2002)364%3C0001:CAOCOT%3E2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2016.1997","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/citipati.html"]},"coelophysis":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"about 215-205 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda","Coelophysoidea","Coelophysidae"],"description":"Coelophysis bauri was a small early neotheropod from the Chinle Group of the south-western United States. The Ghost Ranch bonebeds contain hundreds of individuals and many articulated skeletons, making its anatomy and growth exceptionally well documented.","facts":["The neotype AMNH 7224 stabilises C. bauri, and Rioarribasaurus colberti is an objective junior synonym.","A famous cannibalism claim was overturned when the supposed juvenile remains inside one ribcage were identified as a crocodylomorph."]},"ageReviewNote":"Diagnostic C. bauri material is late Norian to Rhaetian in the Chinle Group, especially the Rock Point Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Bonebed accumulation does not prove pack living.","Some older Coelophysis referrals represent other genera.","Feathering is not directly preserved."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/12250297","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/coelophysis.html","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2006.0524"]},"coelurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 155-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria"],"description":"Coelurus fragilis was a small basal coelurosaur from the Morrison Formation of Wyoming. It is known chiefly from one incomplete, disarticulated postcranial skeleton without a skull; many bones historically referred to it belong to other small theropods.","facts":["The secure material includes vertebrae, pelvis and limb bones, but no diagnostic skull.","Bipedal carnivory is a broad coelurosaur inference; exact prey, feathers and speed are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian within the Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Historical referrals are heterogeneous.","Its exact position near the base of Coelurosauria is weakly resolved.","A modern comprehensive redescription is needed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/40662862_New_small_theropod_dinosaur_from_the_Upper_Jurassic_Morrison_Formation_of_Wyoming","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/coelurus.html"]},"coloradisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"approximately 227-208 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massospondylidae"],"description":"Coloradisaurus brevis was a massospondylid sauropodomorph from the Los Colorados Formation of Argentina. Its holotype preserves a skull and much of the postcranial skeleton, providing strong evidence for a medium-sized bipedal early sauropodomorph.","facts":["Detailed skull and postcranial studies support massospondylid affinities.","Broad herbivory or omnivory is plausible, but exact food and feeding behaviour are not preserved."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Los Colorados Formation is Norian-Rhaetian, though its long numerical span is not resolved at every locality.","residualUncertainty":["The formation age is broad.","Some referred remains have weaker association.","Massospondylid relationships vary modestly."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2012.01198.x","https://staff.mef.org.ar/images/investigadores/diego_pol/papers/68.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/coloradisaurus.html"]},"compsognathus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"about 150-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Compsognathidae"],"description":"Compsognathus longipes was a small coelurosaur from the Tithonian lagoonal limestones of Germany and France. Two nearly complete skeletons of different sizes document its slender bipedal body; C. corallestris is generally treated as the same species.","facts":["The smaller German specimen contains lizard remains in its abdominal region, direct evidence of predation or scavenging.","The larger French specimen shows that the famously tiny German individual was not the maximum adult size."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Solnhofen and Canjuers lagoonal deposits are Tithonian.","residualUncertainty":["The ontogenetic stage of each specimen is debated.","No direct feather impressions are known for Compsognathus itself.","Compsognathid monophyly is contested."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2007.00269.x","https://www.si.edu/object/nmnhpaleobiology_3451169","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/compsognathus.html","https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2006)26%5B879:AROCFT%5D2.0.CO;2"]},"concavenator":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 130-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Carcharodontosauria"],"description":"Concavenator corcovatus was a carcharodontosaurian theropod from the Barremian Las Hoyas locality of Spain. One nearly complete articulated skeleton preserves unusually tall dorsal vertebrae and patches of scale impressions.","facts":["The tall neural spines over the hips directly support a raised dorsal structure, but its soft-tissue outline and function are unknown.","Bumps on the ulna were proposed as feather-quill attachment sites; that interpretation remains contested and does not demonstrate a fully feathered body."]},"ageReviewNote":"The La Huerguina Formation at Las Hoyas is Barremian.","residualUncertainty":["The ulnar bumps may not be homologous with avian quill knobs.","Soft tissue over the dorsal spines is unknown.","Its exact carcharodontosaurian position varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09181","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/concavenator.html"]},"conchoraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"approximately 84-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Oviraptorosauria","Oviraptoridae"],"description":"Conchoraptor gracilis was a small, crestless oviraptorid from the Campanian Barun Goyot Formation of Mongolia. Multiple skulls and partial skeletons document its toothless beak and light build, but its name does not constitute evidence that it ate shellfish.","facts":["Jaw mechanics are compatible with powerful biting, but exact food items are not preserved.","Some specimens once assigned to Conchoraptor may represent other closely related oviraptorids."],"diet":"diet uncertain; omnivory or herbivory plausible"},"ageReviewNote":"The Barun Goyot Formation is Campanian, with a broad numerical age.","residualUncertainty":["Diet is unknown despite the shellfish-based name.","Several small oviraptorid taxa from Mongolia are difficult to distinguish.","No direct plumage is preserved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00114-007-0219-1","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2017.10.023","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/conchoraptor.html"]},"confuciusornis":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"about 125-120 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Avialae","Pygostylia","Confuciusornithidae"],"description":"Confuciusornis sanctus was a toothless early pygostylian bird from the Jehol Biota of northeastern China. Hundreds of articulated skeletons preserve wing feathers and, in some individuals, paired ribbon-like tail feathers, allowing unusually detailed study of growth and variation.","facts":["The long paired tail feathers occur only in part of the sample; they are likely related to sexual display, but they cannot simply be assigned to males in every specimen.","Wing anatomy and feathers support flight ability, while take-off performance and flight style remain debated."],"diet":"diet varied or uncertain; evidence supports both plant and animal foods"},"ageReviewNote":"Most C. sanctus specimens are from Barremian-Aptian beds of the Yixian Formation, around 125-120 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["Several named Confuciusornis species may be synonyms or represent variation.","Tail-streamer sex association is probabilistic rather than universal.","Flight capacity varies with biomechanical assumptions."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.19615/j.cnki.1000-3118.180530","https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms2377","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2022.02.010"]},"corythosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"about 77-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae"],"description":"Corythosaurus casuarius was a helmet-crested lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. Many skulls and skeletons across growth stages, including skin impressions, document its anatomy and the dramatic ontogenetic growth of its hollow crest.","facts":["The internal nasal passages of the crest are preserved and could affect sound resonance, but no specific call can be reconstructed as fact.","Many historical Corythosaurus species are now interpreted as growth or individual variation within C. casuarius."]},"ageReviewNote":"Diagnostic C. casuarius material is late Campanian in the Dinosaur Park Formation.","residualUncertainty":["The exact acoustic properties of living soft tissues are unknown.","Some species-level distinctions and referrals remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.si.edu/object/nmnhpaleobiology_3440896","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8667728/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/corythosaurus.html","https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/3a313ae3-f10d-42bc-8e32-b23f018b4e6d","https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/3e6e3523-2b83-468d-9ea7-0b60f651cee8","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00611.x"]},"cryolophosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"about 191-182 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda"],"description":"Cryolophosaurus ellioti was a large early neotheropod from the Hanson Formation of Antarctica. The principal specimen preserves a partial skull with a distinctive transverse crest and an incomplete postcranial skeleton; it is Early Jurassic, not Middle Jurassic.","facts":["The crest is directly preserved, but colour and display function are unknown.","It has sometimes been called an allosauroid, yet modern analyses place it variably among early neotheropods rather than securely within Allosauridae."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Hanson Formation type horizon is Pliensbachian, approximately 191-182 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal specimen is fully described.","Additional material is incompletely published.","Its position among early neotheropods varies strongly."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.264.5160.828","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2007.00325.x","https://data.aad.gov.au/aadc/biodiversity/taxon_profile.cfm?taxon_id=117961"]},"dacentrurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, about 157-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae","Dacentrurinae"],"description":"Dacentrurus armatus was a large quadrupedal stegosaur from the Late Jurassic of western Europe. The English type is incomplete, while additional Portuguese and Spanish skeletons expand knowledge of the genus; referrals outside the type must be assessed specimen by specimen.","facts":["The name is based on a partial postcranial skeleton from the Kimmeridge Clay of England.","European material includes vertebrae, girdle and limb bones, plates and tail spines, but no single complete skeleton.","Its low-crowned teeth and stegosaur anatomy support herbivory and quadrupedal locomotion; exact size and armour arrangement are reconstructed from incomplete individuals."]},"ageReviewNote":"The broad range covers accepted western European records; it must not be inferred from PBDB occurrence counts alone.","residualUncertainty":["Some Iberian referrals do not overlap sufficiently with the English type.","Maximum body size and plate arrangement are not directly preserved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201908002459","https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlae074","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dacentrurus"]},"daspletosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, approximately 77-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Tyrannosauridae","Tyrannosaurinae"],"description":"Daspletosaurus was a large tyrannosaurine predator from Campanian rocks of western North America. D. torosus is known from multiple skulls and partial skeletons, while newer named species and proposed anagenetic succession remain actively debated.","facts":["The genus has strong cranial and postcranial evidence from multiple individuals.","Bonebeds and close associations do not by themselves prove permanent pack hunting.","Species-level ranges and assignments should be stated separately rather than merged into one long genus average."]},"ageReviewNote":"D. torosus is concentrated in the Oldman Formation near 77-76 Ma; younger Daspletosaurus material may represent other species.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries and the proposed anagenetic sequence remain contested.","Social behaviour and precise mass estimates are indirect."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/srep44942","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.14461","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Daspletosaurus"]},"datousaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian-Callovian, about 168-163 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"locomotion":"on 4 legs","length":null,"description":"Datousaurus bashanensis was a quadrupedal herbivorous sauropod from the Lower Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan, China. It is known from incomplete associated skeletons, including cranial material, but still lacks a comprehensive modern redescription.","facts":["The original material includes postcranial remains and a skull associated with one of the partial skeletons.","Its broad placement among early eusauropods is better supported than any narrow family assignment.","Published whole-body dimensions are extrapolations because no complete articulated skeleton is known."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the Middle Jurassic Lower Shaximiao Formation; older broad wiki ranges were not specimen-based.","residualUncertainty":["The hypodigm needs modern specimen-level revision.","Precise phylogenetic placement and body size remain poorly constrained."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.ivpp.cas.cn/cbw/gjzdwxb/xbwzxz/200903/t20090327_2386210.html","https://juraparkbaltow.pl/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/Xing-Lockley-Chen-Gierlinski-Li-Persons-Matsukawa-Gingras-and-Wang-2013.pdf","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Datousaurus"]},"deinocheirus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Maastrichtian, about 71-68 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria","Deinocheiridae"],"description":"Deinocheirus mirificus was a giant, toothless, bipedal deinocheirid from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. Two later partial skeletons, together with the arm-dominated holotype, reveal a deep-bodied animal with a long duck-like snout, tall neural spines and broad feet.","facts":["The holotype preserves the shoulder girdle, enormous forelimbs and a few axial elements, not a complete body.","Two additional individuals preserve much of the skull and skeleton, but portions were reconstructed after poached bones were recovered.","Stomach-region remains and gastroliths support a mixed, opportunistic diet that included plants and fish; they do not establish a single exclusive diet."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Nemegt Formation is Maastrichtian; the genus should not be extended to the end-Cretaceous boundary without direct stratigraphic evidence.","residualUncertainty":["The exact diet proportions and function of the tall neural spines are unresolved.","The known adults remain incomplete and maximum mass is model-dependent."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13874","https://www.palaeontologia.pan.pl/Archive/1970-21_5-19_1-5.pdf","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Deinocheirus"]},"deinonychus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, roughly 115-108 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae","Eudromaeosauria"],"description":"Deinonychus antirrhopus was a medium-sized predatory dromaeosaurid from the Cloverly Formation and equivalent units of North America. Multiple partial skeletons, skull elements, teeth and growth stages document the animal, although many early reconstructions combined several individuals.","facts":["The enlarged second-toe claw is directly preserved and was held clear of the ground in walking.","Feathering is strongly supported by close phylogenetic bracketing, even though no direct body-feather impressions are known for Deinonychus.","Co-occurrence of several individuals and Tenontosaurus does not establish coordinated pack hunting."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure Cloverly occurrences are late Aptian-Albian, broadly 115-108 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Several specimens were mixed in early composite reconstructions.","Social hunting, prey preference and exact feather distribution remain inferential."],"sourceUrls":["https://elischolar.library.yale.edu/peabody_museum_natural_history_bulletin/30/","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0121476","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Deinonychus","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0028964"]},"deltadromeus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria?"],"description":"Deltadromeus agilis was a large, gracile-limbed bipedal theropod from the Kem Kem Group of Morocco. The holotype is a partial skeleton without a skull; its placement has shifted among coelurosaurs, ceratosaurs and noasaurids, so detailed head, diet and life appearance claims are not secure.","facts":["No skull or diagnostic teeth are known for the holotype.","Commercial Kem Kem teeth cannot be identified as Deltadromeus from the current type material.","Carnivory is plausible for a theropod but exact feeding ecology and even its closest relationships remain unresolved."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the Cenomanian Kem Kem Group type occurrence.","residualUncertainty":["The skull and teeth are unknown.","Higher-level classification remains strongly disputed.","The referred Bahariasaurus material is not securely conspecific."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.272.5264.986","https://doi.org/10.3897/zookeys.928.47517","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Deltadromeus"]},"dicraeosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian, about 157-152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Dicraeosauridae"],"description":"Dicraeosaurus hansemanni was a short-necked, quadrupedal herbivorous dicraeosaurid from the Lower and Middle Dinosaur members of the Tendaguru Formation in Tanzania. Several partial skeletons and skull material make it comparatively informative, but the material is disarticulated and distributed among individuals.","facts":["D. hansemanni occurs lower in the Tendaguru succession than D. sattleri and the two species should not be conflated.","Skulls, vertebral columns and limb elements document a relatively short neck and bifid neural spines.","The old 150-135 Ma range is too young and too broad for the type species."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected from an erroneous Late Jurassic-Early Cretaceous range to the Kimmeridgian lower/middle Tendaguru record.","residualUncertainty":["Association of disarticulated bones to individuals is incomplete.","Neck posture and precise adult mass are model-dependent."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5194/fr-12-141-2009","https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2011.0095","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2015.1008134"]},"dilophosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Sinemurian, approximately 193 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda"],"description":"Dilophosaurus wetherilli was a large early theropod from the Kayenta Formation of Arizona. Several partial skeletons, including substantial skull material, document paired cranial crests and a powerful bipedal predator very unlike its fictionalised venom-spitting portrayal.","facts":["There is no fossil evidence for venom glands or an expandable neck frill.","The paired crests were thin and probably visual structures, though their exact function is unknown.","Modern re-preparation shows a more robust skull and skeleton than older weak-jawed reconstructions."]},"ageReviewNote":"U-Pb dating places the fossil-bearing Kayenta interval near 193 Ma in the Sinemurian.","residualUncertainty":["Several individuals are incomplete and partly distorted.","Crest function and exact position among early neotheropods remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/jpa.2020.14","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dilophosaurus","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0149140","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/dilophosaurus.html"]},"diplodocus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 154-152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Diplodocidae","Diplodocinae"],"description":"Diplodocus was a long-necked diplodocid sauropod from the Morrison Formation of western North America. D. carnegii and related material provide several substantial skeletons and skulls, but famous museum mounts are composites and species assignments have been extensively revised.","facts":["The Carnegie mount combines principal specimens and reconstructed parts; it is not one complete individual.","Peg-like front teeth and low skull support selective cropping, while exact vegetation and feeding height varied with posture.","Extreme tail-whip speeds and supersonic cracking remain biomechanical hypotheses rather than consensus facts."]},"ageReviewNote":"Most diagnostic Diplodocus material comes from Kimmeridgian-Tithonian Morrison intervals near 154-152 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level referral of several historic specimens remains debated.","Neck posture, tail performance and body mass depend on modelling."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5962/bhl.title.46734","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.857","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Diplodocus","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/diplodocus.html"]},"dracorex":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Maastrichtian, about 67-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae","Pachycephalosaurus"],"taxonomicStatus":"probable juvenile synonym of Pachycephalosaurus wyomingensis","acceptedName":false,"description":"Dracorex hogwartsia is based on a single immature, flat-headed pachycephalosaur skull and a few associated vertebrae from the Hell Creek Formation. Histology and growth-series comparisons strongly support interpreting it as a juvenile stage of Pachycephalosaurus rather than a separate accepted genus.","facts":["The holotype is not a complete skeleton.","Its open sutures, thin skull roof and ornament pattern are juvenile features.","A separate adult Dracorex morph has not been demonstrated."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the late Maastrichtian Hell Creek record of the holotype.","residualUncertainty":["The synonymy is strongly supported but depends on an incomplete ontogenetic series.","The precise growth stage of the holotype is not known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0007626","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2016.1078343","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dracorex"]},"dreadnoughtus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian-Maastrichtian, broadly about 84-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Lithostrotia"],"description":"Dreadnoughtus schrani was a giant titanosaur from the Cerro Fortaleza Formation of Patagonia. Two individuals are known, including one associated partial skeleton preserving much of the limbs, girdles and tail but no complete skull, neck or articulated trunk.","facts":["The principal specimen is substantial but not the exceptionally high percentage once claimed when counted by every repeated tail bone.","The original mass estimate near 59 tonnes was challenged by volumetric and scaling studies; no single mass value is consensus.","Bone histology indicates the main individual was still growing."],"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Cerro Fortaleza Formation is Late Cretaceous, but its numerical age is poorly constrained across a wide Campanian-Maastrichtian interval.","residualUncertainty":["Formation age, body mass and exact titanosaur relationships remain debated.","The skull and much of the neck and torso are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/srep06196","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2016.1225303","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dreadnoughtus"]},"dromaeosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 76-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae","Dromaeosaurinae"],"description":"Dromaeosaurus albertensis was a medium-sized predatory dromaeosaurid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. It is based on a partial skull and lower jaw; isolated teeth and fragmentary bones enlarge the record but do not amount to a complete skeleton.","facts":["The skull is incomplete and most of the postcranium is unknown.","Its robust jaws and teeth support carnivory, but prey choice and hunting behaviour are not directly known.","Common full-body reconstructions depend heavily on related dromaeosaurids."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the late Campanian Dinosaur Park Formation rather than a broad Late Cretaceous interval.","residualUncertainty":["Most of the skeleton is unknown.","Referral of isolated material and exact dromaeosaurine relationships remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1995.10011250","https://doi.org/10.1206/748.1","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dromaeosaurus"]},"dromiceiomimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-early Maastrichtian, roughly 76-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria","Ornithomimidae"],"taxonomicStatus":"validity disputed; recent reassessments support separation from Ornithomimus","description":"Dromiceiomimus brevitertius is an ornithomimid name based on Canadian Late Cretaceous skull and skeletal material. It was long synonymized with Ornithomimus edmontonicus, but renewed anatomical work has argued that a distinct, slender-limbed taxon may be recognisable.","facts":["The page must present the synonymy debate, not one resolution as settled fact.","Toothless jaws and long cursorial limbs are documented for the referred ornithomimid material.","Diet remains unresolved and should not be labelled confidently as herbivorous or carnivorous."],"diet":null,"length":null,"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The hypodigm spans more than one Canadian formation and may mix taxa; each referral needs horizon-level checking.","residualUncertainty":["The genus-level validity and boundaries of the referred hypodigm remain disputed.","Age range depends on which specimens are accepted."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/cjes-2018-0162","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0058853","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dromiceiomimus"]},"dryosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 155-148 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia","Ornithopoda","Dryosauridae"],"description":"Dryosaurus altus was a small bipedal dryosaurid herbivore from the Morrison Formation of western North America. Multiple partial skeletons and growth stages document most of the postcranium, while skull material is less complete and historic species referrals have been revised.","facts":["Long hindlimbs and a balancing tail support agile bipedal locomotion.","The hands were not built for weight-bearing in habitual quadrupedal walking.","Some specimens formerly called Dryosaurus belong to Dysalotosaurus or other ornithopods."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure D. altus material spans Kimmeridgian-Tithonian Morrison strata rather than a single narrow horizon.","residualUncertainty":["Skull anatomy and species-level referral of several Morrison specimens remain incomplete.","Maximum adult size and exact dryosaurid relationships vary."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5962/p.234845","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.12362","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dryosaurus"]},"dryptosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-67 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea"],"description":"Dryptosaurus aquilunguis was a large bipedal predatory tyrannosauroid from the New Egypt Formation of New Jersey. It is known chiefly from one incomplete skeleton without a skull, so its body form and exact position within Tyrannosauroidea remain less certain than popular reconstructions imply.","facts":["The holotype includes limb, girdle, vertebral and hand elements but no diagnostic complete skull.","A large manual claw is real, but the full proportions and use of the forelimb are uncertain.","Isolated eastern North American theropod remains should not automatically be referred to Dryptosaurus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The secure type occurrence is Maastrichtian; a broader 84-66 Ma range conflates uncertain referrals.","residualUncertainty":["No skull is securely known.","Precise tyrannosauroid relationships and adult size are unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1997.10011003","https://www.research.ed.ac.uk/files/8230851/PDF_Brusatteetal2011Dryptosaurus.pdf","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dryptosaurus"]},"dubreuillosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian, about 168-166 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Megalosauridae"],"description":"Dubreuillosaurus valesdunensis was a megalosaurid theropod from marine-influenced Bathonian deposits in Normandy, France. A single immature individual preserves a partial skull and much of the postcranium, but it is incomplete and was transported before burial.","facts":["The type was originally named as a species of Poekilopleuron and later given its own genus.","Its long, low skull and serrated teeth support a predatory diet.","The specimen is immature, limiting adult size estimates."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type comes from the middle Bathonian Calcaire de Caen rather than a generic Middle Jurassic range.","residualUncertainty":["Only one immature individual is known.","Some elements were lost or damaged before formal description."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2005)025%5B0850:TPAOTM%5D2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Dubreuillosaurus"]},"edmontonia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-early Maastrichtian, roughly 73-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae"],"description":"Edmontonia longiceps was a large quadrupedal nodosaurid from the Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta. It is known from a skull and several partial skeletons with armour, although much material historically assigned to Edmontonia belongs to other species or cannot be identified that narrowly.","facts":["It lacked the bony tail club of ankylosaurids.","Large shoulder spines and other osteoderms are documented, but complete armour layouts combine multiple individuals.","E. rugosidens is commonly separated as Panoplosaurus or treated in other revised combinations rather than automatically included here."]},"ageReviewNote":"Age tied to the Horseshoe Canyon Formation; species-level referral outside it requires direct anatomical support.","residualUncertainty":["Historic referrals have mixed several nodosaurid taxa.","Armour arrangement and exact formation-level ranges remain incomplete."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2015.1059985","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.12362","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Edmontonia"]},"edmontosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-early Maastrichtian, roughly 73-69 million years ago for E. regalis","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Edmontosaurini"],"description":"Edmontosaurus regalis was a large, broad-beaked saurolophine hadrosaurid from the Horseshoe Canyon and equivalent formations of Canada. Numerous skulls and skeletons across growth stages document its anatomy; later Maastrichtian E. annectens is a separate species and should not be blended into one undifferentiated profile.","facts":["Multiple specimens preserve skin impressions, and one Edmontosaurus specimen preserves a fleshy head comb.","Dental batteries and wear surfaces document sophisticated plant processing.","It could move both quadrupedally and bipedally; habitual speed and herd structure are not directly known."]},"ageReviewNote":"The profile is scoped to E. regalis, primarily about 73-69 Ma; E. annectens extends the genus into the latest Maastrichtian but is taxonomically distinct.","residualUncertainty":["Some historic specimens and formations require species-level reassignment.","Soft-tissue distributions, herd behaviour and locomotor frequencies remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0025186","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2024.2326644","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Edmontosaurus%20regalis","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0175253","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2013.11.008"]},"einiosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, approximately 75.5-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae"],"description":"Einiosaurus procurvicornis was a centrosaurine ceratopsid from the upper Two Medicine Formation of Montana. Two large bonebeds preserve skull and postcranial material from many individuals and growth stages, documenting a forward-curving nasal horn and a distinctive frill.","facts":["The record is a disarticulated multi-individual bonebed, not one complete skeleton.","Nasal horn and frill shape changed during growth.","The bonebeds support group aggregation but do not reveal permanent herd organisation."]},"ageReviewNote":"The upper Two Medicine Formation bonebeds are tightly placed in the late Campanian near 75.5-74 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Bonebed disarticulation complicates individual association.","Horn function, population structure and exact cause of death remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.4202/app.00797.2020","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.1997.tb00340.x","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Einiosaurus"]},"elaphrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian, about 155-152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Noasauridae","Elaphrosaurinae"],"description":"Elaphrosaurus bambergi was a lightly built, long-legged bipedal elaphrosaurine from the Middle Dinosaur Member of the Tendaguru Formation. Its single partial skeleton lacks a skull and hands, so diet and head shape cannot be stated directly.","facts":["The holotype is an associated partial postcranial skeleton, not a complete animal.","Modern analyses place it among noasaurid ceratosaurs rather than ornithomimosaurs.","Tooth loss and herbivory evolved in some close relatives, but cannot be demonstrated directly for Elaphrosaurus because its skull is unknown."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the Kimmeridgian Middle Dinosaur Member of Tendaguru.","residualUncertainty":["The skull, dentition and hands are unknown.","Diet and maximum adult size cannot be directly established."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/zoj.12425","https://doi.org/10.5194/fr-12-141-2009","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Elaphrosaurus"]},"emausaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Toarcian, about 182 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora"],"description":"Emausaurus ernsti was an early armoured ornithischian from the Ciechocinek Formation of northern Germany. It is known from one incomplete, probably immature skeleton with a partial skull and scattered osteoderms; most of the limbs and tail are missing.","facts":["The wiki Middle Jurassic age is incorrect; Emausaurus is Early Jurassic.","The specimen is immature and incomplete.","It was a herbivorous thyreophoran, but its complete armour arrangement is unknown."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected to early Toarcian, near 182 Ma, based on ammonite-constrained marine strata.","residualUncertainty":["Only one immature and incomplete individual is known.","A modern redescription is needed for armour, anatomy and exact thyreophoran position.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.zobodat.at/pdf/Palaeontologische-Abhandlungen-A_15_0001-0124.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1525/california/9780520242098.003.0016","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Emausaurus"]},"enigmacursor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 154-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia"],"diet":null,"description":"Enigmacursor mollyborthwickae was a small bipedal neornithischian from the Morrison Formation of Colorado. It is known from one associated but incomplete skeleton, including much of the limbs, pelvis and vertebral column but no complete skull.","facts":["The specimen was formerly displayed and discussed under the invalid informal name Nanosaurus rex.","It is the most complete small-bodied Morrison neornithischian yet described, but it is not a complete skeleton.","Teeth and close relationships support herbivory, while detailed diet is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The holotype is from the Brushy Basin Member; the numerical range remains formation-level rather than a direct specimen date.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is described.","The skull and precise position within Neornithischia remain poorly known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.242195","https://doi.org/10.2110/jsr.2019.54","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Enigmacursor"]},"eodromaeus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"late Carnian, approximately 231-229 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda?"],"description":"Eodromaeus murphi was a small, lightly built early saurischian from the Ischigualasto Formation of Argentina. Several partial skeletons, including a substantial skull and limbs, support a bipedal animal close to the origin of Theropoda, although some analyses place it just outside the theropod crown.","facts":["Serrated, recurved teeth support small-prey carnivory.","The best specimens are substantial but not complete.","Its importance near the dinosaur root means classification remains sensitive to competing phylogenies."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Cancha de Bochas Member of the Ischigualasto Formation is radiometrically constrained near 231-229 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Its placement as a basal theropod versus non-theropod saurischian remains debated.","Some skeletal associations and adult size are imperfectly known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1198467","https://doi.org/10.1038/nature24011","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Eodromaeus"]},"eolambia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Cenomanian, about 99-98 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauromorpha"],"description":"Eolambia caroljonesa was a large herbivorous hadrosauromorph from the Mussentuchit Member of Utahs Cedar Mountain Formation. Abundant remains from several localities include juveniles and adults, skulls and much of the postcranial skeleton.","facts":["The record represents many individuals, not one nearly complete skeleton.","Jaw and limb anatomy support a primarily quadrupedal herbivore capable of bipedal movement.","It lies outside Hadrosauridae in most modern analyses despite its hadrosaur-like build."]},"ageReviewNote":"High-resolution dating places the Mussentuchit record near 99-98 Ma, much narrower than a generic 99-94 Ma interval.","residualUncertainty":["Exact placement among non-hadrosaurid hadrosauromorphs varies.","Body-mass estimates depend on composite reconstruction."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0045712","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0176896","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Eolambia"]},"eoraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"late Carnian, approximately 231-229 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha?"],"description":"Eoraptor lunensis was a small early saurischian from the Ischigualasto Formation of Argentina. A nearly complete holotype and additional partial specimens preserve much of the skeleton; modern analyses commonly place it near the base of Sauropodomorpha, though dinosaur-root relationships remain debated.","facts":["The mixed tooth shapes do not by themselves prove omnivory.","Its lightly built body was bipedal.","Classification has shifted from basal theropod to basal sauropodomorph or basal saurischian as datasets changed."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"Radiometric dates constrain the fossil-bearing Ischigualasto interval near 231-229 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Root-level saurischian relationships and exact diet remain uncertain.","Some referred material is less complete than the holotype."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/361064a0","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2013.820113","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Eoraptor","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/eoraptor.html"]},"eotyrannus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 126-121 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea"],"description":"Eotyrannus lengi was an early tyrannosauroid from the Wessex Formation of the Isle of Wight. One associated partial skeleton preserves parts of the skull, vertebral column, girdles and limbs, documenting a lightly built, three-fingered relative of later tyrannosaurids.","facts":["Only one incomplete individual is securely known.","The holotype was not fully grown, so adult dimensions are uncertain.","Serrated teeth support carnivory; prey and hunting style are not known."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Wessex Formation occurrence is Barremian, with no direct date on the skeleton.","residualUncertainty":["The skeleton is incomplete and immature.","Its exact position among early tyrannosauroids varies modestly by analysis."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.12727","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1193304","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Eotyrannus"]},"equijubus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, roughly 125-105 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauromorpha"],"description":"Equijubus normani was a non-hadrosaurid hadrosauromorph from the Xinminpu Group of Gansu, China. It is known from one associated partial skull and skeleton; the broad age of its unit remains less secure than many profile dates imply.","facts":["The holotype includes a substantial skull and incomplete postcranium.","Its teeth and jaws support herbivory and its limbs support bipedal-to-facultatively quadrupedal locomotion.","Putative grass microfossils associated with the specimen are controversial and do not securely prove habitual grazing."]},"ageReviewNote":"Aptian-Albian is retained because the middle grey unit of the Xinminpu Group is not narrowly radiometrically constrained.","residualUncertainty":["The formation age is broad.","The biological association and identity of the reported plant microfossils remain debated.","Only one individual is known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/S0195-6671(03)00048-X","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwx145","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Equijubus"]},"erketu":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Santonian, approximately 100-86 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Somphospondyli"],"description":"Erketu ellisoni was a long-necked somphospondylan titanosauriform from the Bayan Shireh Formation of Mongolia. One partial skeleton includes six elongated cervical vertebrae and associated dorsal, pelvic and limb elements, but no skull and only part of the neck.","facts":["Its exceptionally elongate known neck vertebrae do not reveal the exact total neck length because the series is incomplete.","Both the original and later analyses place it among titanosauriforms, on the somphospondylian side of the brachiosaurid-somphospondylian split; its precise relationships remain uncertain.","Quadrupedal herbivory is secure at broad level; feeding height and plants are not."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Bayan Shireh Formation is broadly mid-Late Cretaceous; a narrow numerical date would overstate current correlation.","residualUncertainty":["The skull and most of the tail are unknown.","Formation age and the genus position within Somphospondyli remain under study."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0082(2006)3508%5B1:EEALSF%5D2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1206/3700.2","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Erketu"]},"erlikosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Santonian, approximately 100-86 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Therizinosauria","Therizinosauridae"],"description":"Erlikosaurus andrewsi was a therizinosaurid from the Bayan Shireh Formation of Mongolia. Its holotype preserves a nearly complete skull and an incomplete postcranial skeleton, making the head unusually well known for the group while leaving much of its body reconstructed from relatives.","facts":["The skull has a keratin-covered beak and small leaf-shaped teeth consistent with predominantly plant processing.","Large claws and a broad trunk are inferred partly from other therizinosaurs because the type is incomplete.","A plant-heavy diet is well supported, but exact foods and any animal matter are unknown."],"diet":"Herbivore"},"ageReviewNote":"Uses the broad current age range of the Bayan Shireh Formation rather than false single-million-year precision.","residualUncertainty":["Much of the postcranium is missing.","The formation numerical age and detailed feeding ecology remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.app.pan.pl/archive/published/app25/app25-187.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2014.874529","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Erlikosaurus"]},"euhelopus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"probably Barremian-Aptian, about 129-113 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Euhelopodidae"],"description":"Euhelopus zdanskyi was a long-necked quadrupedal herbivorous titanosauriform from the Mengyin Formation of Shandong, China. Two associated partial skeletons, including a well-preserved skull, make it important, but the age of the Mengyin Formation has a long history of conflicting assignments.","facts":["The type material comprises two individuals with complementary skull and postcranial material.","A complete body is not preserved and the commonly illustrated skeleton is composite.","Modern redescription places it among East Asian somphospondylan titanosauriforms, often in Euhelopodidae."]},"ageReviewNote":"Early Cretaceous follows the modern redescription; older Late Jurassic assignments remain part of the stratigraphic debate and the numerical range is approximate.","residualUncertainty":["The age of the Mengyin Formation is not tightly constrained.","The association and completeness of the two individuals limit size estimates.","Position within Somphospondyli varies among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201908002691","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0079932","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Euhelopus"]},"euoplocephalus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, roughly 76.5-74.4 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae","Ankylosaurinae"],"description":"Euoplocephalus tutus was an ankylosaurid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta and Montana. Multiple skulls and partial skeletons document a broad, armoured quadruped with a tail club, but modern revision removed many historic specimens into Anodontosaurus, Dyoplosaurus and Scolosaurus.","facts":["No one specimen preserves the complete armour layout.","The tail club is real, while its behavioural use is inferred biomechanically.","Taxonomic revision greatly reduced the geographic and temporal range once attributed to Euoplocephalus."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted mainly to the Dinosaur Park Formation, about 76.5-74.4 Ma, rather than an 80-66 Ma composite range.","residualUncertainty":["Some historical referrals remain difficult to allocate.","Armour placement, tail-club behaviour and individual variation are partly inferential."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1096-3642.2003.00045.x","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0062421","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Euoplocephalus","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/euoplocephalus.html"]},"europasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"late Kimmeridgian, about 154 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Brachiosauridae"],"description":"Europasaurus holgeri was a small-bodied brachiosaurid from the Langenberg Quarry in northern Germany. Disarticulated remains from more than a dozen individuals span juvenile to adult growth stages; bone histology supports evolutionary dwarfing in an island environment.","facts":["The assemblage is rich but does not contain one complete articulated skeleton.","Adult status of small individuals is supported by growth marks and slowed growth, not body size alone.","Skulls, vertebrae and limb bones document much of the anatomy across different individuals."]},"ageReviewNote":"The quarry horizon is late Kimmeridgian and commonly placed near 154 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Elements are disarticulated and must be associated by size and morphology.","The exact number of individuals and fine brachiosaurid position vary by study."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04633","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2019.1683770","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Europasaurus"]},"eustreptospondylus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"late Callovian, about 164 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Megalosauridae"],"description":"Eustreptospondylus oxoniensis was a megalosaurid theropod from the Oxford Clay Formation of England. It is founded on one relatively complete but still incomplete subadult skeleton with a partial skull, recovered from marine sediment after its carcass was transported offshore.","facts":["The holotype is not an adult and lacks parts of the skull, limbs and tail.","Marine burial does not show that the animal was aquatic.","Serrated teeth and theropod anatomy support terrestrial carnivory."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the upper Callovian Peterborough Member of the Oxford Clay Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Only one subadult skeleton is securely known.","Adult dimensions and exact megalosaurid position are uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/25761900.2022.12131807","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Eustreptospondylus"]},"fukuiraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian, about 121-113 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Coelurosauria","Megaraptora"],"description":"Fukuiraptor kitadaniensis was a medium-sized bipedal predatory megaraptoran from the Kitadani Formation of Japan. It is represented by a partial type skeleton and additional fragmentary individuals, including juveniles; early allosauroid interpretations are outdated.","facts":["The holotype is a partial skeleton, not a complete mounted animal.","The large hand claw was initially mistaken for a foot claw.","Megaraptoran relationships remain debated between allosauroid and coelurosaur/tyrannosauroid hypotheses."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the Aptian Kitadani Formation; referrals outside it are not used to extend the range.","residualUncertainty":["Megaraptoran higher relationships remain disputed.","Several referred bones are isolated and adult size is uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/e00-064","https://www.dinosaur.pref.fukui.jp/archive/memoir/memoir005-001.pdf","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Fukuiraptor"]},"fukuisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian, about 121-113 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Fukuisaurus tetoriensis was a herbivorous non-hadrosaurid hadrosauroid from Japans Kitadani Formation. The name was founded on associated skull bones, with additional quarry material providing a partial skeleton; the familiar full mount contains reconstructed elements.","facts":["The type series is cranial, centred on a maxilla and jugal.","Additional remains support a medium-sized facultatively quadrupedal iguanodontian body plan.","No complete individual preserves every part shown in museum reconstructions."]},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed range follows the Aptian Kitadani Formation rather than the former 121-99 Ma profile span.","residualUncertainty":["The postcranial hypodigm has not received one comprehensive modern description.","Mass and exact hadrosauroid position vary by reconstruction."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2003)23%5B166:ANIDOF%5D2.0.CO;2","https://dinosaurmuseum.jp/en/dinosaur/fukuisaurus/","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Fukuisaurus"]},"gallimimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Maastrichtian, roughly 70-68 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria","Ornithomimidae"],"description":"Gallimimus bullatus was a large, long-limbed ornithomimid from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. Multiple partial and nearly complete skeletons, including skulls and juveniles, document a toothless, cursorially specialised biped.","facts":["The jaws were toothless and covered by a beak.","Long hindlimbs support efficient running, but a maximum speed cannot be read directly from fossils.","Diet remains unresolved; proposed plant, small-animal and filter-feeding components are hypotheses."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Nemegt Formation is early Maastrichtian in most current schemes, broadly 70-68 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Diet and achievable running speed remain model-dependent.","Some referred specimens require individual reassessment."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/35091139","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gallimimus","https://app.pan.pl/article/item/app17-103.html"]},"gargoyleosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 154-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria"],"description":"Gargoyleosaurus parkpinorum was an early ankylosaur from the Morrison Formation of Wyoming. The holotype includes a partial skull and much of the front half of a skeleton with armour; additional material is limited, and the tail and complete armour arrangement are poorly known.","facts":["The skull is informative but crushed and incomplete.","It was a low, quadrupedal herbivore without evidence for an ankylosaurid tail club.","Its placement just inside Ankylosauria versus near Nodosauridae varies among analyses."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is from the Upper Jurassic Morrison Formation; member-level correlation gives only an approximate numerical age.","residualUncertainty":["The rear body, tail and complete armour layout are incompletely represented.","Basal ankylosaur relationships remain sensitive to analysis."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1127/njgpa/237/2005/111","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0079887","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gargoyleosaurus"]},"garudimimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Santonian, approximately 100-86 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria"],"description":"Garudimimus brevipes was an early-diverging ornithomimosaur from the Bayan Shireh Formation of Mongolia. A single partial skeleton with skull shows a toothless beak, shortened lower legs compared with later ornithomimids and a grasping forelimb.","facts":["The type is associated but incomplete and was altered by damage and early reconstruction.","It was bipedal but probably less cursorially specialised than Gallimimus-like ornithomimids.","Its toothless jaws do not by themselves resolve whether it ate plants, animals or both."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"Formation age is kept broad because Bayan Shireh correlations remain debated.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is known.","Diet, adult variation and exact formation age remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/e05-044","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.1093","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Garudimimus"]},"gasosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian-Callovian, roughly 168-163 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae?"],"description":"Gasosaurus constructus was a medium-sized theropod from the Lower Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan, China. It is based on an incomplete, disarticulated skeleton without a skull; its exact position among ceratosaurs and early tetanurans remains unresolved.","facts":["No diagnostic skull is known.","The principal material includes vertebrae, pelvis and limb bones rather than a complete skeleton.","Carnivory is likely from theropod identity, but detailed feeding claims are unsupported."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Lower Shaximiao Formation is generally Bathonian-Callovian; specimen-level numerical dating is unavailable.","residualUncertainty":["The original description is brief and the material lacks a modern comprehensive redescription.","Its validity and higher-level placement should be checked by a specialist against the type specimen.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gasosaurus","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gasosaurus"]},"gasparinisaura":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, roughly 83-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia","Ornithopoda","Elasmaria"],"description":"Gasparinisaura cincosaltensis was a small bipedal ornithopod from the Anacleto Formation of Patagonia. Several partial skeletons, including juveniles and cranial material, document a lightly built southern neornithischian usually placed in Elasmaria.","facts":["More than one growth stage is known.","Leaf-shaped teeth support herbivory, but exact plants are unknown.","Older assignment to Hypsilophodontidae reflects a now-abandoned catch-all grouping."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Anacleto Formation is Campanian; local horizons are not dated closely enough for a narrow species range.","residualUncertainty":["Exact position within Elasmaria and adult maximum size remain uncertain.","Some specimens are incomplete and taphonomically compressed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022336000035861","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2015.09.009","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gasparinisaura"]},"gastonia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian-early Aptian, roughly 129-123 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae","Polacanthinae"],"description":"Gastonia burgei was a heavily armoured quadrupedal herbivore from the Yellow Cat Member of Utahs Cedar Mountain Formation. Bonebed material represents multiple individuals and includes skulls, much of the skeleton, a pelvic shield and varied osteoderms, but armour positions are partly reconstructed.","facts":["The record is a multi-individual assemblage, not one complete skeleton.","The absence of a tail club and presence of a sacral shield fit a polacanthine nodosaurid interpretation.","Exact species assignments within the broader Gastonia material have been revised."]},"ageReviewNote":"Age follows the Yellow Cat Member and remains approximate because Cedar Mountain member chronology has been repeatedly revised.","residualUncertainty":["The bonebed is disarticulated and armour placement is partly inferential.","The precise age and boundaries of the Yellow Cat Member vary among studies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1127/njgpa/2016/0605","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2014.11.008","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gastonia"]},"giganotosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Cenomanian, roughly 99-97 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Carcharodontosauridae","Carcharodontosaurinae"],"description":"Giganotosaurus carolinii was a giant carcharodontosaurid predator from the Candeleros Formation of Patagonia. It is known from one incomplete skeleton with much of the skull and a referred partial lower jaw; no complete skeleton exists.","facts":["Its skull is reconstructed from incomplete and distorted bones.","It rivalled the largest theropods in length, but claims that it was definitively larger than Tyrannosaurus depend on uncertain reconstructions and mass models.","Blade-like serrated teeth support predation on large vertebrates; specific prey and hunting groups are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Candeleros Formation is early Cenomanian, approximately 99-97 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal skeleton and a referred dentary are known.","Total size, mass, skull proportions and social hunting remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/377224a0","https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2002)022%5B0802:TBOGCD%5D2.0.CO;2","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Giganotosaurus"]},"gigantoraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Campanian, broadly about 96-84 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Oviraptorosauria","Caenagnathidae"],"description":"Gigantoraptor erlianensis was a giant caenagnathid oviraptorosaur from the Iren Dabasu Formation of Inner Mongolia. One partial adult-sized skeleton preserves vertebrae, girdles and limbs but no skull, so its head and feeding apparatus are reconstructed from smaller relatives.","facts":["The holotype is incomplete and lacks the skull, hands and much of the vertebral column.","Bone histology indicates rapid growth and that the individual had not reached maximum skeletal maturity.","Diet remains unresolved; gigantic size and toothless relatives do not identify specific foods."],"diet":null,"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Iren Dabasu Formation age is debated; the broad mid-Late Cretaceous range is more honest than a single narrow date.","residualUncertainty":["The skull is unknown and only one principal skeleton exists.","Formation age, maximum adult size and diet remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature05849","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-15709-7","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gigantoraptor"]},"gilmoreosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Campanian, broadly about 96-84 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Gilmoreosaurus mongoliensis was a non-hadrosaurid hadrosauroid from the Iren Dabasu Formation of Inner Mongolia. The species is represented by disarticulated bones from several individuals, including skull, vertebral and limb elements, but no single complete skeleton.","facts":["The hypodigm is a multi-individual assemblage and associations between elements are limited.","It lies outside Hadrosauridae in most modern analyses.","Teeth and jaws support herbivory; specific browse and herd behaviour are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The numerical age of the Iren Dabasu Formation remains debated, so the audit retains a broad range.","residualUncertainty":["Disarticulation complicates individual association.","Formation age and precise hadrosauroid position are unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/3694.2","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0036745","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gilmoreosaurus"]},"giraffatitan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 155-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Brachiosauridae"],"description":"Giraffatitan brancai was a high-shouldered brachiosaurid sauropod from the Tendaguru Formation of Tanzania. Several individuals provide skull and skeletal material, but the famous Berlin mount is a composite assembled chiefly from two incomplete skeletons and reconstructed elements.","facts":["The genus was originally named Brachiosaurus brancai and is now widely separated from North American Brachiosaurus.","Long forelimbs and elongate neck vertebrae support a high browsing envelope, though exact habitual neck angle is debated.","No single complete skeleton is known."]},"ageReviewNote":"Material spans the Middle and Upper Dinosaur members of Tendaguru, broadly Kimmeridgian-Tithonian.","residualUncertainty":["The mounted skeleton combines individuals.","Species-level referrals, neck posture and exact body mass remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/039.029.0309","https://doi.org/10.5194/fr-12-141-2009","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Giraffatitan"]},"gobisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, approximately 125-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae","Shamosaurinae"],"description":"Gobisaurus domoculus was a large ankylosaur from the Ulansuhai Formation of Inner Mongolia. Its holotype is dominated by a well-preserved skull; associated postcranial remains have been mentioned but are incompletely described, so most body details come from relatives.","facts":["The skull is the primary diagnostic evidence.","A broad muzzle and herbivorous ankylosaur jaw apparatus are documented.","A tail club cannot be confidently described for Gobisaurus from the published type material."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Ulansuhai Formation is Early Cretaceous, but its detailed numerical age has varied among correlations.","residualUncertainty":["Most postcranial material is unpublished or insufficiently described.","Formation age and shamosaurine relationships remain approximate."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/e01-051","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2015.1059985","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gobisaurus"]},"gorgosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, roughly 76.6-75.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Tyrannosauridae","Albertosaurinae"],"description":"Gorgosaurus libratus was an albertosaurine tyrannosaurid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta and equivalent strata in Montana. Dozens of skulls and skeletons spanning juvenile to adult stages make its growth and anatomy unusually well documented.","facts":["Juveniles were more slender and long-legged than adults.","Preserved stomach contents in a juvenile provide direct evidence that at least young Gorgosaurus ate small caenagnathid dinosaurs.","That single meal does not establish the full diet of all ages or coordinated hunting."]},"ageReviewNote":"Most secure G. libratus material comes from the Dinosaur Park Formation, approximately 76.6-75.1 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Age-related ecological partitioning beyond the stomach-content specimen is inferred.","Sociality and population-level hunting behaviour remain unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2021.2041651","https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adi0505","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gorgosaurus","https://doi.org/10.4095/101672","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/gorgosaurus.html"]},"goyocephale":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, roughly 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae"],"description":"Goyocephale lattimorei was a flat-headed pachycephalosaurid from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia. One partial skull and associated incomplete skeleton preserve much of the jaw, skull roof, vertebral column and limbs, but the specimen is not complete.","facts":["The flat skull roof is a genuine feature, though maturity influences pachycephalosaur skull shape.","Large canine-like teeth do not by themselves prove carnivory.","Bipedal locomotion is well supported; detailed diet and head-use behaviour are not."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Baruungoyot Formation is Campanian; its local numerical calibration is approximate.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete principal individual is known.","Maturity, diet and head-strike behaviour remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.app.pan.pl/archive/published/app27/app27-115.pdf","https://www.researchgate.net/publication/254313214_A_taxonomic_review_of_the_Pachycephalosauridae_Dinosauria_Ornithischia","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Goyocephale"]},"graciliceratops":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Santonian, approximately 100-86 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia"],"description":"Graciliceratops mongoliensis was a small neoceratopsian from the Bayan Shireh Formation of Mongolia. It is based on a fragmentary partial skeleton, probably from an immature individual; the skull is represented only by limited jaw and frill material.","facts":["The holotype is neither complete nor securely adult.","Small body size may partly reflect immaturity.","Herbivory is supported by ceratopsian teeth and jaws, but detailed diet is unknown."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"Bayan Shireh dating remains broad and should not be rendered as a precise single age.","residualUncertainty":["The type is fragmentary and immature.","A comprehensive modern redescription and renewed differential diagnosis are needed.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://books.google.com/books?id=ZP9UDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA480","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2018.1509866","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Graciliceratops"]},"gryposaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, approximately 76.5-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Kritosaurini"],"description":"Gryposaurus was a saurolophine hadrosaurid with an arched nasal profile, known from Campanian rocks of western North America. G. notabilis is represented by numerous skulls and skeletons from the Dinosaur Park Formation; other species must be kept separate because they differ in age, geography and diagnosis.","facts":["The nasal arch is bony and likely supported soft tissue, whose exact form is unknown.","Multiple growth stages show that skull proportions changed during ontogeny.","Dental batteries document herbivory and complex chewing."]},"ageReviewNote":"G. notabilis is mainly 76.5-75.5 Ma; other Gryposaurus species extend the genus but should not be merged into that species record.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries and synonymy, including G. incurvimanus, remain debated.","Soft-tissue form over the nasal arch is unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724631003762971","https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.25021","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gryposaurus"]},"guaibasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Norian, roughly 225-208 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia"],"description":"Guaibasaurus candelariensis was a small bipedal saurischian from the Caturrita Formation of southern Brazil. Several partial skeletons provide much of the postcranium but no well-preserved skull; analyses disagree over whether it was an early theropod or a very basal sauropodomorph/saurischian.","facts":["No complete diagnostic skull is known.","A crouched pose in one specimen records burial posture, not habitual resting behaviour with certainty.","Diet cannot be stated confidently while its relationships and teeth remain poorly known."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Caturrita Formation is Norian; its numerical duration remains subject to South American Triassic correlations.","residualUncertainty":["The skull is essentially unknown.","Its position at the base of Saurischia and resulting dietary inference remain disputed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S175569101102007X","https://doi.org/10.1038/nature24011","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Guaibasaurus"]},"guanlong":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, about 161-158 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Proceratosauridae"],"description":"Guanlong wucaii was an early crested tyrannosauroid from the Shishugou Formation of Xinjiang, China. Two associated skeletons, an adult and a juvenile, preserve much of the skull and body and show the long arms and three-fingered hands of early tyrannosauroids.","facts":["The adult skull bears a large, thin midline crest that was structurally unsuited to forceful combat.","The two known individuals provide limited but direct ontogenetic comparison.","Serrated teeth support predation; exact prey and social behaviour are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Ash-bed dating places the relevant Shishugou interval in the Oxfordian, close to 160 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Only two individuals are known.","Display is a plausible function of the crest but cannot be directly demonstrated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04511","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2013.781067","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Guanlong"]},"hadrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, approximately 80-78 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae"],"description":"Hadrosaurus foulkii was a hadrosaurid from the Woodbury Formation of New Jersey. Its historic type is one incomplete skeleton without a skull; limb, pelvic and vertebral material supports a distinct hadrosaurid, but most details of the head must not be reconstructed as if directly known.","facts":["The holotype was the first mounted dinosaur skeleton displayed to the public, but it is far from complete.","No skull is preserved with the type.","It was a large herbivore capable of bipedal and quadrupedal locomotion, as inferred from hadrosaurid anatomy."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Woodbury Formation type horizon is late Campanian; broad 84-66 Ma ranges are not species-level evidence.","residualUncertainty":["The skull is unknown and the skeleton is incomplete.","Its exact position near the base of Saurolophinae varies among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.app.pan.pl/article/item/app51-077.html","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Hadrosaurus"]},"hagryphus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 76-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Oviraptorosauria","Caenagnathidae"],"description":"Hagryphus giganteus was a caenagnathid oviraptorosaur from the Kaiparowits Formation of Utah. The holotype consists mainly of a partial hand and associated foot fragments, so the skull, trunk and most body proportions are unknown.","facts":["The genus is diagnosed from very limited appendicular material.","Large body-size estimates are extrapolated and should be presented as uncertain.","Diet cannot be determined because no skull or jaws are known for the taxon."],"diet":null,"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the late Campanian Kaiparowits Formation, approximately 76-74 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Most anatomy, adult size and diet are unknown.","Fragmentary material makes comparison with other caenagnathids difficult."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2005)025%5B0897:ANOTMF%5D2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-19896-w","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Hagryphus"]},"haplocanthosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian, roughly 155-152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Diplodocoidea"],"description":"Haplocanthosaurus was a moderately large quadrupedal herbivorous sauropod from the Morrison Formation of the western United States. Several incomplete skeletons represent H. priscus and H. delfsi, but skull material is extremely limited and no single specimen is complete.","facts":["The genus is diagnosed largely from vertebral and limb anatomy.","Most modern analyses place it near the base of Diplodocoidea, though exact position varies.","Adult length and mass estimates depend on incomplete specimens."]},"ageReviewNote":"Known horizons fall mainly in older-to-middle Morrison intervals, broadly Kimmeridgian.","residualUncertainty":["Cranial anatomy is almost unknown.","Species boundaries, referrals and basal diplodocoid position need further work."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.25520","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.857","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Haplocanthosaurus"]},"harpymimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Albian, about 113-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria"],"description":"Harpymimus okladnikovi was an early-diverging ornithomimosaur from the Khuren Dukh Formation of Mongolia. A single partial skeleton includes a skull and lower-jaw teeth, showing an intermediate stage before the fully toothless jaws of later ornithomimids.","facts":["The holotype is incomplete but associated and includes much of the skeleton.","Small teeth are restricted to the lower jaw.","It was bipedal; its exact diet cannot be reduced confidently to herbivory, carnivory or omnivory."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Albian range reflects the Khuren Dukh Formation; older broad Early Cretaceous ranges lack equivalent resolution.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is known.","Diet and exact position among early ornithomimosaurs remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://books.google.com/books?id=1ACFGKQJnyUC&pg=PA97","https://doi.org/10.1139/e05-044","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Harpymimus"]},"herrerasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"late Carnian, approximately 231-229 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Herrerasauridae"],"description":"Herrerasaurus ischigualastensis was an early predatory dinosaur from the Ischigualasto Formation of Argentina. Multiple skeletons, including complete skulls and very substantial postcrania, document a lightly built biped; whether herrerasaurids lie inside Theropoda or just outside the sauropodomorph-theropod split remains debated.","facts":["Ischisaurus cattoi and Frenguellisaurus ischigualastensis are generally regarded as synonyms of Herrerasaurus.","Recurved serrated teeth and grasping hands support carnivory.","Its mixture of primitive and derived features makes root-level classification unstable."]},"ageReviewNote":"The principal Ischigualasto occurrences are radiometrically constrained near 231-229 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Its position inside or outside Theropoda remains disputed.","Some functional and locomotor details are reconstructed from differently preserved individuals."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.258.5085.1137","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1994.10011523","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Herrerasaurus","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/herrerasaurus.html"]},"hesperosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian, approximately 156-152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae"],"taxonomicStatus":"valid species; generic placement disputed","acceptedName":true,"description":"Hesperosaurus mjosi was a stegosaurid from the Morrison Formation of Wyoming and Montana. Partial skeletons include skull and armour, but some researchers combine the species as Stegosaurus mjosi while others retain Hesperosaurus as a distinct genus.","facts":["The type is incomplete but includes rare cranial material and dorsal plates.","The characteristic plate and tail-spike arrangement is reconstructed from more than one specimen.","It was a quadrupedal herbivore; exact social behaviour and display functions are not directly known."]},"ageReviewNote":"Hesperosaurus-bearing lower Morrison strata are Kimmeridgian and generally older than classic Stegosaurus stenops horizons.","residualUncertainty":["Generic separation from Stegosaurus remains disputed.","Armour orientation and functions remain partly inferential."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00015-010-0023-3","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2017.1406366","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Hesperosaurus"]},"heterodontosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Hettangian-Sinemurian, roughly 200-190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Heterodontosauridae","Heterodontosaurinae"],"description":"Heterodontosaurus tucki was a small bipedal ornithischian from the upper Elliot Formation of South Africa. Several skulls and one nearly complete skeleton document a compact herbivore with a beak, cheek teeth and enlarged canine-like teeth.","facts":["The profile Late Triassic age is incorrect; secure material is Early Jurassic.","Canine-like teeth do not prove a carnivorous diet and may have had display or defence roles.","Jaw mechanics and tooth wear support effective plant processing, while opportunistic animal matter remains possible but unproven."]},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected to Early Jurassic based on upper Elliot Formation stratigraphy.","residualUncertainty":["The function and sex distribution of the caniniform teeth remain unclear.","Exact diet breadth and heterodontosaurid relationships continue to be debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2011.00697.x","https://doi.org/10.1038/264324a0","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Heterodontosaurus","https://doi.org/10.1038/1961074a0","https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.66036"]},"heyuannia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, roughly 83-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Oviraptorosauria","Oviraptoridae"],"taxonomicStatus":"accepted species with disputed generic combination","description":"The Mongolian oviraptorid long known as Ingenia yanshini was renamed Ajancingenia because Ingenia was preoccupied, and has subsequently been combined as Heyuannia yanshini by some analyses. It is represented by a partial skull and skeleton from the Baruungoyot Formation; this page must explain the nomenclatural and phylogenetic disagreement.","facts":["The profile species is Mongolian and is not the Chinese type species Heyuannia huangi.","The holotype preserves substantial skeletal material but is not complete.","Oviraptorid jaws were toothless and locomotion bipedal, while exact diet remains unresolved."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected from a 72-68 Ma range to the older Campanian Baruungoyot record; the formation is not securely late Maastrichtian.","residualUncertainty":["Whether yanshini should remain in Ajancingenia or be combined with Heyuannia is not settled.","Diet cannot be specified from the toothless skull alone."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.11646/zootaxa.3737.2.6","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2017.10.023","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Ajancingenia"]},"homalocephale":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-early Maastrichtian, roughly 75-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae"],"taxonomicStatus":"tentatively valid; possible immature Prenocephale debated","description":"Homalocephale calathocercos was a flat-headed pachycephalosaurid from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia. It is based on one incomplete, probably immature skeleton with a partial skull; its distinctness from Prenocephale has been questioned but is not conclusively rejected.","facts":["The individual was not fully mature, complicating diagnosis from skull shape.","The famous broad pelvis does not demonstrate live birth or any particular reproductive posture.","Herbivory or omnivory remains a broad inference rather than direct dietary proof."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Nemegt Formation spans the latest Campanian or early Maastrichtian into the late Maastrichtian; precise correlation remains debated.","residualUncertainty":["Ontogeny may account for some diagnostic features.","Only one incomplete individual is known and genus-level validity remains debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2011.546287","https://www.app.pan.pl/archive/published/app19/app19-045.pdf","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Homalocephale"]},"huayangosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian-Callovian, roughly 168-163 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Huayangosauridae"],"description":"Huayangosaurus taibaii was an early stegosaur from the Lower Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan, China. Several partial skeletons, including skulls and armour, document a small-bodied quadrupedal herbivore, but no one specimen preserves every part of the animal.","facts":["Cranial anatomy is unusually well represented for a stegosaur.","Shoulder spines and dorsal plates are known, while their exact sequence and orientation combine evidence from different specimens.","It retained several primitive features relative to later stegosaurids."]},"ageReviewNote":"Lower Shaximiao strata are generally Bathonian-Callovian; published numerical correlations still vary.","residualUncertainty":["The hypodigm combines disarticulated and incomplete individuals.","The complete armour arrangement and exact formation age remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1992.10011463","https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2006)26%5B944:ROTPSO%5D2.0.CO;2","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Huayangosaurus"]},"hylaeosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Valanginian, about 140-133 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae"],"description":"Hylaeosaurus armatus was a quadrupedal armoured herbivore from the Grinstead Clay Formation of southern England. Its name is based on one incomplete, partly articulated skeleton with vertebrae, girdle and limb elements and large spines; most other British Wealden ankylosaur material cannot be referred to it merely by geography.","facts":["The holotype lacks a complete skull and much of the limbs.","Large spines were preserved near the shoulder/neck region, but the full armour arrangement is unknown.","Modern revision retains Hylaeosaurus as distinct from Polacanthus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type horizon is Valanginian Grinstead Clay, not a 150-135 Ma interval extending into the Jurassic.","residualUncertainty":["Most of the skull and many limb elements are unknown.","The original position of individual osteoderms is incompletely documented."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2020.1826956","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/armored-dinosaurs/dermal-armor-of-polacanthine-dinosaurs/7D939DE2659A77425B990A10CE7A65AB","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Hylaeosaurus"]},"hypacrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Maastrichtian, roughly 71-69 million years ago for H. altispinus","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae"],"description":"Hypacrosaurus altispinus was a tall-spined lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from the Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta. Several skulls and skeletons document a hollow-crested herbivore; famous nests, eggs and embryos belong to the distinct species H. stebingeri and must not be attributed automatically to H. altispinus.","facts":["The tall neural spines formed a deep back profile, not necessarily a thin sail.","The hollow crest contained nasal passages plausibly involved in sound production, though exact calls are modelled.","Nesting and embryology evidence is species-specific to H. stebingeri."]},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected to early Maastrichtian for H. altispinus; the profile must not use the older Two Medicine age of H. stebingeri.","residualUncertainty":["Genus monophyly and distinctions between H. altispinus and H. stebingeri continue to be tested.","Crest acoustics and display are biomechanical inferences."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00611.x","https://doi.org/10.1139/cjes-2025-0094","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Hypacrosaurus%20altispinus"]},"hypsilophodon":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, roughly 126-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia","Ornithopoda"],"description":"Hypsilophodon foxii was a small bipedal herbivorous ornithopod from the Wessex Formation of the Isle of Wight. Numerous skulls and skeletons, many from a restricted locality, document a lightly built runner with a stiffened tail.","facts":["The old idea that Hypsilophodon climbed trees is not supported by modern anatomy.","There is no bony body armour; supposed plates were misidentified material.","Its hands and feet are consistent with terrestrial bipedal locomotion."]},"ageReviewNote":"The main Hypsilophodon bed is Barremian, close to 126-125 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Some historic referred material and exact neornithischian position remain under review.","Specific vegetation and social organisation are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5962/p.313819","https://doi.org/10.1525/california/9780520242098.003.0018","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Hypsilophodon"]},"iguanodon":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Barremian-early Aptian, roughly 126-122 million years ago for I. bernissartensis","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Ankylopollexia","Styracosterna"],"description":"Iguanodon bernissartensis was a large herbivorous iguanodontian best known from the Bernissart mine in Belgium, where numerous associated skeletons and skulls were recovered. Modern revision restricts Iguanodon more narrowly than older books that placed many Early Cretaceous species from Europe and elsewhere in the genus.","facts":["The thumb spike was on the hand, not the nose as in the earliest reconstruction.","Forelimb and hand anatomy support habitual quadrupedal walking with facultative bipedality.","The Bernissart assemblage contains multiple individuals but does not by itself prove a permanent herd."]},"ageReviewNote":"I. bernissartensis is late Barremian-early Aptian; older broad genus ranges largely reflect reassigned species.","residualUncertainty":["Some historical European specimens remain difficult to assign.","Exact gait frequency, herd behaviour and species boundaries outside Bernissart remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2007.04.009","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2017.02.006","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Iguanodon%20bernissartensis","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/iguanodon.html"]},"indosuchus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauridae?"],"description":"Indosuchus raptorius is a name based on fragmentary skull bones from the Lameta Formation of India. The material has generally been interpreted as abelisaurid, but it is incomplete, was collected without a tightly documented association and may not be diagnosable against other Indian abelisaurids.","facts":["No associated complete skeleton is known.","The type material is cranial and fragmentary.","Carnivory is secure only at the broad theropod level; size and appearance are highly uncertain."],"taxonomicStatus":"validity and diagnosis disputed","length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Lameta Formation record is Maastrichtian; precise local ages and associations are limited.","residualUncertainty":["Type association, diagnosis and distinction from other Lameta abelisaurids require specialist re-examination.","Most body anatomy is unknown.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.22179/revmacn.6.74","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Indosuchus"]},"irritator":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Aptian, roughly 113-110 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Spinosauridae","Spinosaurinae"],"diet":null,"description":"Irritator challengeri was a spinosaurid theropod from the Romualdo Formation of northeastern Brazil. It is known principally from one nearly complete but artificially altered skull; no associated postcranial skeleton is known, so body shape and locomotion must be inferred from other spinosaurs.","facts":["Commercial fossil dealers modified parts of the skull before scientific study, and later preparation corrected the reconstruction.","Long conical teeth and a narrow snout support catching relatively small prey, including fish, but not an exclusively fish diet.","The proposed synonymy with Angaturama limai remains debated."],"length":null,"massKg":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Romualdo Formation is late Aptian in current correlations, broadly 113-110 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Postcranial anatomy is unknown for the holotype.","Angaturama synonymy, precise provenance and feeding breadth remain unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.26879/1242","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0187070","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Irritator"]},"isisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Lithostrotia"],"description":"Isisaurus colberti was a titanosaur from the Lameta Formation of India. Its holotype is an associated but incomplete skeleton with much of the neck, forequarters, vertebral column and limbs; it lacks a complete skull and tail.","facts":["The species was originally named Titanosaurus colberti and transferred to Isisaurus when Titanosaurus became taxonomically problematic.","Its tall shoulders and relatively short neck proportions are based on preserved vertebrae and limbs, not a complete articulated mount.","It was a quadrupedal herbivore; exact browse height and diet are not directly known."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the Maastrichtian Lameta Formation rather than a broad Late Cretaceous span.","residualUncertainty":["The skull, distal tail and parts of the skeleton are missing.","Relationships within Lithostrotia and the precise Lameta horizon remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/291789651_New_titanosaurid_Dinosauria_Sauropoda_from_the_Late_Cretaceous_of_India","https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201903001044","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Isisaurus"]},"janenschia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Tithonian, roughly 152-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauriformes?"],"taxonomicStatus":"valid genus with restricted hypodigm; phylogenetic placement uncertain","description":"Janenschia robusta was a large sauropod from the Upper Dinosaur Member of the Tendaguru Formation in Tanzania. The genus is securely based on a partial hindlimb and associated tail material; many bones historically referred to it have since been reassigned or regarded as indeterminate.","facts":["The accepted hypodigm is much smaller than older museum reconstructions implied.","No diagnostic skull is known.","Its placement as a basal titanosauriform, titanosaur or other eusauropod differs among analyses."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type horizon is Tithonian Upper Dinosaur Member, not Early Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["Historic referrals have inflated the known skeleton.","A modern specimen-level revision is still needed to resolve sauropod relationships."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.schweizerbart.de/publications/detail/isbn/9783510611724","https://doi.org/10.5194/fr-12-141-2009","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Janenschia"]},"jaxartosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Coniacian-Santonian, approximately 90-84 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae"],"description":"Jaxartosaurus aralensis was a hadrosaurid from dinosaur-bearing beds near Kyrk-Kuduk and Alym-Tau in southern Kazakhstan. Its type material is chiefly an incomplete skull; fragmentary postcranial bones from the same deposit have also been referred, but no complete skeleton is known.","facts":["The skull is incomplete and the same-deposit postcranium is fragmentary.","The shape of a complete crest cannot be recovered confidently from the preserved type alone.","Herbivory is secure at the hadrosaurid level, but detailed ecology is unknown."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Rozhdestvensky placed the southern Kazakhstan dinosaur beds containing Jaxartosaurus in the Coniacian-Santonian; the unrelated Syuk-Syuk occurrence is excluded.","residualUncertainty":["The type is fragmentary and lacks a modern comprehensive redescription.","Crest form, referred postcrania and precise lambeosaurine relationships require specialist review.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Rozhdestvensky%25201968.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0069835","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Jaxartosaurus"]},"jingshanosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"probably Sinemurian-Pliensbachian, roughly 199-183 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropodiformes"],"description":"Jingshanosaurus xinwaensis was a large bipedal-to-facultatively quadrupedal sauropodiform from the Lower Lufeng Formation of Yunnan, China. A skull and substantial skeleton diagnose the genus, and a modern cranial revision synonymized Chuxiongosaurus lufengensis with it.","facts":["The type is relatively informative but not a complete articulated skeleton.","Its teeth and sauropodomorph jaw anatomy indicate herbivory, possibly with opportunistic feeding not directly testable.","It lies close to the origin of Sauropoda rather than within traditional Prosauropoda as a natural group."]},"ageReviewNote":"Lower Lufeng units are Early Jurassic; a narrow 205-190 Ma headline overstates their dating precision.","residualUncertainty":["The Lower Lufeng numerical age remains debated.","Postcranial anatomy still lacks a complete modern redescription."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.24113","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67754-4","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Jingshanosaurus"]},"jinzhousaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"early Aptian, roughly 125-122 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Jinzhousaurus yangi was a basal hadrosauroid from the Yixian Formation of Liaoning, China. The holotype is a largely complete articulated skeleton with a skull, although the specimen is flattened and some bones are obscured or damaged.","facts":["The skeleton is unusually complete but not perfectly preserved.","Its jaws and teeth indicate herbivory.","Modern analyses place it near the transition between non-hadrosauroid iguanodontians and Hadrosauroidea."]},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant Yixian strata are early Aptian, around 125-122 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Compression and preparation obscure some anatomy.","Its exact branching position near Hadrosauroidea varies among matrices."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.app.pan.pl/archive/published/app54/app54-035.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0036745","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Jinzhousaurus"]},"jobaria":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"probably late Middle Jurassic, about 168-161 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Jobaria tiguidensis was a large quadrupedal herbivorous eusauropod from the Tiouraren Formation of Niger. Several individuals provide skull and postcranial material, but the assemblage is disarticulated and the formation was long misdated as Early Cretaceous.","facts":["The material represents multiple individuals and should not be described as one complete skeleton.","A 2009 stratigraphic reassessment favours a late Middle Jurassic age.","Its exact position among non-neosauropod eusauropods varies across analyses."]},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected from Early Cretaceous to the late Middle Jurassic interpretation of the Tiouraren Formation; no direct radiometric date is available.","residualUncertainty":["The formation lacks direct radiometric dates.","The association of disarticulated material and exact eusauropod position remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.286.5443.1342","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2008.10.019","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Jobaria"]},"juravenator":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"late Kimmeridgian, about 152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria"],"description":"Juravenator starki was a very small juvenile coelurosaur from the Painten Formation of Germany. One nearly complete articulated skeleton preserves skin impressions and filament-like integument in different body regions, but juvenile status and mosaic soft tissues complicate comparison with adult relatives.","facts":["Only one juvenile individual is known.","The specimen preserves both scale impressions and simple filamentous structures; this is not evidence that the entire adult was uniformly scaly or feathered.","Small sharp teeth support carnivory or insectivory broadly, not a specific prey list."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Painten occurrence is late Kimmeridgian rather than a broad 155-145 Ma span.","residualUncertainty":["Adult anatomy is unknown.","Its exact coelurosaur position and distribution of integument over the body remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04579","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2020.08.066","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Juravenator"]},"kentrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 155-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae"],"description":"Kentrosaurus aethiopicus was a stegosaurid from the Tendaguru Formation of Tanzania. Abundant disarticulated remains from many individuals represent most of the skeleton and armour, but no single complete skeleton preserves the full sequence of plates and spikes.","facts":["The familiar armour arrangement is a composite reconstruction supported by repeated isolated osteoderm shapes.","Tail spikes provided a plausible defensive weapon, as supported by anatomy and biomechanical modelling.","Bone histology records growth but does not provide exact population ages for every specimen."]},"ageReviewNote":"Most material comes from Kimmeridgian-Tithonian Middle and Upper Dinosaur members of Tendaguru.","residualUncertainty":["No complete articulated armour sequence is known.","Individual variation, exact plate orientation and detailed species-level stratigraphic range remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.22701","https://doi.org/10.5194/fr-12-141-2009","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Kentrosaurus","https://doi.org/10.1127/0077-7749/2011/0114","https://doi.org/10.1007/s00015-010-0024-2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/kentrosaurus.html"]},"khaan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, roughly 75-71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Oviraptorosauria","Oviraptoridae"],"description":"Khaan mckennai was a small oviraptorid from the Djadokhta Formation of Mongolia. Several well-preserved skeletons with skulls document most of its anatomy, including toothless jaws and a short, deep skull.","facts":["Multiple associated individuals are known, rather than a single complete specimen.","Proposed sex differences in tail anatomy remain hypotheses because skeletal sex has not been independently confirmed.","Its exact diet is unresolved despite the toothless beak."],"diet":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Djadokhta Formation is generally Campanian; local numerical ages remain approximate.","residualUncertainty":["Diet and proposed sexual dimorphism are not directly resolved.","The number and association of some referred specimens need specimen-level care."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/803.1","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2017.10.023","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Khaan"]},"kotasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"probably Toarcian, roughly 183-174 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda"],"description":"Kotasaurus yamanpalliensis was an early sauropod from the Kota Formation of India. A disarticulated bonebed represents at least a dozen individuals and includes much of the postcranial skeleton, but almost no informative skull material.","facts":["The assemblage is multi-individual, not a complete skeleton.","Robust limbs support habitual quadrupedal locomotion.","The age of the Kota Formation and its position near the base of Sauropoda are both actively discussed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The lower Kota Formation is usually treated as Early Jurassic and often Toarcian, but radiometric control is limited.","residualUncertainty":["Cranial anatomy is essentially unknown.","Formation age, element association and exact basal sauropod position remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2001)021%5B0242:TOOKYA%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1525/9780520941434-018/html","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Kotasaurus"]},"kritosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, approximately 74-73 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Kritosaurini"],"description":"Kritosaurus navajovius was a saurolophine hadrosaurid from the Kirtland Formation of New Mexico. Its type is an incomplete skull with missing snout and lower jaw; many historic North and South American referrals have been removed to other kritosaurins.","facts":["The famous high nasal arch was partly reconstructed in the original specimen and is now interpreted more cautiously.","No complete type skeleton exists.","Herbivory and sophisticated dental-battery chewing are secure at hadrosaurid level."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the De-na-zin Member of the Kirtland Formation, late Campanian.","residualUncertainty":["The type skull is incomplete and postcranial referrals are limited.","Species limits among North American kritosaurins continue to be revised."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2013.770417","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Kritosaurus"]},"lambeosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, roughly 76.7-74.4 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae","Lambeosaurini"],"description":"Lambeosaurus was a hollow-crested lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta and equivalent strata. L. lambei is represented by multiple skulls and partial skeletons; L. magnicristatus is a separate, rarer species and should not be merged uncritically with it.","facts":["The crest enclosed extended nasal passages and changed shape during growth.","Acoustic and display functions are plausible and modelled, but exact calls and behaviours are unknown.","Dental batteries document herbivory and complex plant processing."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure Dinosaur Park Formation occurrences are late Campanian, approximately 76.7-74.4 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries, ontogenetic crest variation and some Montana referrals remain debated.","Crest acoustics cannot recover exact vocalisations."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2007)27%5B373:AAROLM%5D2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0069835","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lambeosaurus","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/lambeosaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00611.x"]},"lapparentosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian, roughly 168-166 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Lapparentosaurus madagascariensis was an early eusauropod from the Isalo III deposits of Madagascar. It is known from disarticulated postcranial material from several mostly immature individuals; no diagnostic skull and no complete skeleton are known.","facts":["Growth rings and bone tissues provide information on juvenile development.","Most body reconstruction combines bones from different individuals.","Its exact position among early eusauropods is not stable."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Isalo III fauna is generally Bathonian; correlations and local horizon control remain approximate.","residualUncertainty":["The material is disarticulated, mostly immature and lacks a skull.","Age, hypodigm and phylogenetic position require further specimen-level work."],"sourceUrls":["https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/210400/","https://documentsdelivered.com/source/036/039/036039814.php","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lapparentosaurus"]},"leaellynasaura":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Aptian-early Albian, approximately 115-110 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia","Ornithopoda?"],"description":"Leaellynasaura amicagraphica was a small neornithischian from the Eumeralla Formation of Victoria, Australia. Secure material is centred on an incomplete skull and referred maxillary remains; the long-tailed postcranial skeletons often illustrated as Leaellynasaura cannot currently be referred to the genus with confidence.","facts":["The wiki must not present the famous long tail as securely belonging to Leaellynasaura.","The type is an incomplete cranium from a polar Cretaceous environment.","Bipedality and herbivory are likely at broad neornithischian level, but most body anatomy is unknown."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Eumeralla Formation record is late Aptian-early Albian, with local dates near 115-110 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The postcranial hypodigm is unresolved.","Fine phylogenetic position and adult size remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/jpa.2018.95","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.6008","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Leaellynasaura"]},"leptoceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Maastrichtian, roughly 69-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia","Leptoceratopsidae"],"description":"Leptoceratops gracilis was a small, robust neoceratopsian from latest Cretaceous rocks of western North America. Several skulls and partial skeletons document a beaked herbivore with powerful jaws and a short frill but no large horns.","facts":["Multiple individuals provide good cranial and postcranial coverage, though no one specimen is perfectly complete.","Dental wear shows complex plant processing.","It was probably mostly quadrupedal, with locomotor flexibility still discussed."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure occurrences are late Maastrichtian in the Scollard, Lance and equivalent latest Cretaceous units.","residualUncertainty":["Exact locomotor proportions and species-level referral of isolated material remain debated.","Detailed plant preferences are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.2132","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2018.1509866","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Leptoceratops"]},"lesothosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Hettangian-Sinemurian, roughly 201-190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia"],"description":"Lesothosaurus diagnosticus was a small bipedal herbivorous ornithischian from the upper Elliot Formation of Lesotho and South Africa. Numerous skulls and partial skeletons provide a strong anatomical record; the larger name Stormbergia is widely regarded as growth-stage variation within Lesothosaurus.","facts":["Multiple growth stages are represented.","Its simple leaf-shaped teeth and beak support plant feeding, though opportunistic animal matter cannot be tested.","It was a lightly built biped rather than a member of a single natural group called Hypsilophodontidae."]},"ageReviewNote":"Upper Elliot occurrences are Early Jurassic, primarily Hettangian-Sinemurian.","residualUncertainty":["Some growth-related referrals and its exact position near the base of Neornithischia remain debated.","Diet breadth cannot be measured directly."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.1494","https://doi.org/10.1111/zoj.12434","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lesothosaurus"]},"liaoceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"early Aptian, around 125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia"],"description":"Liaoceratops yanzigouensis was a small early neoceratopsian from the Lujiatun beds of the Yixian Formation in Liaoning, China. Several skulls and partial skeletons show an early stage in the evolution of the ceratopsian frill and cheek region.","facts":["More than one individual and growth stage is known.","The frill was small and lacked the enlarged horns of ceratopsids.","Beak and tooth anatomy support herbivory; exact plants are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Radiometric dating places the Lujiatun beds close to 125 Ma in the early Aptian.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred specimens and ontogenetic differences need continued study.","Exact branching order among early neoceratopsians varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/416314a","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2018.1509866","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Liaoceratops"]},"ligabuesaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Aptian-early Albian, about 113-108 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Titanosauriformes","Somphospondyli"],"description":"Ligabuesaurus leanzai was a quadrupedal herbivorous titanosauriform from the Lohan Cura Formation of Argentina. The holotype and a referred specimen provide an incomplete skeleton; modern redescription places it outside or near the base of Titanosauria rather than securely within a narrow titanosaur family.","facts":["Known material includes vertebral, girdle and limb elements and limited cranial remains.","No complete neck, tail or articulated body is known.","Adult length and mass estimates remain extrapolations from incomplete individuals."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the late Aptian-early Albian Lohan Cura Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred material is incomplete and association is imperfect.","Placement near Titanosauria and adult size vary among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlac003","https://www.researchgate.net/publication/237609887_Ligabuesaurus_leanzai_gen_et_sp_nov_Dinosauria_Sauropoda_a_new_titanosaur_from_the_Lohan_Cura_Formation_Aptian_Lower_Cretaceous_of_Neuquen_Patagonia_Argentina","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Ligabuesaurus"]},"liliensternus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"late Norian, roughly 208-205 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda"],"description":"Liliensternus liliensterni was a medium-sized early theropod from late Norian Knollenmergel beds in Thuringia, equivalent to the Trossingen Formation succession elsewhere in Germany. Its principal material comprises disarticulated partial skeletons representing at least two individuals, with only fragmentary cranial remains; a second species once assigned to it is now Lophostropheus.","facts":["No complete skull or skeleton is known.","The principal L. liliensterni record is late Norian and should not be extended into the Rhaetian using former L. airelensis material.","Bipedality and carnivory are supported at broad theropod level.","Its exact position relative to coelophysoids and dilophosaur-grade theropods is unsettled."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the late Norian Knollenmergel material from Thuringia; Rhaetian-Hettangian material formerly called L. airelensis belongs to Lophostropheus.","residualUncertainty":["The type requires a comprehensive modern redescription.","Cranial anatomy, species diagnosis and fine phylogeny remain poorly resolved.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.dinochecker.com/papers/A_review_of_European_Triassic_theropods_Rahaut%26Hungerbuhler_%281998%29.pdf","https://doi.org/10.3897/fr.28.164405","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Liliensternus"]},"lophorhothon":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Campanian, approximately 83-80 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea"],"taxonomicStatus":"valid hadrosauroid; position near Hadrosauridae disputed","description":"Lophorhothon atopus was a hadrosauroid from the marine Mooreville Chalk of Alabama. The holotype is an incomplete juvenile skull and skeleton, supplemented by new fragmentary adult-sized material; transport into marine sediment does not imply an aquatic lifestyle.","facts":["The type individual is immature.","Its crest and adult skull proportions are not fully known.","It was a terrestrial herbivore at the hadrosauroid grade."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Mooreville Chalk occurrence is early Campanian rather than spanning the whole Late Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["The type is juvenile and incomplete.","Its position inside or immediately outside Hadrosauridae remains analysis-dependent."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/cjes-2020-0173","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lophorhothon"]},"lophostropheus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"near the Triassic-Jurassic boundary, about 201 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda","Coelophysoidea?"],"description":"Lophostropheus airelensis was an early neotheropod from the Moon-Airel Formation of Normandy, France. It is known from fragmentary vertebral, pelvic and other postcranial remains without a skull; the material was formerly assigned to Halticosaurus and Liliensternus.","facts":["No skull or diagnostic teeth are known.","Its age close to the Triassic-Jurassic boundary makes it important but does not improve skeletal completeness.","Carnivory is inferred broadly from theropod identity."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Moon-Airel assemblage lies close to the Rhaetian-Hettangian boundary near 201 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The material is fragmentary and headless.","Its coelophysoid placement and individual association remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2007)27%5B73:TCLAGN%5D2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lophostropheus"]},"lufengosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Sinemurian-Pliensbachian, roughly 199-183 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massospondylidae"],"description":"Lufengosaurus huenei was a medium-to-large herbivorous sauropodomorph from the Lower Lufeng Formation of Yunnan, China. Numerous skeletons, skulls and embryonic material document its anatomy and growth, although some historically referred specimens and a second named species require careful taxonomic separation.","facts":["Multiple substantially complete individuals are known; occurrence count is not a completeness measure.","Embryonic bones and eggs provide direct growth evidence, not proof of a specific parental-care strategy.","It was primarily bipedal but could plausibly use the forelimbs in slow locomotion or feeding; exact gait frequencies are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Lower Lufeng age is Early Jurassic but numerical limits vary; the record is not restricted securely to 200-195 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The numerical age of Lower Lufeng units is imprecise.","Taxonomic boundaries among Lufeng sauropodomorph specimens need continued revision.","Parental care is not directly demonstrated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature11978","https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2005)025%5B0806:COOLHY%5D2.0.CO;2","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lufengosaurus"]},"lurdusaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian, about 125-113 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Styracosterna"],"diet":null,"description":"Lurdusaurus arenatus was an unusually robust quadrupedal-to-facultatively bipedal iguanodontian from the Elrhaz Formation of Niger. It is known from one incomplete associated skeleton without a skull; reconstructions of a very low, barrel-bodied animal are based on its robust limb and girdle proportions.","facts":["The holotype preserves much of the postcranium but no diagnostic skull.","Herbivory is inferred from iguanodontian relationships; detailed feeding anatomy is unknown.","Claims of semiaquatic habits are speculative and not established consensus."]},"ageReviewNote":"Restricted to the Aptian Elrhaz Formation rather than the former 121-112 Ma generic interval.","residualUncertainty":["The skull is unknown and only one principal skeleton is available.","Body mass, habitual posture and any aquatic association are uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doc.rero.ch/record/14477/files/PAL_E1671.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2007.04.009","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lurdusaurus"]},"lusotitan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, roughly 152-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria","Brachiosauridae"],"description":"Lusotitan atalaiensis was a large brachiosaurid sauropod from the Lourinha Formation of Portugal. Its name is based on a substantial but incomplete postcranial skeleton originally described as Brachiosaurus atalaiensis; no diagnostic complete skull is known.","facts":["The lectotype represents one incomplete skeleton dominated by vertebral, girdle and limb material.","Long forelimbs support a high-shouldered brachiosaurid body plan.","Mass, total length and exact neck posture remain model-dependent."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Sobral Member of the Lourinha Formation is late Kimmeridgian to early Tithonian.","residualUncertainty":["The skull, much of the neck and tail are unknown.","Some referred Portuguese brachiosaurid material may not belong to the lectotype species."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/S1631-0683(03)00003-4","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2016.1247447","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lusotitan"]},"lycorhinus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Hettangian-Sinemurian, about 201-190 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Heterodontosauridae","Heterodontosaurinae"],"diet":null,"description":"Lycorhinus angustidens was a small bipedal heterodontosaurid from the upper Elliot Formation of South Africa. The name is anchored by a dentary preserved largely as an impression; a partial skull and other jaws have been referred, but some referrals and the synonymy of Lanasaurus remain debated.","facts":["The wiki Late Triassic age is incorrect; the upper Elliot Formation record is Early Jurassic.","The type is a jaw, not a skeleton.","Heterodont teeth support plant processing with possible opportunistic feeding, but omnivory is not directly demonstrated."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"Corrected from Late Triassic to Early Jurassic based on the upper Elliot Formation provenance.","residualUncertainty":["Referral of the partial skull and Lanasaurus material remains disputed.","The type dentary is incomplete and mostly an impression.","Specific diet breadth is unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.3897/zookeys.226.2840","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.1970.tb00739.x","https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Lycorhinus"]},"magyarosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 71-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria"],"description":"Magyarosaurus dacus was a small-bodied titanosaur from the Hateg Basin of Romania. It is known from abundant disarticulated bones belonging to multiple individuals, not a single complete skeleton.","facts":["Bone histology indicates that small individuals were mature, supporting island dwarfism rather than juvenility.","The skull is poorly known and full-body reconstructions are composites.","Other species historically assigned to Magyarosaurus are dubious or belong elsewhere."]},"ageReviewNote":"The core material is Maastrichtian Densus-Ciula Formation; older broad referrals are excluded.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level assignment of many isolated bones is difficult.","Precise adult size and phylogenetic position vary by specimen sample."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1000781107","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/magyarosaurus.html"]},"maiasaura":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 77-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Brachylophosaurini"],"description":"Maiasaura peeblesorum was a hadrosaurid from the Two Medicine Formation of Montana, documented by nesting grounds, eggs, juveniles, adults and large bonebeds.","facts":["Nest colonies and very young individuals in nests show repeated nesting-site use and extended nest occupancy.","The evidence is consistent with parental care but does not specify which sex cared for young or every detail of provisioning.","Large growth-series samples document rapid growth and population structure."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Two Medicine Maiasaura record is late Campanian, approximately 76.7-75 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Details of parental provisioning and social structure exceed the direct evidence.","Bonebeds may combine mortality events."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/282296a0","https://doi.org/10.1017/pab.2015.19","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.4734","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/maiasaura.html"]},"majungasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauridae","Majungasaurinae"],"description":"Majungasaurus crenatissimus was an abelisaurid from the Maevarano Formation of Madagascar, represented by numerous skulls and postcranial remains from multiple individuals.","facts":["Its short deep skull and reduced forelimbs are directly documented by a strong sample.","Majungasaurus tooth marks on Majungasaurus bones provide evidence of cannibalistic feeding, though not necessarily active predation.","Growth studies indicate slow, prolonged growth relative to many large theropods."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Maevarano Formation sample is late Maastrichtian.","residualUncertainty":["Cannibal tooth marks do not distinguish scavenging from predation.","Some composite skeletal proportions unite individuals."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228681379_Majungasaurus_crenatissimus_Theropoda_Abelisauridae_from_the_Late_Cretaceous_of_Madagascar","https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01532"]},"malawisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian, about 121-113 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Lithostrotia"],"description":"Malawisaurus dixeyi was an early lithostrotian titanosaur from the Dinosaur Beds of Malawi. Multiple individuals provide cranial and postcranial material, but the skeleton is reconstructed as a composite.","facts":["Cranial remains include braincase and jaw elements, unusual completeness for an early titanosaur.","Associated osteoderms have been reported, although their arrangement is unknown.","Its placement close to the base of Lithostrotia makes it important for titanosaur evolution."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Dinosaur Beds are treated as Aptian; a point range narrower than formation dating is avoided.","residualUncertainty":["Bones derive from several individuals and quarry associations are incomplete.","Precise placement within early Lithostrotia varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.palass.org/publications/palaeontology-journal/archive/36/3/article_pp523-534","https://palaeo-electronica.org/2005_1/gomani27/main.htm","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/malawisaurus.html"]},"mamenchisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"about 168-157 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda","Mamenchisauridae"],"description":"Mamenchisaurus is a historically broad genus of long-necked Chinese eusauropods. The type species M. constructus is based on a partial skeleton from the Sichuan Basin; other named species differ greatly in completeness and may not form a natural genus.","facts":["Several species preserve substantial skeletons, but no single completeness claim applies to the whole genus.","The extraordinary neck length is directly supported in some species; the largest whole-body estimates depend on scaling incomplete individuals.","A 2023 analysis recovered named Mamenchisaurus species in a non-monophyletic series, requiring genus-level revision."]},"ageReviewNote":"The range is restricted to well-supported Middle-Late Jurassic Chinese species; Early Cretaceous and non-Chinese referrals are not used to extend it.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level assignments and the limits of the genus remain unsettled.","Maximum size is poorly constrained for the most fragmentary species."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2023.2171818","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/mamenchisaurus.html","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071172"]},"mapusaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-early Turonian, about 97-93 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Carcharodontosauridae","Giganotosaurini"],"description":"Mapusaurus roseae was a giant carcharodontosaurid from the Huincul Formation of Argentina, known from a monospecific bonebed containing disarticulated remains of at least seven individuals of different sizes.","facts":["The bonebed collectively preserves much of the skeleton but no complete individual.","Its close relationship to Giganotosaurus is well supported.","The aggregation is evidence that multiple individuals died together; cooperative pack hunting is not demonstrated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Huincul Formation bonebed is Cenomanian-early Turonian, close to 96-94 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The cause and behavioural meaning of the multi-individual accumulation are unresolved.","Maximum size relies on fragmentary large individuals."],"sourceUrls":["https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/sites/default/files/articles/pdf/g2006n1a4.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/mapusaurus.html"]},"marshosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, about 157-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae"],"description":"Marshosaurus bicentesimus was a medium-sized theropod from the Morrison Formation, founded on a partial left ilium. Referred cranial and postcranial bones are fragmentary and not all are securely associated.","facts":["The type is far less complete than many popular reconstructions imply.","It is a tetanuran, but placement as a megalosauroid, piatnitzkysaurid or other basal branch varies.","Carnivory and bipedality are secure at broad theropod level; precise size is uncertain."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Morrison occurrences are Late Jurassic; the 154-142 Ma range extends too young into the Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype is limited and referrals need renewed comparison.","Fine phylogenetic placement is unstable.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/marshosaurus.html"]},"masiakasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauroidea","Noasauridae"],"description":"Masiakasaurus knopfleri was a small noasaurid theropod from the Maevarano Formation of Madagascar, known from disarticulated material collectively representing much of the skeleton but not one complete individual.","facts":["Combined specimens preserve roughly two-thirds of the skeleton; the 2002 sample described about 40%.","Its front teeth project forward and differ from the rear dentition.","Diet was carnivorous, but proposed specialisations for small prey or fish remain functional hypotheses."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Maevarano Formation sample is late Maastrichtian, not a Campanian-Maastrichtian range.","residualUncertainty":["The association of isolated bones and exact prey preference remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2002)022%5B0510:TOOMKA%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/masiakasaurus.html"]},"massospondylus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Hettangian-Pliensbachian, about 201-183 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massospondylidae"],"description":"Massospondylus carinatus was a common Early Jurassic sauropodomorph of southern Africa, known from many partial and articulated skeletons, skulls, embryos and juvenile material.","facts":["The original name-bearing material was destroyed; a well-preserved neotype anchors modern usage.","Embryos and nesting-site material document early growth, but direct prolonged parental care is not established.","Adults were predominantly bipedal in current biomechanical interpretations; young animals had different proportions."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure upper Elliot and Clarens material spans much of the Early Jurassic, not the former 208-204 Ma interval.","residualUncertainty":["Several named southern African species have complex synonymy.","Growth and gait estimates remain model-dependent."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.4224","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1114942","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/massospondylus.html","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.13918"]},"maxakalisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"probably Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 84-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria"],"description":"Maxakalisaurus topai was a titanosaur from the Adamantina Formation of Brazil. The holotype is a disarticulated partial skeleton with limited cranial material and osteoderms; additional jaw and tooth material has been referred from the type locality.","facts":["The type includes vertebrae, limb and girdle bones, skull fragments and osteoderms.","A referred dentary and teeth expanded knowledge of its feeding anatomy.","Precise placement within derived Titanosauria varies among analyses."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Adamantina Formation is not uniformly dated to a single 80 Ma point; a broad Late Cretaceous interval is more defensible.","residualUncertainty":["The formation age and association of the referred jaw remain imperfectly constrained.","The original National Museum material was affected by the 2018 fire and its present condition requires collection-level confirmation."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.2054","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/maxakalisaurus.html"]},"megalosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"middle Bathonian, about 168-166 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Megalosauridae"],"description":"Megalosaurus bucklandii was a large megalosaurid from the Taynton Limestone Formation of Oxfordshire. Modern revision restricts the genus to the type dentary and a limited set of comparable local bones, not the worldwide wastebasket once called Megalosaurus.","facts":["The lectotype is a partial right dentary with teeth.","Some cranial, vertebral, pelvic and limb bones from the same regional unit are referred, but no complete skeleton exists.","Most historical species named Megalosaurus have been removed or are dubious."]},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed age follows the middle Bathonian Taynton Limestone and does not import unrelated Megalosaurus referrals.","residualUncertainty":["Association of referred bones with the dentary-bearing taxon is not direct.","Maximum adult size is not constrained by a complete individual."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00569.x","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2008.00751.x"]},"melanorosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Norian, about 227-208 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropodiformes"],"description":"Melanorosaurus readi is a large non-sauropodan sauropodomorph from the lower Elliot Formation of South Africa. Its name-bearing series is incomplete and historically confused with other Elliot sauropodomorph material.","facts":["A modern redescription designated a lectotype from the original syntype material.","The well-known skull once assigned to Melanorosaurus was not part of the type and should not be used uncritically in reconstructions.","Its robust limbs support habitual terrestrial quadrupedal capacity, while exact gait remains biomechanically debated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type material is from the lower Elliot Formation and is treated as Norian; older generic ranges assembled from referrals are not accepted.","residualUncertainty":["The referral of several historical specimens remains unresolved.","Its exact position close to Sauropoda varies among matrices."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2024.2337802","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.1639"]},"metriacanthosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, about 163-157 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Metriacanthosauridae"],"description":"Metriacanthosaurus parkeri was a medium-large theropod from the Oxford Clay Formation of England, known from one incomplete postcranial skeleton without a skull.","facts":["The holotype includes vertebrae, pelvic and hindlimb elements.","Its tall neural spines inspired the name but do not establish a skin sail.","Placement in Metriacanthosauridae is supported in major tetanuran analyses, while anatomy and exact size remain poorly constrained."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Oxford Clay type horizon is Oxfordian; the age is not extended outside that unit.","residualUncertainty":["No skull is securely known.","Only one incomplete individual supports the genus."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/metriacanthosaurus.html"]},"microceratus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"probably Campanian, about 84-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia"],"description":"Microceratus gobiensis is the replacement name for the preoccupied Microceratops gobiensis. Its Mongolian type material is fragmentary and historically mixed with fossils later named Graciliceratops.","facts":["The name change solved a nomenclatural homonym but did not improve the fossil diagnosis.","Common skeletal reconstructions often incorporate material no longer referred to Microceratus.","A small bipedal neoceratopsian identity is plausible, but detailed anatomy and precise size are weakly supported."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"replacement name for fragmentary Microceratops gobiensis; validity uncertain"},"ageReviewNote":"Only a broad Campanian age can be defended for the relevant Gobi material; the former 86-66 Ma range is excessive.","residualUncertainty":["The type needs comprehensive modern redescription.","Diagnostic validity and assignment of referred material remain uncertain.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1666/07-069.1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/microceratus.html"]},"micropachycephalosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 84-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Cerapoda incertae sedis"],"description":"Micropachycephalosaurus hongtuyanensis is a very fragmentary small ornithischian from the Wangshi Group of Shandong, China. Redescription found it diagnosable but found no character demonstrating that it was a pachycephalosaur.","facts":["The holotype consists principally of incomplete vertebral, pelvic and hindlimb material.","Purported skull-roof bones could not be located for the modern revision.","The name does not establish a tiny dome-headed dinosaur; its position within Cerapoda is unresolved."]},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed age follows the Campanian assignment of the Wangshi material; a narrower numerical range is not justified.","residualUncertainty":["Its relationships cannot be resolved from the surviving material.","Some originally mentioned elements are missing."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2008.03.002","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/micropachycephalosaurus.html"]},"microraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian, about 120 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae","Microraptorinae"],"description":"Microraptor was a small feathered dromaeosaurid from the Jiufotang Formation of Liaoning, known from hundreds of articulated specimens, especially M. gui; species limits within the genus remain debated.","facts":["Long flight feathers on arms and legs form a four-winged aerodynamic configuration.","Aerodynamic studies support gliding or some aerial capacity, while launch and powered-flight performance remain debated.","Gut contents document a varied carnivorous diet including birds, mammals, lizards and fish in different specimens."]},"ageReviewNote":"The main Jiufotang sample is Aptian, close to 120 Ma; species and localities should not be merged into a broader generic range without review.","residualUncertainty":["Species synonymy within Microraptor is unresolved.","Powered flight versus gliding remains debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01342","https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1117727108","https://doi.org/10.1038/35047056","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0009223","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1213780"]},"minmi":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"lower Aptian, approximately 121-118 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria"],"description":"Minmi paravertebra was an Australian ankylosaur based on a partial skeleton from the Bungil Formation of Queensland. The much more complete skeleton formerly called Minmi sp. was renamed Kunbarrasaurus and must not supply Minmi anatomy.","facts":["The holotype includes vertebrae, ribs and limb and armour elements but no well-preserved skull.","Paravertebral ossifications are prominent in the type.","Upper Albian Australian ankylosaur material once discussed under Minmi belongs to Kunbarrasaurus or other ankylosaurs and must not extend the range or anatomy of Minmi paravertebra."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Minmi paravertebra holotype is from the lower Aptian Bungil Formation. Upper Albian Toolebuc and Allaru material belongs to Kunbarrasaurus or other ankylosaurs, not securely to Minmi.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype needs renewed detailed description.","Many popular traits belong to Kunbarrasaurus, not Minmi.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2022.803505","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.1475","https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/48741913"]},"monolophosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Callovian, about 166-163 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae"],"description":"Monolophosaurus jiangi was a medium-sized basal tetanuran from the Shishugou Formation of Xinjiang, represented by one unusually complete partial skeleton with a nearly complete crested skull.","facts":["The holotype preserves a high proportion of the skull and postcranium but is not a complete skeleton.","The midline cranial crest is directly preserved; its display function is plausible but untested.","Analyses consistently place it near the base of Tetanurae rather than within Allosauroidea or Megalosauroidea."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type horizon in the Shishugou Formation is Callovian; the very broad NHM interval is replaced with formation-level dating.","residualUncertainty":["Only one individual is securely known.","Soft-tissue appearance and crest function are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00563.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/monolophosaurus.html"]},"mononykus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Alvarezsauria","Alvarezsauridae","Parvicursorinae"],"description":"Mononykus olecranus was a small alvarezsaurid from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia, known from an incomplete skeleton lacking a skull.","facts":["Its very short, powerful forelimb bears one dominant functional digit.","The specialised arm is directly preserved, but proposed use in opening insect nests remains a functional hypothesis.","The skull, diet and external covering are inferred mainly from other alvarezsaurs."]},"ageReviewNote":"The holotype is from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation; older intervals should not be added from relatives.","residualUncertainty":["No skull is known for the holotype.","Forelimb function and diet are not directly demonstrated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/362623a0","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/mononykus.html"]},"mussaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Sinemurian, about 193 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massopoda"],"description":"Mussaurus patagonicus was a sauropodomorph from the Laguna Colorada Formation of Patagonia, known from eggs, hatchlings, juveniles and adults. The name was coined from tiny juveniles, but adults were much larger.","facts":["A growth series documents major changes from quadrupedal hatchlings to predominantly bipedal adults.","Egg clutches and age-segregated groups provide strong evidence of colonial nesting and social aggregation.","Herbivory is inferred from sauropodomorph anatomy; exact plant selection is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Radiometric and stratigraphic work places the nesting locality in the Early Jurassic, not the Late Triassic.","residualUncertainty":["The precise locomotor transition through growth remains model-dependent.","Social evidence represents particular assemblages, not every population."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-99176-1","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2013.769444","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/mussaurus.html"]},"muttaburrasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Albian-Cenomanian, about 105-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Rhabdodontomorpha"],"description":"Muttaburrasaurus langdoni was a large Australian ornithopod known from two partial skeletons and additional bones, including two skulls. Recent analyses place it among early-diverging iguanodontians, often close to Rhabdodontomorpha.","facts":["The holotype and second skull differ in ways that may reflect variation, deformation or taxonomy.","The enlarged nasal region is preserved, but a resonating or display function is speculative.","Herbivory is secure; a special omnivorous diet proposed from tooth form is not established."]},"ageReviewNote":"Material comes from upper Albian to Cenomanian units; the old 112-99 Ma span is too broad.","residualUncertainty":["The two principal skulls need renewed comparative study.","Fine phylogenetic position and nasal function remain unsettled."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/48741695","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2020.1793979"]},"nanshiungosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 72-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Therizinosauria"],"description":"Nanshiungosaurus brevispinus was a derived therizinosaur from the Nanxiong Basin of southern China, based on a skull-less partial axial and pelvic skeleton.","facts":["The type preserves vertebrae and pelvis but little basis for a precise whole-body reconstruction.","Nanshiungosaurus bohlini from Gansu is poorly comparable and should not automatically extend the type genus.","Plant-eating or omnivorous habits are inferred from therizinosaur anatomy; no direct gut contents are known."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type species is tied to Maastrichtian deposits of the Nanxiong Basin; the old 84-71 Ma range is too broad.","residualUncertainty":["The type is incomplete and has not received a comprehensive modern redescription.","The second named species may not belong to Nanshiungosaurus.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2010.488045","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/nanshiungosaurus.html"]},"nedoceratops":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 67-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae"],"description":"Nedoceratops hatcheri is founded on a single chasmosaurine skull from the Lance Formation of Wyoming. Its lack of a nasal horn and small frill openings have produced an unresolved debate over whether it is distinct or an unusual Triceratops.","facts":["No postcranial skeleton is securely associated with the type.","Farke found diagnostic differences supporting validity.","Scannella and Horner interpreted the skull as transitional morphology within Triceratops ontogeny; neither view should be hidden."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"disputed: valid genus in some studies, unusual Triceratops in others"},"ageReviewNote":"The Lance Formation specimen is latest Maastrichtian, immediately before the end-Cretaceous extinction.","residualUncertainty":["Its generic validity remains actively disputed.","The only skull may include individual, pathological or ontogenetic variation.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0016196","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0028705"]},"nemegtosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria"],"description":"Nemegtosaurus mongoliensis is a titanosaur from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia, named from a skull and lower jaw. Sparse postcranial remains have been proposed as referrals, but no complete Nemegtosaurus skeleton is known.","facts":["The type is principally cranial and bears narrow-crowned replacement teeth.","It may represent the same animal as the skull-less titanosaur Opisthocoelicaudia, but non-overlapping type material prevents a secure synonymy.","A possible additional skull-associated specimen supports titanosaur affinity but does not settle the synonymy."]},"ageReviewNote":"Both the type locality and rediscovered localities place the genus in the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Whether Nemegtosaurus and Opisthocoelicaudia are synonyms remains unresolved.","Postcranial anatomy and adult size are weakly constrained."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2017.10.035","https://www.app.pan.pl/article/item/app005962019.html","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/nemegtosaurus.html"]},"neovenator":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 130-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Carcharodontosauria"],"description":"Neovenator salerii was a large allosauroid theropod from the Wessex Formation of the Isle of Wight, known from one relatively complete partial skeleton with skull and postcranial material.","facts":["The holotype and associated discoveries preserve substantial cranial, vertebral, girdle and limb anatomy.","Its placement as a basal carcharodontosaurian is common, while the content of Neovenatoridae varies among analyses.","Pathologies are present in the type, but stories about their causes or survival are inferential."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Wessex Formation type horizon is Barremian, approximately 130-125 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The limits and membership of Neovenatoridae remain debated.","Maximum body size is based chiefly on one individual."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/25761900.2008.12452864","https://doi.org/10.1007/s00114-009-0614-x"]},"neuquensaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 83-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Saltasauridae","Saltasaurinae"],"description":"Neuquensaurus australis was a small-bodied saltasaurine titanosaur from the Neuquen Basin of Argentina. It is represented by abundant, mostly disarticulated bones from multiple individuals, not by one complete skeleton.","facts":["The available material documents much of the postcranial skeleton and includes osteoderms.","Historical species and referrals overlap with the complicated genus Saltasaurus and require specimen-level care.","Its relatively small adult dimensions are supported by mature bone histology, although exact mass estimates vary."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure material spans Upper Cretaceous units of the Neuquen Basin; a Maastrichtian-only range is too narrow.","residualUncertainty":["Association of older collections to species and individuals is imperfect.","Exact species boundaries within the historical material remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2009.0099","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2015.1079630","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/neuquensaurus.html"]},"nigersaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 115-105 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Rebbachisauridae"],"description":"Nigersaurus taqueti was a rebbachisaurid sauropod from the Elrhaz Formation of Niger, represented by multiple partial skeletons and unusually complete, delicate skull material.","facts":["Its broad muzzle carried dental batteries with hundreds of replacement teeth.","The skull and neck support low browsing, but claims that it held its head permanently downward overstate biomechanical inference.","Several individuals collectively document much of the skeleton; no single complete skeleton is known."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Elrhaz Formation is Aptian-Albian; the age remains formation-level rather than a precise 115-105 Ma lifespan.","residualUncertainty":["Habitual head posture and exact vegetation preference remain debated.","Skeletons are composite across individuals."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0001230","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.286.5443.1342"]},"nipponosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Santonian, about 86-84 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae"],"description":"Nipponosaurus sachalinensis was a lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from southern Sakhalin, Russia, represented by one roughly 60%-complete immature skeleton.","facts":["The holotype includes parts of the skull, vertebral column and limbs recovered in two excavations.","Histological and skeletal features show it was not fully grown.","Modern reanalysis supports its validity, but its exact position within Lambeosaurinae is sensitive to ontogeny."]},"ageReviewNote":"The marine Yezo Group horizon is Santonian; the specimen was found on Sakhalin when southern Sakhalin was under Japanese administration.","residualUncertainty":["Only one immature individual is known.","Adult crest form and mature size are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doc.rero.ch/record/15180/files/PAL_E2456.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2017.1317766","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/nipponosaurus.html"]},"noasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 75-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauroidea","Noasauridae"],"description":"Noasaurus leali was a small noasaurid theropod from the Lecho Formation of Argentina, based on a fragmentary disarticulated skeleton including skull and limb elements.","facts":["The prominent curved ungual was once reconstructed as a raptorial toe claw but is now identified as a hand claw.","The holotype is too incomplete for a precise total length or body mass.","Carnivory is supported by the teeth and theropod anatomy; specialised fish-catching remains a recent hypothesis, not consensus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Lecho Formation is latest Cretaceous; the genus is not established across the full 84-66 Ma interval.","residualUncertainty":["Feeding specialisation is disputed.","Many aspects of the skeleton are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5479/si.00810266.95.1","https://doi.org/10.1007/s12542-009-0044-2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/noasaurus.html"]},"nodosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae"],"description":"Nodosaurus textilis is an early named nodosaurid ankylosaur from the Frontier Formation of Wyoming, based on a partial postcranial skeleton with armour but no skull.","facts":["The holotype preserves vertebrae, girdle and limb elements and dermal armour.","No skull or tail club is known; head shape and much of the armour arrangement are reconstructed from relatives.","It was a quadrupedal herbivore by nodosaurid anatomy, but precise size and feeding height are estimates."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Frontier Formation type horizon is Cenomanian; an Early Cretaceous 110-100 Ma range is incorrect.","residualUncertainty":["The old type needs modern redescription.","Its exact position within Nodosauridae is unstable.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/16045349","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/nodosaurus.html"]},"nomingia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Oviraptorosauria","Caenagnathidae","Elmisaurus"],"diet":null,"taxonomicStatus":"probable junior synonym of Elmisaurus rarus","acceptedName":false,"description":"Nomingia gobiensis was named for a partial oviraptorosaur skeleton from the Nemegt Formation, notable for fused terminal tail vertebrae. A 2021 revision found it most likely to be the same taxon as Elmisaurus rarus.","facts":["The holotype lacks a skull but preserves vertebral, pelvic and limb material.","Its short fused tail tip is real, though calling it identical to a modern bird pygostyle can overstate convergence.","This page should be retained as a historical synonym rather than counted as a separate accepted genus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The specimen is from the Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation, not merely a generic 72-68 Ma range.","residualUncertainty":["The synonymy is well argued but could be retested with additional overlapping material.","Tail soft tissues are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.app.pan.pl/article/item/app45-097.html","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0254564","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/nomingia.html"]},"nothronychus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Turonian, about 93-90 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Therizinosauria","Therizinosauridae"],"description":"Nothronychus was a derived therizinosaur from the Turonian of the western United States, represented by two species known from partial postcranial skeletons; N. graffami also preserves more complete axial and girdle anatomy.","facts":["Large manual claws, a broad pelvis and leaf-shaped teeth support a specialised herbivorous or omnivorous theropod body plan.","No complete skull is known.","Feathers are likely by phylogenetic bracketing but are not directly preserved with the named skeletons."]},"ageReviewNote":"Both named species are Turonian, around 93-90 Ma; the genus is not limited to a single 91 Ma point.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level differences and exact diet remain open.","Cranial anatomy and integument are incomplete."],"sourceUrls":["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4465624/","https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2009.1029"]},"nqwebasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"probably Berriasian-Valanginian, about 145-133 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria"],"diet":null,"description":"Nqwebasaurus thwazi was a small early ornithomimosaur from the Kirkwood Formation of South Africa, known from one incomplete but associated skeleton.","facts":["The holotype includes skull fragments, vertebrae, forelimb and hindlimb elements.","Further preparation revealed anatomy supporting placement near the base of Ornithomimosauria.","Small stones in the abdominal region may be gastroliths, but they do not by themselves demonstrate a particular diet."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Kirkwood type horizon is Early Cretaceous; the former Mid Jurassic label and 159-132 Ma range are erroneous.","residualUncertainty":["Only one individual is known.","The age of parts of the Kirkwood succession remains imprecise.","Diet is uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jafrearsci.2012.05.005","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/nqwebasaurus.html"]},"omeisaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bajocian-Callovian, about 170-164 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda","Mamenchisauridae"],"description":"Omeisaurus is a genus of Chinese eusauropods centred on the Middle Jurassic Shaximiao succession. Several species and many skeletons have been assigned to it, but genus limits and species referrals require modern revision.","facts":["Material attributed to different species collectively covers much of the skeleton, including skulls in some species.","The tail-club often shown in reconstructions is based on referred material whose species association is uncertain.","Long neck and quadrupedal herbivory are well supported broadly, but measurements must remain species-specific."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed range follows Middle Jurassic type and core species; later referrals are not used to extend the genus automatically.","residualUncertainty":["The genus is probably oversplit or non-monophyletic.","Several historical specimens lack rigorous species-level reassessment.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2020.1743286","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/omeisaurus.html"]},"opisthocoelicaudia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria"],"description":"Opisthocoelicaudia skarzynskii was a titanosaur from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia, represented by one well-preserved, largely articulated postcranial skeleton lacking the neck and skull.","facts":["The holotype preserves most of the trunk, limbs, pelvis and tail.","The missing head and neck prevent direct comparison with the skull-only Nemegtosaurus.","Proposed rearing or tripod posture is biomechanically debated and should not be presented as observed behaviour."]},"ageReviewNote":"The rediscovered type locality confirms a Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation origin.","residualUncertainty":["Possible synonymy with Nemegtosaurus cannot be tested using overlapping type anatomy.","The skull and neck remain unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://palaeontologia.pan.pl/Archive/1977-37_5-64_1-19.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2017.10.035","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/opisthocoelicaudia.html"]},"ornitholestes":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian, about 154-152 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria"],"description":"Ornitholestes hermanni was a small coelurosaurian theropod from the Morrison Formation, known primarily from one incomplete but articulated skeleton with a crushed skull.","facts":["The holotype preserves much of the skeleton but lacks portions and has a distorted skull.","A nasal horn once reconstructed from a displaced bone is not supported.","Carnivory and bipedality are secure; prey choice, plumage extent and exact coelurosaur branch are uncertain."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Bone Cabin Quarry holotype is Kimmeridgian Morrison Formation, near 154-152 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal skeleton is known.","Skull distortion complicates reconstruction and relationships."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/26891815","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2006.00585.x"]},"ornithomimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 77-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria","Ornithomimidae"],"diet":null,"description":"Ornithomimus was an ostrich-like ornithomimid from western North America. The well-known species O. edmontonicus is represented by multiple skeletons, including specimens preserving feather impressions; the type species O. velox is far more fragmentary.","facts":["Juvenile and adult specimens show down-like body feathers, with larger arm feathers in adults.","The edentulous beak is preserved, but herbivory, omnivory and filter-feeding proposals remain debated.","Genus-level ranges must distinguish O. edmontonicus from fragmentary O. velox and historical referrals."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure species records span late Campanian to early Maastrichtian formations; a range to 66 Ma relies on disputed referrals.","residualUncertainty":["Diet is unresolved.","Species content and latest occurrences require specimen-level review."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1225376","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2015.1034593"]},"orodromeus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 76-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Thescelosauridae"],"description":"Orodromeus makelai was a small thescelosaurid ornithopod from the Two Medicine Formation of Montana, known from skulls and partial skeletons of several growth stages.","facts":["The type and referred material establish a small bipedal herbivorous ornithopod.","Eggs and nests once assigned to Orodromeus are now attributed chiefly to the troodontid Troodon/Stenonychosaurus complex.","Burrowing is plausible for close relatives but is not directly demonstrated for Orodromeus by an in-burrow skeleton."]},"ageReviewNote":"Two Medicine occurrences are late Campanian, approximately 76-74 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Some juvenile and nesting associations were historically misidentified.","Its exact position within Thescelosauridae varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/039.029.0312","https://doi.org/10.1038/383103b0","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/orodromeus.html"]},"oryctodromeus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Thescelosauridae"],"description":"Oryctodromeus cubicularis was a small ornithopod from the Blackleaf and Wayan formations of Montana and Idaho. An adult and juveniles preserved within a burrow provide direct evidence of den use.","facts":["The original burrow contained an adult-sized individual and two juveniles.","Skeletal features are consistent with digging, but it was not as specialised as modern obligate burrowers.","Herbivory or omnivory is inferred from thescelosaurid anatomy; direct diet evidence is absent."]},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant formations are Cenomanian; the type locality is not simply a 99-94 Ma point.","residualUncertainty":["Whether the preserved group represents parental care is uncertain.","The extent of habitual burrowing across the species is unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2006.0443","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/oryctodromeus.html"]},"othnielia":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, about 157-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia"],"description":"Othnielia rex is a historical Morrison Formation name founded on a nondiagnostic isolated femur. It is a nomen dubium, and the type cannot be linked securely to the more complete small-bodied Morrison ornithischian skeletons once referred to Othnielia.","facts":["The holotype alone does not establish a complete body plan, precise size or a distinct valid genus.","Carpenter and Galton (2018) proposed combining Othnielia and several other names within Nanosaurus.","Barrett and Maidment (2025) found that the proposed Nanosaurus synonymy was unsupported because the inadequate type specimens cannot be compared diagnostically."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"nomen dubium based on a nondiagnostic isolated femur","acceptedName":false},"ageReviewNote":"The historical material is Late Jurassic Morrison Formation; the former range extended too young.","residualUncertainty":["The exact neornithischian affinity of the isolated type femur cannot be resolved.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.31711/giw.v5.pp167-207","https://doi.org/10.3374/014.066.0102","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/othnielia.html"]},"ouranosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 115-105 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Styracosterna"],"description":"Ouranosaurus nigeriensis was a large iguanodontian from the Elrhaz Formation of Niger, known from two substantial skeletons including skull material.","facts":["Tall neural spines along the trunk and tail are directly preserved.","Whether they supported a sail, fatty hump or another soft-tissue profile is unresolved.","It had a dental battery and broad herbivorous feeding anatomy; exact diet and habitual gait are not directly observed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Elrhaz Formation is broadly Aptian-Albian; the single 112 Ma value is illustrative rather than a direct date.","residualUncertainty":["Soft tissue over the neural spines is unknown.","Its exact position among styracosternans varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/sites/default/files/articles/pdf/memoiresMNHN-Zoologie-1976-1.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1002/spp2.70034"]},"oviraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 75-71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Oviraptorosauria","Oviraptoridae"],"diet":null,"description":"Oviraptor philoceratops was an oviraptorid from the Djadokhta Formation of Mongolia, known from one incomplete skeleton with a crushed skull found beside a nest. Many complete museum reconstructions actually represent Citipati.","facts":["The eggs near the type are oviraptorosaur eggs, overturning the original idea that it was stealing Protoceratops eggs.","The holotype is less complete and has a lower crest than the animal often illustrated as Oviraptor.","Brooding is strongly evidenced across oviraptorids, but exact diet remains unresolved."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Djadokhta type horizon is Campanian, approximately 75-71 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The crushed type skull leaves crest reconstruction uncertain.","Diet is not settled.","Much familiar anatomy belongs to Citipati."],"sourceUrls":["https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/5dd7157d-f9ea-4d37-89d7-bd7a6c049de6","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.266.5186.779","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/oviraptor.html"]},"pachycephalosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 69-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae"],"description":"Pachycephalosaurus wyomingensis was a large dome-headed pachycephalosaurid from latest Cretaceous western North America, known mainly from skull roofs and incomplete skulls; postcranial remains are scarce.","facts":["Histology and morphology support interpreting Dracorex as a juvenile and Stygimoloch as a younger stage or close taxon, though details remain debated.","The thick dome is directly preserved, but combat style is inferred rather than observed.","Body size is less secure than skull anatomy because associated skeletons are rare."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure records are latest Maastrichtian Hell Creek, Lance and equivalent units.","residualUncertainty":["The status of Stygimoloch and extent of synonymy remain discussed.","Head-striking mechanics and diet are unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0007626","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2016.1078343","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pachycephalosaurus.html"]},"pachyrhinosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 73-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae","Pachyrhinosaurini"],"description":"Pachyrhinosaurus was a centrosaurine ceratopsid represented by three named North American species and large bonebeds. The type species P. canadensis was named from the St. Mary River Formation of Alberta, with referred material from the Horseshoe Canyon Formation, and bears a thick nasal boss rather than a conventional nasal horn.","facts":["Bonebeds contain many disarticulated individuals and growth stages, not one herd frozen in behaviour.","Boss and frill ornament changed markedly during growth.","Migration and horn-covering shape are plausible but not directly demonstrated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The genus-level range spans late Campanian P. lakustai through Maastrichtian P. canadensis and P. perotorum; species ages must not be merged.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries and some bonebed referrals remain under study.","Keratinous boss covering and migration are uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.4095/105057","https://doi.org/10.1139/9780660198194","https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2011.0033","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pachyrhinosaurus.html"]},"panoplosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 76-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae","Panoplosaurini"],"description":"Panoplosaurus mirus was a nodosaurid ankylosaur from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. The holotype preserves a complete skull, partial postcranium and armour; additional specimens add postcranial information.","facts":["CT redescription supports separation from the related Edmontonia.","It carried extensive osteoderms but no ankylosaurid-style tail club is known.","Quadrupedal herbivory is secure; exact armour layout and body mass remain reconstructed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Dinosaur Park Formation type and secure referrals are late Campanian.","residualUncertainty":["Some cranial ornament varies among individuals for unknown reasons.","Not all historical panoplosaurin material is securely referable."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2026.2636589","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/panoplosaurus.html"]},"pantydraco":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Rhaetian, about 205.7-201.4 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha"],"description":"Pantydraco caducus is a small basal sauropodomorph from fissure deposits at Pant-y-ffynnon, Wales, based mainly on an immature partial skeleton formerly assigned to Thecodontosaurus.","facts":["The material includes partial skull and postcranium but represents a young individual.","The genus was erected for differences from Thecodontosaurus antiquus.","Recent comparison has questioned whether those differences exceed juvenile variation, so validity remains open."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"possibly valid, but may represent juvenile Thecodontosaurus"},"ageReviewNote":"The Pant-y-ffynnon fissure fauna is Rhaetian and latest Triassic; 205.7-201.4 Ma gives the current stage bounds rather than a tightly dated fossil horizon.","residualUncertainty":["Its distinction from juvenile Thecodontosaurus is unresolved.","Adult anatomy and size are unknown.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1127/0077-7749/2007/0243-0119","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2020.1770774","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pantydraco.html"]},"paralititan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria"],"description":"Paralititan stromeri was a giant titanosaur from the Bahariya Formation of Egypt, based on an incomplete, partly associated skeleton dominated by girdle and limb material.","facts":["The holotype includes very large humeri plus vertebral and girdle elements, not a complete skeleton.","Gigantic size is secure qualitatively, but precise length and mass estimates span a wide range.","The fossils came from coastal mangrove-influenced sediments; this does not make the animal aquatic."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Bahariya type horizon is Cenomanian, approximately 100-94 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Much of the axial skeleton and skull is unknown.","Maximum mass is highly sensitive to scaling assumptions."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1060561","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/paralititan.html"]},"parasaurolophus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 77-73 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae","Parasaurolophini"],"description":"Parasaurolophus was a long-crested lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from western North America, represented by three named species with several skulls and partial skeletons.","facts":["The hollow crest contains nasal passages and changed shape during growth.","Acoustic resonance and visual display are well-supported functional hypotheses, but exact calls cannot be recovered.","Species differ in crest curvature and stratigraphic distribution and should not be merged into one skeleton."]},"ageReviewNote":"The genus spans late Campanian Dinosaur Park, Kaiparowits and Kirtland formations, approximately 77-73 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Exact crest sounds and soft-tissue structures remain unknown.","Species-level referral of fragmentary material is difficult."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.10669","https://doi.org/10.1017/S0094837300004036","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.182","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parasaurolophus_walkeri,_a_new_genus_and_species_of_trachodont_dinosaur.pdf"]},"parksosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Maastrichtian, about 72-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Thescelosauridae","Thescelosaurinae"],"description":"Parksosaurus warrenae was a small thescelosaurid from the Horseshoe Canyon Formation of Alberta, known chiefly from one partial skeleton with an incomplete skull.","facts":["The holotype preserves much of the postcranium and parts of the skull but is not complete.","It was a bipedal, low-browsing ornithopod; more specific diet claims are not directly evidenced.","Its close relationship to Thescelosaurus is supported, while fine thescelosaurid branching varies."]},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant Horseshoe Canyon strata are early Maastrichtian, younger than the former 76-74 Ma range.","residualUncertainty":["The skull is incomplete and only one principal skeleton is known.","Published size estimates are approximate."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2022.105369","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/parksosaurus.html"]},"patagosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Toarcian-Bajocian, about 179-170 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Patagosaurus fariasi was a basal eusauropod from the Canon Asfalto Formation of Patagonia. Its holotype is a partial postcranial skeleton, while additional disarticulated material represents several individuals.","facts":["Modern redescription confirmed diagnostic characters in the holotype.","The skull is poorly represented, so feeding details beyond broad sauropod herbivory are uncertain.","A reported juvenile assemblage and referred bones should not be merged into a single complete skeleton."]},"ageReviewNote":"Modern geochronology places relevant Canon Asfalto strata near the Early-Middle Jurassic transition, older than the former 164-159 Ma label.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred material may represent other sauropods.","The exact age of individual collecting horizons is not uniform."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.5252/geodiversitas2021v43a16","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/patagosaurus.html"]},"patagotitan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"latest Albian, about 102-101 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Lognkosauria"],"massKg":null,"description":"Patagotitan mayorum was a giant titanosaur from the Cerro Barcino Formation of Patagonia, represented by partial skeletons from at least six individuals accumulated at one locality.","facts":["The combined sample preserves much of the postcranial skeleton, but no single individual is complete and the skull is unknown.","It was among the largest well-sampled terrestrial vertebrates.","Published mass estimates vary substantially with scaling method and should be presented as ranges, not one exact value."]},"ageReviewNote":"Radiometric dating near 101.6 Ma places the quarry in the latest Albian, shortly before the Early-Late Cretaceous boundary.","residualUncertainty":["Skull and much of the neck are unknown.","Mass and total length estimates remain method-sensitive."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2017.1219","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/patagotitan.html"]},"pelecanimimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Barremian, about 126-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimosauria"],"description":"Pelecanimimus polyodon was a basal ornithomimosaur from the Las Hoyas Konservat-Lagerstatte of Spain, represented by one articulated front half of a skeleton with exceptional soft-tissue preservation.","facts":["The specimen preserves skull, neck, forelimbs and anterior trunk but lacks most of the hind body.","It had more than 200 small teeth, unlike later toothless ornithomimids.","Soft tissues around the throat and body outline are preserved, but a pelican-like pouch is not established."]},"ageReviewNote":"Las Hoyas is late Barremian, around 126-125 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is known.","Diet and the function of throat soft tissues remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/370363a0","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pelecanimimus.html"]},"pelorosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Valanginian, about 139-133 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Neosauropoda","Macronaria"],"description":"Pelorosaurus conybeari is a historically important but taxonomically difficult English sauropod name based on Early Cretaceous Wealden material. Its type concept involves a humerus and historically associated caudal vertebrae; many species once placed in Pelorosaurus do not belong there.","facts":["The name has been applied inconsistently since the nineteenth century.","Pelorosaurus becklesii was removed to the separate genus Haestasaurus in a modern revision.","A precise body plan or family-level placement for P. conybeari is not secure."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"historically valid name with disputed diagnosis and content"},"ageReviewNote":"The type material is from the Hastings Group and is older than a single 125 Ma estimate.","residualUncertainty":["The composition and diagnostic validity of P. conybeari remain disputed.","Historical referrals cannot be treated as one genus.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0125819","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pelorosaurus.html"]},"pentaceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 76-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae"],"description":"Pentaceratops sternbergii was a chasmosaurine ceratopsid from the Kirtland Formation of New Mexico, known from several skulls and partial skeletons with a deep frill and long brow horns.","facts":["The name refers to three true facial horns plus elongated cheek projections.","OMNH 10165, once reconstructed as a giant Pentaceratops, was proposed as Titanoceratops; that separation remains debated.","Exact frill soft tissues and horn use are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure P. sternbergii material is late Campanian Kirtland Formation, around 75.9-73.4 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The identity of OMNH 10165 remains disputed.","Some historical species and referrals have been reassigned."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022336000032200","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2010.12.007"]},"piatnitzkysaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Toarcian-Bajocian, about 179-170 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Piatnitzkysauridae"],"description":"Piatnitzkysaurus floresi was a medium-sized basal megalosauroid theropod from the Canon Asfalto Formation of Patagonia, represented by two incomplete skeletons and isolated material.","facts":["The type preserves skull, vertebral, girdle and limb elements but is not close to complete.","Braincase anatomy supports tetanuran affinity.","Carnivory and bipedal locomotion are secure; exact body mass is model-dependent."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Canon Asfalto occurrences are older than the former Late Jurassic label and lie near the Early-Middle Jurassic transition.","residualUncertainty":["Formation horizons span a substantial interval.","Piatnitzkysaurid relationships vary among theropod analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/e04-053","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/piatnitzkysaurus.html"]},"pinacosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 80-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Pinacosaurus grangeri was an ankylosaurid from Campanian deposits of Mongolia and northern China, represented by numerous skulls and skeletons, especially juvenile groups.","facts":["Exceptional juvenile assemblages preserve multiple associated individuals rather than one mass-complete skeleton.","Skulls show characteristic accessory openings near the nostrils, whose soft-tissue function remains debated.","A tail club is supported by ankylosaurid anatomy and referred caudal material, but armour layouts vary with preservation and growth."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure occurrences are Campanian Djadokhta-equivalent deposits; the range is not extended using undiagnostic ankylosaur material.","residualUncertainty":["The status of P. mephistocephalus and some referrals remains debated.","Nasal soft tissues are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://palaeontologia.pan.pl/Archive/1977-37_85-151_20-32.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0082(2003)395%3C0001:ANSOPG%3E2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pinacosaurus.html"]},"plateosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Norian-Rhaetian, about 214-204 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Plateosauridae"],"description":"Plateosaurus was a large non-sauropodan sauropodomorph from Late Triassic Europe, represented by many skeletons and bonebed assemblages. Species-level nomenclature, especially P. engelhardti versus P. trossingensis, has a complex history.","facts":["The Trossingen and Frick bonebeds preserve many individuals of different sizes.","Biomechanical work favours habitual bipedality for adults rather than elephant-like quadrupedal walking.","Size varied substantially among mature individuals, consistent with developmental plasticity."]},"ageReviewNote":"Well-dated occurrences span Norian-Rhaetian European strata; species-level horizons should be retained separately.","residualUncertainty":["Species nomenclature and assignment of some bonebeds remain contested.","Mass estimates vary strongly with specimen and method."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2009.0075","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1120125","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/plateosaurus.html"]},"podokesaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Hettangian-Sinemurian, about 201-193 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda","Coelophysoidea"],"description":"Podokesaurus holyokensis is based on one incomplete, partly articulated small theropod skeleton from Massachusetts that was destroyed by fire in 1917. Study is limited to photographs, published drawings and casts.","facts":["The original specimen came from the Portland Formation, not the Middle Jurassic.","Its anatomy is coelophysoid, but the lost material may not preserve a unique diagnosis.","Proposed synonymy with Coelophysis cannot be tested adequately without the original fossil."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"dubious or indeterminate coelophysoid; possible Coelophysis synonym","acceptedName":false},"ageReviewNote":"Portland Formation evidence places the specimen in the Early Jurassic.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype is lost and its validity cannot be retested directly.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/8714367","https://www.jsg.utexas.edu/rowe/files/047-Tykoski_Rowe-20041.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/podokesaurus.html"]},"poekilopleuron":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian, about 168-166 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea"],"description":"Poekilopleuron bucklandii was a large theropod from the Calcaire de Caen of Normandy. Its partial skeleton was destroyed during the Second World War; casts, illustrations and a few separated elements preserve the evidence.","facts":["The holotype included vertebrae, ribs, forelimbs and hindlimb parts but no complete skull.","Its megalosauroid affinity is likely, but exact placement and even continued diagnosability are debated.","Many later species once assigned to Poekilopleuron have been removed."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"historically valid but difficult to diagnose because the holotype was destroyed"},"ageReviewNote":"The Calcaire de Caen type unit is Bathonian, approximately 168-166 million years old.","residualUncertainty":["The original type is destroyed, limiting modern character testing.","Some surviving material and casts require a modern comprehensive audit.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-4983.00277","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/poekilopleuron.html"]},"polacanthus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 130-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae sensu lato"],"description":"Polacanthus foxii was an armoured dinosaur from the Wessex Formation of the Isle of Wight, based on an incomplete, disarticulated postcranial skeleton with extensive armour but no skull.","facts":["The holotype preserves pelvis, hindlimb, vertebral and osteoderm material including a pelvic shield.","Skull shape and much of the front-body armour are inferred from relatives.","Whether polacanthines form a distinct family or a basal nodosaurid grade remains debated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Wessex Formation type is Barremian, approximately 130-125 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The holotype lacks a skull and forequarters.","Polacanthine relationships remain unstable."],"sourceUrls":["https://palass.org/publications/palaeontology-journal/archive/30/3/article_pp557-580","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/polacanthus.html"]},"prenocephale":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae"],"description":"Prenocephale prenes was a dome-headed pachycephalosaurid from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia, known from a skull with partial postcranium and additional cranial material.","facts":["The rounded frontoparietal dome and surrounding nodes are directly preserved.","Homalocephale has been proposed as a juvenile growth stage, but this synonymy is not universally accepted.","Bipedality is secure; herbivory or omnivory and head-striking behaviour remain inferential."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is late Campanian Baruungoyot Formation; a range to 66 Ma is not supported by the type species.","residualUncertainty":["Ontogenetic synonymy with Homalocephale remains debated.","Diet and dome function are unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1671/0272-4634%282003%2923%5B181%3AROTDSL%5D2.0.CO%3B2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/prenocephale.html"]},"probactrosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 125-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Probactrosaurus gobiensis was a non-hadrosaurid hadrosauroid from the Dashuigou Formation of Inner Mongolia, known from multiple partial skeletons including skull and jaws.","facts":["It documents an intermediate-grade hadrosauroid, but it is not established as the direct ancestor of later hadrosaurids.","Probactrosaurus alashanicus is generally treated as a junior synonym, while P. mazongshanensis was removed to Gongpoquansaurus.","Quadrupedal and bipedal movement were anatomically possible; exact gait frequency is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Dashuigou Formation is Aptian-Albian; the broad original catalogue interval is retained only at formation resolution.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level historical referrals remain complex.","Formation dating is not a narrow numerical point."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1096-3642.2002.00027.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/probactrosaurus.html"]},"proceratosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian, about 168-166 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Proceratosauridae"],"length":null,"description":"Proceratosaurus bradleyi was a small early tyrannosauroid from the White Limestone Formation of England, known from one incomplete but well-preserved skull.","facts":["CT and redescription identified it as a proceratosaurid tyrannosauroid, not a close relative of Ceratosaurus.","A low crest rises from the nasal region, but its complete shape and function are unknown.","No secure postcranial skeleton exists, so body size and proportions are inferred from relatives."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type horizon is Bathonian Middle Jurassic.","residualUncertainty":["Only one partial skull is known.","Body size, forelimbs and covering are not directly known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00591.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/proceratosaurus.html"]},"procompsognathus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Norian, about 215-208 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda incertae sedis"],"description":"Procompsognathus triassicus is based on a small, incomplete skeleton from the Lowenstein Formation of Germany. The specimen is poorly preserved, and parts of the skull may belong to a non-dinosaurian archosaur.","facts":["The postcranium is broadly consistent with a small neotheropod or coelophysoid.","Cranial material was historically overinterpreted and may be chimaeric.","Precise diet, size and relationships cannot be established confidently."],"length":null,"taxonomicStatus":"validity and skeletal composition disputed"},"ageReviewNote":"The type is Norian Late Triassic; the 221-210 Ma range is broader than its horizon supports.","residualUncertainty":["The skull association and diagnostic validity need further revision.","Fine theropod placement is unresolved.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1992.10011473","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/procompsognathus.html"]},"prosaurolophus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 76-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Saurolophini"],"description":"Prosaurolophus maximus was a saurolophine hadrosaurid from the Dinosaur Park and Two Medicine formations, known from numerous articulated skulls and partial skeletons across a growth series.","facts":["Ten articulated skulls document substantial individual and ontogenetic variation.","Prosaurolophus blackfeetensis is best treated as a junior synonym of P. maximus in the modern quantitative revision.","The small nasal crest is bony; any associated inflatable soft-tissue display structure remains speculative."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure P. maximus material spans the late Campanian Dinosaur Park and Two Medicine formations.","residualUncertainty":["Soft-tissue structures associated with the crest are unknown.","Species-level range depends on accepting P. blackfeetensis synonymy."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/zoj.12009","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/prosaurolophus.html"]},"protarchaeopteryx":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian, about 125-120 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Oviraptorosauria"],"description":"Protarchaeopteryx robusta was an early oviraptorosaur from the Yixian Formation of Liaoning, represented by feathered partial skeletons.","facts":["Long symmetrical feathers occur on the arms and especially the tail.","Its feathers do not demonstrate powered flight; skeletal anatomy places it among non-avian oviraptorosaurs.","Teeth and jaw form do not establish a single precise diet; omnivory or herbivory remains inferred."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Yixian Formation specimens are Aptian Early Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["The number and association of referred specimens warrant continued review.","Feather colour and exact locomotor role are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/48723","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/protarchaeopteryx.html"]},"protoceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 75-71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Neoceratopsia","Protoceratopsidae"],"description":"Protoceratops andrewsi was a sheep-sized neoceratopsian from the Djadokhta Formation of Mongolia, known from abundant skulls and skeletons across growth stages.","facts":["The famous fighting specimen preserves direct interaction with Velociraptor.","Eggs historically assigned to Protoceratops were oviraptorosaur eggs; some genuine protoceratopsian nesting evidence is now known separately.","Large samples document growth, but strong sexual dimorphism in frill shape has not been demonstrated securely."]},"ageReviewNote":"The core P. andrewsi record is Campanian Djadokhta Formation; P. hellenikorhinus is a separate species from Bayan Mandahu.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries and purported sexual dimorphism continue to be tested.","Colour and social structure are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/427f573c-56ce-4dde-9a1d-6c4a3f66400c","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0126464","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/protoceratops.html"]},"protohadros":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 96-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea"],"length":null,"description":"Protohadros byrdi was a non-hadrosaurid hadrosauroid from the Woodbine Formation of Texas, known from a partial skull with limited postcranial material.","facts":["Its name does not mean it was the direct ancestor of Hadrosauridae.","The downturned jaw and dental battery support herbivory, but a specialised grazing ecology is not demonstrated.","Most of the body and exact adult size are reconstructed from related hadrosauroids."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Woodbine Formation type horizon is Cenomanian, approximately 96-94 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Postcranial anatomy is very limited.","Its exact position among non-hadrosaurid hadrosauroids varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1998.10011101","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/protohadros.html"]},"psittacosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian-Albian, about 126-101 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Psittacosauridae"],"description":"Psittacosaurus was a diverse Early Cretaceous ceratopsian genus from Asia, represented by hundreds of specimens and numerous named species. The type species P. mongoliensis is anatomically well known, but genus-wide claims must distinguish species.","facts":["Exceptional specimens preserve tail bristles, skin outlines, pigmentation patterns, a cloacal region and stomach contents.","Those soft-tissue observations belong to particular specimens and species, not automatically every Psittacosaurus.","Juveniles were more quadrupedal and adults predominantly bipedal in major growth studies."]},"ageReviewNote":"The genus spans multiple Barremian-Albian formations; individual species occupy narrower intervals.","residualUncertainty":["Several named species may be synonyms or based on juvenile differences.","Soft-tissue evidence is specimen-specific."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/270162979_Taxonomy_cranial_morphology_and_relationships_of_parrot-beaked_dinosaurs_Psittacosaurus","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2016.06.065","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2020.12.039","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0069265","https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms3079","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/psittacosaurus.html"]},"puertasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 76-70 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Lognkosauria"],"length":null,"description":"Puertasaurus reuili was a giant titanosaur from southern Patagonia, based on only four vertebrae: one cervical, one dorsal and two caudals.","facts":["The enormous dorsal vertebra demonstrates a very broad trunk region.","No skull or limb bones are known from the holotype.","Exact length and mass estimates are highly speculative because scaling four vertebrae requires uncertain proportions."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Cerro Fortaleza/Pari Aike unit has a broad Campanian-Maastrichtian age; the type cannot support a precise point date.","residualUncertainty":["Formation nomenclature and age remain debated.","Nearly the entire skeleton and all precise size metrics are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://revista.macn.gob.ar/ojs/index.php/RevMus/article/view/74","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/puertasaurus.html"]},"pyroraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-early Maastrichtian, about 72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Dromaeosauridae"],"description":"Pyroraptor olympius was a small dromaeosaurid from Provence, France, based on a handful of disarticulated bones including a curved second-toe claw, teeth and limb elements.","facts":["The French type material is extremely fragmentary and does not preserve a full skeleton.","Some Spanish teeth and bones have been referred, but their genus-level identity is uncertain.","Feathering is probable from dromaeosaurid bracketing; swimming ability and exact colour are unsupported."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality near the Campanian-Maastrichtian boundary supports a much narrower range than 84-71 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The diagnosis and exact dromaeosaurid position are limited by sparse material.","Spanish referrals need specimen-level review."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2000)020%5B0404:ANGODD%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/pyroraptor.html"]},"quaesitosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Nemegtosauridae"],"description":"Quaesitosaurus orientalis is a titanosaur from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia, based almost entirely on one incomplete skull.","facts":["The type skull has the elongate muzzle and slender teeth associated with nemegtosaurid titanosaurs.","Postcranial anatomy, total length and armour are unknown for the type.","Its distinction from Nemegtosaurus has been questioned but cannot be resolved confidently from current material."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The type comes from the late Campanian Baruungoyot Formation; it should not be assigned the age of the younger Nemegt Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Validity relative to Nemegtosaurus needs modern specimen-level reassessment.","Almost the entire body is unknown.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://palaeontologia.pan.pl/Archive/1983-25_1-2_89-113_14-15.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/quaesitosaurus.html"]},"rajasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauridae","Majungasaurinae"],"description":"Rajasaurus narmadensis was an abelisaurid theropod from the Lameta Formation of India, based on an associated partial skull and postcranial skeleton assembled from a locality-level collection.","facts":["The skull roof bears a low midline horn.","The material is incomplete but includes braincase, vertebral, pelvic and hindlimb elements.","Its close relationship to Majungasaurus supports Gondwanan biogeographic comparisons, while exact size remains approximate."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Lameta Formation type material is Maastrichtian and close to the end of the Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["Association of all locality bones and exact individual count require care.","Adult size is not directly constrained by a complete skeleton."],"sourceUrls":["https://hdl.handle.net/2027.42/48667","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/rajasaurus.html"]},"rebbachisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 100-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Rebbachisauridae"],"description":"Rebbachisaurus garasbae was a rebbachisaurid sauropod from the Kem Kem Group of Morocco, known from a prepared partial skeleton including vertebrae, scapula, humerus and ischium.","facts":["The giant neural spine belongs to a dorsal vertebra but does not by itself establish a tall skin sail.","Rebbachisaurus tessonei was removed to the genus Limaysaurus.","The skull and most of the neck and limbs are unknown; body mass estimates are moderate for a sauropod, not reliably gigantic."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is lower Upper Cretaceous Cenomanian, not Early Cretaceous.","residualUncertainty":["Some Kem Kem sauropod material cannot be referred securely.","Soft-tissue profile over the neural spines is unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2014.1000701","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/rebbachisaurus.html"]},"rhabdodon":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 84-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Rhabdodontomorpha","Rhabdodontidae"],"description":"Rhabdodon priscus was a robust rhabdodontid ornithopod from southern France. Its lectotype is a dentary; broader anatomy comes from numerous disarticulated specimens whose species assignments require locality-level care.","facts":["A 2025 reassessment diagnosed R. priscus using the type material rather than the old continent-wide wastebasket.","Romanian and Austrian material once called Rhabdodon belongs to Zalmoxes and Mochlodon.","R. septimanicus may be distinct, so French material should not all be merged automatically."]},"ageReviewNote":"The genus spans Campanian to lower Maastrichtian French units, not a narrow 76-70 Ma interval.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries within French material remain under revision.","Many postcranial referrals are not associated with diagnostic jaws."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-98083-z","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/rhabdodon.html"]},"rhoetosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bajocian-Bathonian, about 176-164 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Gravisauria"],"description":"Rhoetosaurus brownei was a basal sauropod from the Evergreen Formation of Queensland, known from a partial skeleton preserving many vertebrae, pelvis and much of one hind limb and foot but no skull.","facts":["Preparation and redescription support its distinctiveness despite missing cranial material.","The nearly complete hind foot provides unusually direct evidence for early sauropod pedal anatomy.","Quadrupedal support is secure; diet is inferred as herbivorous from sauropod identity rather than teeth or gut contents."]},"ageReviewNote":"Refined geological work supports a Middle Jurassic age but not a narrow point date.","residualUncertainty":["The skull and most of the neck and forelimb are unknown.","Its exact position among basal sauropods varies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2012.622324","https://doi.org/10.1002/jmor.20989","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gr.2019.05.008","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/rhoetosaurus.html"]},"rinchenia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Oviraptorosauria","Oviraptoridae"],"diet":"Herbivore or omnivore (uncertain)","description":"Rinchenia mongoliensis was an oviraptorid from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia, represented by a single partial skeleton with a crested skull, originally described as Oviraptor mongoliensis.","facts":["The combination was separated from Oviraptor because of cranial and postcranial differences.","The type preserves a substantial skeleton but not a broad population sample.","Diet cannot be narrowed beyond likely omnivory or herbivory from this specimen alone."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Baruungoyot Formation is late Campanian; the species should not be assigned the younger Nemegt age.","residualUncertainty":["Only one principal individual is known.","The crest soft tissue, diet and exact oviraptorid relationships are uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1525/california/9780520242098.003.0010","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/rinchenia.html"]},"riojasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Norian-Rhaetian, about 221-205 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massopoda","Riojasauridae"],"description":"Riojasaurus incertus was a large non-sauropodan sauropodomorph from the Los Colorados Formation of Argentina, known from several partial skeletons; cranial material is limited and not associated with every skeleton.","facts":["Robust limb bones show a heavy-bodied animal capable of quadrupedal support.","Whether adults were obligate quadrupeds or retained meaningful bipedal ability is biomechanically debated.","Herbivory is strongly inferred from sauropodomorph anatomy, while precise feeding height and body mass remain estimates."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Los Colorados Formation is Norian-Rhaetian; its exact numerical duration remains under refinement.","residualUncertainty":["Skull association and species-level variation need modern redescription.","Habitual gait is unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/52135118","https://palass.org/publications/special-papers-palaeontology/archive/77/article_pp9-55"]},"rugops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 96-94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Ceratosauria","Abelisauroidea","Abelisauridae"],"description":"Rugops primus was an abelisaurid theropod from the Echkar Formation of Niger, known from one partial skull and associated fragments rather than a skeleton.","facts":["The skull roof bears texturing and openings, but elaborate skin display structures are speculative.","Its discovery helped document mid-Cretaceous Gondwanan abelisaurid distribution.","Body size, forelimbs and most postcranial anatomy are inferred from relatives."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Echkar type horizon is Cenomanian, close to 95 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Only one partial skull is securely known.","Its exact abelisaurid position and adult size remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2004.2692","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/rugops.html"]},"saichania":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Saichania chulsanensis was an ankylosaurid from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia, based on a skull and partial associated skeleton with additional referred material.","facts":["The holotype preserves an excellent skull, neck armour, vertebrae, girdles and forelimbs but not a complete body.","Some mounted or referred specimens have been questioned or reassigned, so tail and hindquarter anatomy must be labelled by specimen."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is late Campanian Baruungoyot Formation; an isolated 80 Ma point is not justified.","residualUncertainty":["Referred skeletons and species boundaries with Tarchia require care."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1127/pala/294/2011/1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/saichania.html"]},"saltasaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-early Maastrichtian, about 75-69 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria","Saltasauridae","Saltasaurinae"],"description":"Saltasaurus loricatus was a small-bodied saltasaurine titanosaur from the Lecho Formation of Argentina, known from disarticulated remains of several individuals and associated osteoderms.","facts":["Much of the postcranial skeleton is known collectively, not as one complete individual.","Dermal armour is directly documented, but its complete arrangement over the body is unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Lecho Formation sample crosses late Campanian-early Maastrichtian estimates; a single 70 Ma date is too precise.","residualUncertainty":["The sample is disarticulated and the armour layout is incomplete."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284147917_Osteologia_de_Saltasaurus_loricatus_Sauropoda-Titanosauridae_del_Cretacico_Superior_del_noroeste_Argentino","https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2009.1101","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2014.12.012","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/saltasaurus.html"]},"sarcosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"early Sinemurian, about 199-196 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda"],"length":null,"description":"Sarcosaurus woodi was an early neotheropod from the Lias Group of England, founded on fragmentary pelvic and hindlimb material. A modern redescription upheld a diagnosis but could not resolve its exact branch.","facts":["The holotype is not a skeleton and preserves only limited anatomy.","The catalogue three-metre length is cleared because historical referrals do not securely constrain it."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type horizon is early Sinemurian, not a broad 202-195 Ma lifespan.","residualUncertainty":["Fine phylogenetic position and referrals remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlaa054","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sarcosaurus.html"]},"saurolophus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"early Maastrichtian, about 72-68 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Saurolophini"],"description":"Saurolophus comprises the North American S. osborni and Asian S. angustirostris, both known from skulls and skeletons. They share a solid backswept cranial crest but differ in anatomy and abundance.","facts":["Mongolian S. angustirostris is represented by many individuals, including skin impressions.","The crest was bony; proposed inflatable soft-tissue sacs remain unconfirmed."]},"ageReviewNote":"Species occur in the Horseshoe Canyon and Nemegt formations during the early Maastrichtian; species ranges should remain separate.","residualUncertainty":["Crest-associated soft tissue and exact vocal function are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0031295","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x"]},"sauropelta":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 115-105 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae"],"description":"Sauropelta edwardsorum was a nodosaurid from the Cloverly Formation of Wyoming and Montana, represented by a partial skeleton and additional specimens with extensive armour.","facts":["Large shoulder spines and rows of osteoderms are directly represented, but full armour placement is composite.","It lacked an ankylosaurid-style tail club."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Cloverly sample is Aptian-Albian and not a genus lifespan to 100 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Skull material and association of armour among individuals are incomplete."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/52128534","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sauropelta.html"]},"saurophaganax":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, about 154-145 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","incertae sedis"],"taxonomicStatus":"contested; the historical type assemblage is chimeric and the name was treated as a nomen dubium in a 2024 reassessment","acceptedName":false,"historicalProfile":true,"length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Saurophaganax maximus is retained here as a historical name, not as a securely established giant allosaurid. A 2024 specimen-level reassessment found that material formerly grouped under the name is chimeric: the name-bearing neural arch could not be confidently identified as theropod and may be sauropod, while diagnosable theropod bones were named Allosaurus anax.","facts":["The holotype OMNH 1123 is an isolated neural arch, not a complete or associated giant theropod skeleton.","Some bones formerly referred to Saurophaganax are more consistent with diplodocid sauropods.","The securely theropod material from the Kenton quarry was separated as Allosaurus anax in 2024.","Older size estimates for a giant Saurophaganax combine referred bones and should not be presented as measurements of the name-bearing specimen."]},"ageReviewNote":"The record is restricted to the Kenton Member of the Morrison Formation in Oklahoma. A broad formation-wide occurrence range does not establish the range of the disputed name.","residualUncertainty":["The dinosaurian identity of the holotype remains uncertain.","The new combination Allosaurus anax and the proposed treatment of the name Saurophaganax require testing in subsequent work."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.18435/vamp29404","https://journals.library.ualberta.ca/vamp/index.php/VAMP/article/view/29404"]},"saurornithoides":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 75-71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Troodontidae"],"diet":null,"description":"Saurornithoides mongoliensis was a troodontid from the Djadokhta Formation of Mongolia, known principally from a partial skull and skeleton.","facts":["Saurornithoides junior was transferred to the separate genus Zanabazar.","Large eyes and teeth support predatory or omnivorous habits, but neither a categorical diet nor nocturnality is established for this genus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Djadokhta type is Campanian; the 85-80 Ma range is too old.","residualUncertainty":["Only limited material anchors the type species."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/648.1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/saurornithoides.html"]},"scelidosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"late Sinemurian, about 195-193 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora"],"description":"Scelidosaurus harrisonii was an early armoured ornithischian from the Charmouth Mudstone Formation of Dorset, known from several skeletons including a nearly complete skull and postcranium.","facts":["Modern redescription places it near the base of Thyreophora rather than securely inside Ankylosauria.","Its osteoderms and spikes are directly preserved, although colour and keratin coverings are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Ammonite zonation places the Dorset material in the upper Sinemurian, approximately 195-193 Ma on the current time scale.","residualUncertainty":["Armour arrangement and exact position at the base of Thyreophora continue to be refined."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlz078","https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlaa061","https://archive.org/details/monographoffoss00owen","https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlz074"]},"scutellosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Sinemurian-Pliensbachian, about 199-183 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora"],"description":"Scutellosaurus lawleri was a small, lightly built early thyreophoran from the Kayenta Formation of Arizona, known from many partial skeletons and hundreds of small osteoderms.","facts":["Long hindlimbs and tail support habitual bipedality.","The exact distribution of its numerous small osteoderms is not preserved as a complete articulated armour covering."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Kayenta record is Early Jurassic and considerably younger/broader than 205-202 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Armour arrangement and formation-level age resolution remain incomplete."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.201676","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/scutellosaurus.html"]},"secernosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian-Maastrichtian, about 75-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Kritosaurini"],"description":"Secernosaurus koerneri was a South American saurolophine hadrosaurid known from fragmentary, disarticulated cranial and postcranial remains.","facts":["It is among the first named South American hadrosaurids but is not known from a complete skeleton.","Kritosaurus australis is generally treated as a junior synonym of Secernosaurus."]},"ageReviewNote":"Patagonian occurrences are late Campanian-Maastrichtian; locality-level ages vary.","residualUncertainty":["Material is disarticulated and species-level referrals remain difficult."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724631003763508","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/secernosaurus.html"]},"segisaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"Sinemurian-Pliensbachian, about 199-183 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Neotheropoda","Coelophysoidea"],"description":"Segisaurus halli was a small coelophysoid theropod from the Kayenta Formation of Arizona, based on one incomplete, poorly preserved skeleton without a skull.","facts":["Redescription corrected several errors in the original mount and confirmed hollow bones.","Diet is inferred as carnivorous from coelophysoid affinity; no teeth or gut contents are preserved."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Kayenta Formation makes the old Mid Jurassic label incorrect.","residualUncertainty":["Only one incomplete individual is known."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2005)025%5B0835:NIOSHA%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/segisaurus.html"]},"segnosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Turonian, about 100-89 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Therizinosauria","Therizinosauridae"],"diet":"Herbivore","description":"Segnosaurus galbinensis was a large therizinosaurid from the Bayanshiree Formation of Mongolia. It is known from several partial individuals, including distinctive lower jaws and postcranial bones. Its jaws and specialised teeth support herbivory or plant-dominated omnivory, so the former catalogue label of carnivore was misleading.","facts":["The material includes both lower jaws, vertebrae, pelvis and hind-limb elements, but no complete skeleton.","The front of the lower jaw was toothless and probably bore a keratinous beak.","Closely packed, coarsely serrated teeth formed a specialised food-processing surface.","The 2016 dental study places Segnosaurus within the broader shift toward herbivory in derived therizinosaurs; exact foods remain unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type and referred specimens are from the Bayanshiree Formation. Its internal age calibration remains broader than a single point estimate.","residualUncertainty":["No direct gut contents identify the plants or other foods eaten.","The specimens are incomplete and do not form one complete individual."],"sourceUrls":["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824891/","https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Perle%252079.pdf"]},"shamosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 121-100 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Shamosaurus scutatus was a basal ankylosaurid from Mongolia, known from a complete skull and partial postcranial remains.","facts":["The skull is strong evidence for diagnosis; most armour and tail anatomy are incomplete.","Proposed synonymy with Gobisaurus has not achieved consensus."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is from Aptian-Albian Khukhtekskaya deposits; the broad formation age is retained.","residualUncertainty":["Postcranial referrals and relationship to Gobisaurus remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2015.1059985","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/shamosaurus.html"]},"shanag":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Hauterivian-Barremian, about 132-125 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Dromaeosauridae"],"length":null,"description":"Shanag ashile was a small dromaeosaurid from the Oosh Formation of Mongolia, known from one very fragmentary specimen consisting mainly of jaw and limited postcranial bones.","facts":["Its dromaeosaurid affinity is supported, but subfamily placement varies.","The catalogue 0.45 m length is cleared because almost all full-body anatomy is extrapolated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Oosh Formation is Hauterivian-Barremian; the catalogue range was reversed and too broad.","residualUncertainty":["The sole specimen is extremely incomplete."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0082(2007)3557%5B1:ASDTFS%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/shanag.html"]},"shantungosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 77-73.5 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Saurolophinae","Edmontosaurini"],"description":"Shantungosaurus giganteus was a very large saurolophine hadrosaurid from the Wangshi Group of Shandong, represented by numerous disarticulated skulls and skeletons from large bonebeds.","facts":["Gigantic body size is supported by multiple large limb and skull elements, but precise maxima vary.","Zhuchengosaurus and Huaxiaosaurus are widely treated as growth stages or junior synonyms of Shantungosaurus."]},"ageReviewNote":"Magnetostratigraphic and radiometric work places the relevant Wangshi Group dinosaur-bearing interval in the late Campanian, approximately 77-73.5 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Bonebed association and maximum size remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-6724.2011.00378.x","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.palaeo.2019.109451","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/shantungosaurus.html"]},"shunosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian-Callovian, about 168-164 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Shunosaurus lii was a basal eusauropod from the Lower Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan, known from numerous skeletons including skulls and articulated tails.","facts":["A bony tail club formed by fused terminal vertebrae and osteoderms is directly preserved.","Several skeletons collectively document nearly the whole body, but reconstructions remain composites."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Lower Shaximiao sample is Middle Jurassic, broadly Bathonian-Callovian.","residualUncertainty":["Species-level referrals and individual association are imperfect."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Zhang_88.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/shunosaurus.html"]},"shuvuuia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 75-71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Alvarezsauria","Alvarezsauridae"],"diet":null,"description":"Shuvuuia deserti was a small alvarezsaurid from Campanian Gobi formations, represented by several skulls and partial skeletons with short, powerful single-clawed forelimbs.","facts":["Feather-like filaments were reported with the type, although their preservation and biochemical identification have been debated.","The categorical omnivore label is cleared: insectivory is plausible, but direct diet evidence is absent."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Djadokhta and Baruungoyot records are Campanian; the original reversed 75-81 Ma range is corrected.","residualUncertainty":["Filament evidence, diet and nocturnal inference remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/32642","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abe7941","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/shuvuuia.html"]},"silvisaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Albian-early Cenomanian, about 103-99 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae"],"description":"Silvisaurus condrayi was a nodosaurid from the Dakota Formation of Kansas, known from one partial skeleton with a damaged skull and osteoderms.","facts":["The skull and postcranium are incomplete but associated.","Armour layout, tail end and exact size are mostly reconstructed."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Dakota Formation type is near the Albian-Cenomanian boundary, younger than 121-112 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["The old specimen needs modern redescription.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/4397517","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/silvisaurus.html"]},"sinocalliopteryx":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 125.8-124.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria"],"description":"Sinocalliopteryx gigas was a large compsognathid-like coelurosaur from the Yixian Formation, known from two articulated feathered skeletons with gut contents.","facts":["Filamentous integument is preserved around the body.","Gut contents include a dromaeosaurid leg and birds in different specimens, directly documenting carnivory but not hunting method.","The monophyly and family-level name of compsognathid-like theropods remain analysis-dependent."]},"ageReviewNote":"High-precision dating brackets the Yixian Formation at 125.755-124.122 Ma, within the Barremian on the current time scale.","residualUncertainty":["Gut contents do not distinguish predation from scavenging.","Family-level placement is unsettled."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285766543_A_new_giant_compsognathid_dinosaur_with_long_filamentous_integuments_from_Lower_Cretaceous_of_Northeastern_China","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0044012","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwab063","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwaf068","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sinocalliopteryx.html"]},"sinornithosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 125.8-124.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Dromaeosauridae","Microraptorinae"],"description":"Sinornithosaurus was a small feathered microraptorine from the Yixian Formation, known from several articulated skeletons including skulls.","facts":["Pennaceous feathers and body covering are directly preserved.","Claims of venom-delivery anatomy are not accepted; the proposed grooves and tooth form are not uniquely associated with venom."]},"ageReviewNote":"High-precision dating brackets the Yixian Formation at 125.755-124.122 Ma, within the Barremian on the current time scale.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries and aerial ability remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/45769","https://doi.org/10.1007/s12542-010-0074-9","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwab063","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sinornithosaurus.html"]},"sinosauropteryx":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 125.8-124.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria"],"description":"Sinosauropteryx was a small compsognathid-like coelurosaur from the Yixian Formation, known from multiple articulated skeletons with filamentous integument and some gut contents.","facts":["It provided the first widely accepted non-avian dinosaur integument outside birds.","Melanosome studies support a banded tail and reddish-brown pattern in at least one specimen, not a universal genus-wide palette.","Recent analyses disagree over the monophyly and family-level name of compsognathid-like theropods."]},"ageReviewNote":"High-precision dating brackets the Yixian Formation at 125.755-124.122 Ma, within the Barremian on the current time scale.","residualUncertainty":["Colour and diet observations are specimen-specific.","Family-level placement is unsettled."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/48566","https://doi.org/10.1038/nature08740","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwab063","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwaf068"]},"sinovenator":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 125.8-124.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Troodontidae"],"description":"Sinovenator changii was a small early troodontid from the Yixian Formation, known from several partial skeletons including skull material.","facts":["Its mosaic anatomy helped establish close relationships among troodontids, dromaeosaurids and birds.","No direct diet or feather impressions are established for the principal type material."]},"ageReviewNote":"High-precision dating brackets the Yixian Formation at 125.755-124.122 Ma, within the Barremian on the current time scale.","residualUncertainty":["Some referred specimens and ontogenetic stages require careful comparison."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/415780a","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwab063","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sinovenator.html"]},"sinraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, about 161-158 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Metriacanthosauridae"],"description":"Sinraptor is a Chinese metriacanthosaurid genus anchored by S. dongi, a substantial skeleton with skull from the Shishugou Formation. S. hepingensis from Sichuan is less securely placed in the same genus.","facts":["The type species preserves an excellent skull and much of the postcranium.","Genus-level age and anatomy should not blend S. dongi and the debated combination S. hepingensis without qualification."]},"ageReviewNote":"The upper Shishugou type horizon of S. dongi is Oxfordian, approximately 161-158 Ma. S. hepingensis should not extend the genus range automatically because its assignment remains disputed.","residualUncertainty":["The generic placement of S. hepingensis remains uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/45474757","https://doi.org/10.1139/cjes-2019-0231","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sinraptor.html"]},"sonidosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"exact stage unresolved; proposed ages range from Turonian to Campanian or younger","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Titanosauria"],"description":"Sonidosaurus saihangaobiensis was a small titanosaur from Inner Mongolia, based on a partial postcranial skeleton.","facts":["The specimen includes vertebrae, ribs, pelvis and limb bones but no skull.","The catalogue nine-metre length is cleared pending modern specimen-level reassessment.","Conflicting biostratigraphic correlations prevent a defensible narrow numerical age."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Iren Dabasu Formation has competing vertebrate and microfossil age models, including Turonian, Coniacian-Campanian and late Campanian-Maastrichtian correlations; the type cannot presently be assigned one narrow range.","residualUncertainty":["Formation age and titanosaur relationships need renewed assessment.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-6724.2006.tb00790.x","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2005.03.004","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2023.2199817","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/sonidosaurus.html"]},"spicomellus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"Bathonian-Callovian, about 168-164 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria"],"length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Spicomellus afer was an early ankylosaur from the El Mers Group of Morocco and is the oldest named ankylosaur currently known. The genus was initially based on a rib bearing fused spikes, but a much more complete specimen described in 2025 confirmed its ankylosaur identity and revealed exceptionally elaborate armour and a tail weapon.","facts":["The 2021 holotype is a partial rib with dermal spikes fused directly to its surface.","A more complete 2025 specimen preserves vertebrae, ribs, limb elements and diverse armour.","Tail handle vertebrae show that a tail weapon evolved much earlier in ankylosaurs than previously documented.","Display and defence are plausible functions of the armour, but behaviour is not directly preserved."]},"ageReviewNote":"Both described specimens are from the Middle Jurassic El Mers Group of Morocco; the age is formation-level rather than a direct date on each bone.","residualUncertainty":["The specimens do not preserve a complete skull or skeleton.","The external arrangement and behavioural functions of every armour element remain partly reconstructed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-021-01553-6","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09453-6"]},"spinosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian, about 100–94 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Spinosauridae","Spinosaurinae","Spinosaurus"],"diet":"Carnivore (fish and other prey)","locomotion":"Bipedal on land; swimming ability disputed","massKg":null,"description":"Spinosaurus aegyptiacus was a very large spinosaurid theropod from Cenomanian North Africa. Its long narrow jaws, conical teeth, retracted nostrils, dense bones and deep tail show strong association with aquatic prey and habitats. How it fed remains actively disputed: proposed underwater pursuit and diving conflict with biomechanical analyses favouring wading or surface feeding.","facts":["The original Egyptian holotype, BSP 1912 VIII 19, was destroyed in the 1944 bombing of Munich; it survives only in Stromer's 1915 description and photographs.","A partial skeleton from the Kem Kem beds of Morocco, FSAC-KK 11888, was designated the neotype in 2014 and expanded in 2020 to include a deep, paddle-like tail.","Shortened hind limbs, dense bones and a tall sail are now well established; their functional meaning is not.","High bone density was interpreted in 2022 as evidence for subaqueous foraging.","A 2022 eLife analysis argued that stability, drag and buoyancy make a fully aquatic pursuit-predator model unlikely; semiaquatic adaptations are well supported, but a single settled swimming and hunting model is not."]},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed range follows Cenomanian material from the Bahariya Formation and Kem Kem Group. Isolated spinosaurid material should not automatically extend the species range.","residualUncertainty":["The association and taxonomic identity of some Moroccan bones remain debated.","Maximum length and mass estimates depend strongly on composite reconstruction.","The extent of diving and underwater propulsion remains an active scientific disagreement.","The soft-tissue form and function of the sail are unknown."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/bibliography/62187","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1258750","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2190-3","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-04528-0","https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.80092"]},"staurikosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Carnian, about 233 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Herrerasauria","Herrerasauridae"],"description":"Staurikosaurus pricei was a small herrerasaurid from the Santa Maria Formation of Brazil, known from one incomplete skeleton with jaw and postcranial material.","facts":["It is substantially older than the catalogue range of 227-221 Ma.","Carnivory and bipedality are supported, but skull and forelimb anatomy are incomplete."]},"ageReviewNote":"Radiometric and biostratigraphic work places the type in the Carnian near 233 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Herrerasaur placement relative to Dinosauria varies among analyses."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.11646/zootaxa.2079.1.1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/staurikosaurus.html"]},"stegoceras":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 77-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae"],"diet":null,"description":"Stegoceras validum was a small pachycephalosaurid from the Belly River Group, known from many domes, skulls and partial skeletons.","facts":["Growth strongly altered dome shape, making juveniles look unlike adults.","Head-impact behaviour is biomechanically plausible but its exact form and diet remain debated."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure material is Campanian Dinosaur Park and Oldman formations.","residualUncertainty":["Diet and combat style are unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0021092","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/stegoceras.html"]},"stegosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian–Tithonian, about 155–150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae","Stegosaurus"],"diet":"Herbivore","locomotion":"Quadrupedal","description":"Stegosaurus was a large stegosaur from the Morrison Formation, known from multiple species and many partial to nearly complete skeletons, including articulated plates and tail spikes.","facts":["The type species is S. stenops, fixed by the ICZN in 2013 because the original type species S. armatus rested on undiagnostic material.","The holotype of S. stenops, USNM 4934, is a nearly complete articulated skeleton with plates in place, collected at Garden Park, Colorado, in 1886.","NHMUK PV R36730 (\"Sophie\"), described in 2015, is the most complete individual known, with about 85 per cent of the skeleton.","Plate and spike positions are directly supported by articulated specimens: two staggered rows of plates and two pairs of tail spikes.","Thermoregulation, display and species recognition have all been proposed for plates; no single exclusive function is established."]},"ageReviewNote":"Core species are Kimmeridgian–Tithonian Morrison Formation; species ranges within the formation differ.","residualUncertainty":["Species taxonomy within the Morrison sample is debated.","Plate function remains unresolved.","The keratinous covering of the plates is inferred rather than preserved.","Feeding height and posture are interpretive."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201908002459","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0138352","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2014.0984","https://www.biotaxa.org/bzn/article/view/38493","https://doi.org/10.2475/ajs.s3-34.203.413"]},"stenonychosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 76-74 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Paraves","Troodontidae"],"taxonomicStatus":"validity and synonymy with Troodon are currently disputed","description":"Stenonychosaurus inequalis is the name applied since 2017 to troodontid skeletal material from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. A 2025 paper instead argued that it is a junior synonym of Troodon formosus and proposed a neotype for Troodon. Because that neotype requires formal ICZN action, the wiki presents both treatments rather than declaring either settled.","facts":["The Stenonychosaurus holotype CMN 8539 comprises hind-limb elements and is itself incomplete.","A 2017 revision separated Dinosaur Park troodontid skeletons from the tooth-based name Troodon.","A 2025 analysis found the Two Medicine and Dinosaur Park material closely comparable and preferred the older name Troodon.","Claims about exceptional intelligence are based mainly on relative braincase size and should not be translated into human-like cognition."]},"ageReviewNote":"The profile is restricted to Campanian Dinosaur Park Formation material; it does not inherit every historical North American occurrence assigned to Troodon.","residualUncertainty":["The 2025 Troodon neotype proposal has not by itself replaced the existing holotype.","The limits of Stenonychosaurus relative to Troodon and other Campanian troodontids remain contested."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/cjes-2017-0031","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-paleontology/article/troodontid-specimens-from-the-cretaceous-two-medicine-formation-of-montana-usa-and-the-validity-of-troodon-formosus/3E58F1FDA3FE53DE569E0D0B20E79F22","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/stenonychosaurus.html"]},"stenopelix":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Berriasian, about 141-139 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","?Marginocephalia"],"description":"Stenopelix valdensis is a small ornithischian from the Lower Cretaceous of north-western Germany, known from one largely articulated skeleton that lacks the skull. Re-examination found its anatomy compatible with Marginocephalia but insufficient to identify it confidently as either a pachycephalosaur or ceratopsian.","facts":["The holotype is the most complete dinosaur skeleton known from the German Wealden deposits but preserves no skull.","Characters formerly used to place it in Pachycephalosauria or Ceratopsia were reinterpreted or found to be more widespread.","It is best treated conservatively as a possible marginocephalian rather than the earliest confirmed pachycephalosaur.","Diet and detailed head anatomy cannot be established directly without the skull."]},"ageReviewNote":"The holotype comes from the Obernkirchen Sandstone of the Bueckeberg Formation and is late Berriasian; the former 127-121 Ma range was too young.","residualUncertainty":["The skull is entirely unknown.","Even referral to Marginocephalia remains tentative."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.app.pan.pl/article/item/app54-021.html","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/stenopelix.html"]},"struthiomimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 77-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Ornithomimidae"],"diet":null,"description":"Struthiomimus altus was an ornithomimid from the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta, known from several partial skeletons including well-preserved limbs and hands.","facts":["Long hindlimbs support rapid bipedal locomotion, but no exact top speed can be recovered.","Its toothless beak does not identify a single diet; herbivory and omnivory remain alternatives."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure S. altus material is late Campanian Dinosaur Park Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Diet and assignment of non-type formations remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2008.00791.x","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/struthiomimus.html"]},"struthiosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Santonian-Maastrichtian, about 86-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Nodosauridae"],"description":"Struthiosaurus was a small European nodosaurid represented by three named species and numerous fragmentary specimens from Austria, Hungary, Romania and France.","facts":["No single specimen supplies a complete skeleton.","Genus-level range and anatomy are composites across geographically separated species; a Santonian Hungarian humerus was referred only as cf. Struthiosaurus."]},"ageReviewNote":"Named species are Campanian-Maastrichtian, while a cf. Struthiosaurus humerus may extend the record into the Santonian.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries and the Santonian referral remain difficult."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2013.03.006","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/struthiosaurus.html"]},"stygimoloch":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 67-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Marginocephalia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae","Pachycephalosaurus"],"taxonomicStatus":"usually treated as a juvenile or subadult Pachycephalosaurus, possibly P. spinifer, rather than a separate genus","acceptedName":false,"length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Stygimoloch spinifer is a historical name for spiky pachycephalosaur skull material from latest Cretaceous North America. Cranial growth and histology studies support interpreting this morphology as an immature stage of Pachycephalosaurus rather than a separate genus, although whether spinifer represents a distinct species within Pachycephalosaurus remains open.","facts":["The holotype and referred material are incomplete skull roofs and skulls, not complete skeletons.","The best-preserved skull shows a small dome with long rear spikes.","Horner and Goodwin interpreted the porous, rapidly changing skull tissue as subadult anatomy between Dracorex-like juveniles and adult Pachycephalosaurus.","The page is retained to explain the familiar historical name and the continuing species-level question."]},"ageReviewNote":"The relevant material is from latest Maastrichtian Hell Creek and equivalent deposits. Occurrence counts do not distinguish growth stages reliably.","residualUncertainty":["The genus-level synonymy is well supported but not universally adopted in phylogenetic datasets.","Whether spinifer is merely an ontogenetic stage of P. wyomingensis or a second Pachycephalosaurus species remains unresolved."],"sourceUrls":["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2762616/","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1998.10011064","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/stygimoloch.html"]},"styracosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 76-75 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Centrosaurinae"],"description":"Styracosaurus albertensis was a centrosaurine ceratopsid from the Dinosaur Park Formation, known from skulls, partial skeletons and bonebed material.","facts":["Its long nasal horn and frill spikes are directly preserved but vary among individuals.","Rubeosaurus ovatus has been proposed as a second Styracosaurus species, a synonymy that remains debated."]},"ageReviewNote":"The species is restricted to late Campanian Dinosaur Park Formation, near 76-75 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Frill variation and Rubeosaurus synonymy remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2007)27%5B944:AROTLC%5D2.0.CO;2","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.200284","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2019.104308"]},"suchomimus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"late Aptian-early Albian, about 115-112 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Spinosauridae","Baryonychinae"],"massKg":null,"description":"Suchomimus tenerensis was a baryonychine spinosaurid from the Elrhaz Formation of Niger, known from a partial skeleton representing much of the body but assembled from disarticulated material.","facts":["Long jaws, conical teeth and large thumb claws support spinosaurid feeding specialisation.","Fish consumption is likely by comparison and anatomy, but a 5,000 kg point mass is unsupported."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Elrhaz Formation is generally correlated to about 115-112 Ma around the Aptian-Albian transition; a 125 Ma lower bound is too old.","residualUncertainty":["Possible synonymy with Cristatusaurus and locomotor ecology remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.282.5392.1298","https://doi.org/10.26879/1110","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0144695"]},"supersaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, about 154-150 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Diplodocoidea","Diplodocidae","Diplodocinae"],"massKg":null,"description":"Supersaurus vivianae was a very large diplodocid from the Morrison Formation, founded on a scapulocoracoid and known through referred partial skeletons.","facts":["The Jimbo specimen adds articulated vertebral material but no complete skeleton exists.","Very long body estimates are plausible; the catalogue 35-tonne point mass is too precise and is removed."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure specimens are Kimmeridgian-Tithonian Morrison Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Referral of widely separated specimens and maximum length remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://biostor.org/reference/248729","https://giw.utahgeology.org/giw/index.php/GIW/article/view/66"]},"talarurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Santonian, about 100-84 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Talarurus plicatospineus was an ankylosaurid from the Bayan Shireh Formation of Mongolia, known from several partial, disarticulated individuals including skull and tail-club material.","facts":["A modern skull redescription corrected a composite reconstruction that had included another ankylosaur.","The tail club is supported, but armour and body proportions are composite."]},"ageReviewNote":"Bayan Shireh dating spans Cenomanian-Santonian; the original 99-89 Ma range is only approximate.","residualUncertainty":["Composite historical material and formation age remain sources of uncertainty."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2019.104340","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/talarurus.html"]},"tanius":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 84-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Tanius sinensis was a basally branching hadrosauroid from the Upper Cretaceous Wangshi Group of Shandong, China. Modern postcranial revision supports the type species as diagnosable, while the other species historically assigned to Tanius are dubious or belong elsewhere.","facts":["The type individual includes a partial skull and much of the postcranial skeleton, but is not complete.","Tanius sinensis falls outside the most derived hadrosaurids in recent analyses.","T. chingkankouensis was regarded as a nomen dubium and T. laiyangensis was separated as Laiyangosaurus.","Herbivory and facultative quadrupedality follow hadrosauroid anatomy; exact behaviour and herd size are not directly preserved."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type species is from the Jiangjunding Formation of the Wangshi Group. A former 89-66 Ma genus-wide range incorrectly blended uncertain species and formations.","residualUncertainty":["A complete modern cranial redescription and full phylogenetic revision remain desirable.","The exact age calibration of Wangshi Group units varies among studies."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2021.1914642","https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.24097","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/tanius.html"]},"tarbosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauridae","Tyrannosaurinae"],"description":"Tarbosaurus bataar was a large tyrannosaurine from the Nemegt Formation of Mongolia, known from numerous skulls and skeletons across growth stages.","facts":["Most current analyses retain Tarbosaurus as distinct from Tyrannosaurus, though the combination Tyrannosaurus bataar has historical use.","Juveniles had proportionally longer, lower skulls and changed feeding mechanics through growth."]},"ageReviewNote":"Nemegt Formation specimens are Maastrichtian, about 70-66 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Generic separation from Tyrannosaurus and species-level referrals remain discussed."],"sourceUrls":["https://app.pan.pl/streszczenie/item/app48-161.html","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2011.557116","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/tarbosaurus.html"]},"tarchia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Tarchia kielanae was an ankylosaurid from the Baruungoyot Formation, founded on a skull and limited postcranial material. Numerous specimens historically called Tarchia have been redistributed among species and genera.","facts":["The type is not a complete skeleton.","Minotaurasaurus has often been synonymised with Tarchia, while recent revisions recognise more than one Tarchia species."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type species is late Campanian Baruungoyot Formation; younger Nemegt referrals need separate review.","residualUncertainty":["Species limits and many referrals remain unsettled."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/zoj.12185","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cretres.2016.10.004","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/tarchia.html"]},"telmatosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 71-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauroidea"],"description":"Telmatosaurus transsylvanicus was a small-bodied hadrosauroid from the Hateg Basin of Romania, known from several partial skulls and skeletons.","facts":["It lies outside or near the base of Hadrosauridae in most analyses.","A facial bone preserves an ameloblastoma-like tumour, an individual pathology rather than a species trait."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure material is Maastrichtian Hateg Basin, not the full 84-66 Ma span.","residualUncertainty":["Species referrals and the degree of island dwarfism remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/srep29271","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x"]},"tenontosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 120-105 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Iguanodontia"],"description":"Tenontosaurus was a medium-to-large iguanodontian ornithopod from the Early Cretaceous of North America. Many skeletons, including a near-complete individual, document its long tail and robust body. Multi-individual sites and associations with Deinonychus are real, but they do not by themselves prove permanent herds or coordinated pack hunting.","facts":["Tenontosaurus tilletti is represented by numerous partial skeletons and at least one near-complete skeleton.","The tail was exceptionally long and reinforced by ossified tendons.","Several bonebeds contain Deinonychus teeth and remains, demonstrating repeated ecological association but not a unique hunting strategy.","Multi-individual accumulations may reflect sociality, drought or transport; the wiki no longer states herd behaviour as established fact."]},"ageReviewNote":"The reviewed range covers secure Aptian-Albian occurrences in the Cloverly and Antlers formations. Other referrals require specimen-level review.","residualUncertainty":["The causes of multi-individual accumulations and predator-prey associations remain debated.","The validity and limits of the second named species T. dossi require further revision."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022336000042402","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.1995.10011256","https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.2616","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/tenontosaurus.html"]},"thecodontosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Triassic","mya":"Rhaetian, about 205-201 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha"],"diet":"Herbivore or omnivore (uncertain)","description":"Thecodontosaurus antiquus was a small basal sauropodomorph from Rhaetian fissure deposits near Bristol, represented by abundant disarticulated bones and newly described partial skeletons.","facts":["Many original Bristol fossils were destroyed in 1940, but surviving and newer material documents much of the anatomy.","A categorical omnivore label is not established; tooth form permits plant-dominated or mixed feeding interpretations."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type assemblage is Rhaetian, much narrower and younger than 227-205 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Pantydraco validity and diet remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2020.1770774","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/thecodontosaurus.html"]},"therizinosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 70-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Therizinosauria","Therizinosauridae"],"massKg":null,"description":"Therizinosaurus cheloniformis was a giant therizinosaurid from the Nemegt Formation, known mainly from enormous forelimbs and claws plus limited hindlimb material.","facts":["No complete skull or skeleton is known.","Herbivory is inferred from close relatives; the five-tonne catalogue mass is too precise for the fragmentary material and is removed."]},"ageReviewNote":"All secure material is Maastrichtian Nemegt Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Size, skull anatomy and claw function remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2014.0497","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2010.488045","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-11063-5","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/therizinosaurus.html"]},"thescelosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 68-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Thescelosauridae"],"diet":"Herbivore or omnivore (uncertain)","description":"Thescelosaurus was a robust small ornithopod from latest Maastrichtian North America, known from several partial and near-complete skeletons across multiple species.","facts":["The alleged fossilised four-chambered heart in one specimen is now generally regarded as an iron-rich concretion.","Jaw and tooth anatomy support plant feeding, but occasional omnivory cannot be excluded."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure records are Hell Creek, Lance and equivalent latest Maastrichtian formations; 76 Ma is too old.","residualUncertainty":["Species limits and diet breadth remain under study."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/039.029.0328","https://doi.org/10.1007/s00114-010-0760-1","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/thescelosaurus.html"]},"torosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 68-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Marginocephalia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae","Triceratopsini"],"taxonomicStatus":"generally retained as distinct, with a disputed hypothesis that it represents mature Triceratops","description":"Torosaurus was a very large chasmosaurine ceratopsid from latest Cretaceous North America, diagnosed mainly from skulls with an elongated, fenestrated frill. A prominent hypothesis interprets Torosaurus as the fully mature form of Triceratops, but other analyses and Canadian material support retaining it as a distinct genus. The wiki presents this as an unresolved taxonomic debate.","facts":["Skulls and frill material are much better represented than the postcranial skeleton.","Scannella and Horner proposed in 2010 that frill remodelling transformed mature Triceratops into the Torosaurus morphology.","Longrich and Field found apparently immature Torosaurus and mature Triceratops characters inconsistent with a simple growth sequence.","Canadian specimens described in 2022 expand the geographic record and were considered consistent with a distinct genus."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure occurrences are latest Maastrichtian. Formation-wide database ranges and contested referrals should not broaden the headline age without specimen review.","residualUncertainty":["Whether all Torosaurus specimens can be separated from mature Triceratops remains disputed.","The postcranial anatomy is less securely known than the skull."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2010.483632","https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0032623","https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlab120"]},"torvosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, about 155-148 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Megalosauroidea","Megalosauridae"],"description":"Torvosaurus was a large megalosaurid represented by T. tanneri in the Morrison Formation and T. gurneyi in Portugal, both known from incomplete but diagnostic material.","facts":["No complete Torvosaurus skeleton exists.","Species estimates and anatomy must not combine the North American and Portuguese samples without qualification."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure material from the accepted species is Kimmeridgian-Tithonian, approximately 155-148 Ma; a 165 Ma lower bound is too old.","residualUncertainty":["Species distinctions and maximum size remain limited by incomplete material."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0088905","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/torvosaurus.html"]},"triceratops":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 68–66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsidae","Chasmosaurinae","Triceratops"],"diet":"Herbivore","locomotion":"Quadrupedal","description":"Triceratops was a large chasmosaurine ceratopsid from the Hell Creek and Lance formations, known from many skulls and partial skeletons. T. horridus and T. prorsus show stratigraphic and morphological separation.","facts":["The holotype of T. horridus, YPM 1820, is a partial skull collected by John Bell Hatcher in 1888 from the Lance Formation of Wyoming and described by Marsh in 1889.","Well over a hundred skulls or partial skulls are known, making Triceratops the most abundant large dinosaur of the Hell Creek Formation.","Growth substantially altered horn orientation and frill shape: juveniles had short, backward-curving brow horns that rotated forward with age.","The proposal that Torosaurus is an old Triceratops remains controversial and is not consensus.","Two species are recognised, T. horridus from lower and T. prorsus from upper Hell Creek strata."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure occurrences are restricted to the final roughly two million years of the Cretaceous; T. horridus occurs lower and T. prorsus higher in the Hell Creek Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Species boundaries within the Hell Creek sequence remain a matter of definition.","The relationship between Triceratops, Torosaurus and Nedoceratops is debated.","Complete articulated postcranial skeletons are rare compared with skulls.","Horn function is inferred from pathology and analogy rather than observed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1313334111","https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2006.3643","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0032623","https://doi.org/10.5962/bhl.title.61748"]},"troodon":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 77-74 million years ago for the currently argued species concept","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Maniraptora","Paraves","Troodontidae"],"taxonomicStatus":"contested tooth-based name; a 2025 paper defended validity and proposed a neotype, reversing the prevalent post-2017 treatment","acceptedName":false,"length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Troodon formosus was named from a single tooth collected in Montana. From 2017 the tooth was widely treated as insufficient to anchor the many skeletons historically assigned to Troodon, and those fossils were reassigned to Stenonychosaurus and other taxa. A 2025 study defended Troodon as valid, synonymised Stenonychosaurus with it and proposed a Two Medicine Formation skeleton as a neotype. That neotype requires ICZN action, so the issue is not presented as settled.","facts":["The holotype ANSP 9259 is one isolated tooth with incomplete locality information.","The familiar full-body reconstruction was assembled from referred troodontid skeletons, not from the holotype.","The 2017 revisions treated Troodon as a doubtful name and restored Stenonychosaurus for Dinosaur Park material.","The 2025 paper argued for priority and stability under Troodon and proposed MOR 553 as a neotype, but explicitly noted that only the ICZN can replace an existing holotype in this way."]},"ageReviewNote":"A single genus-wide 77-66 Ma range is not defensible while the name-bearing tooth and referred skeletons remain taxonomically disputed. The current 2025 species concept focuses on Campanian Montana and Alberta material.","residualUncertainty":["The proposed neotype has not yet been established by an ICZN ruling.","Researchers disagree on whether the original tooth can anchor the expanded skeletal species concept.","Historic records from many formations cannot all be retained without specimen-level revision."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1139/cjes-2017-0031","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-paleontology/article/troodontid-specimens-from-the-cretaceous-two-medicine-formation-of-montana-usa-and-the-validity-of-troodon-formosus/3E58F1FDA3FE53DE569E0D0B20E79F22","https://doi.org/10.1017/jpa.2024.67","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/troodon.html"]},"tsagantegia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Cenomanian-Santonian, about 100-84 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Ankylosauria","Ankylosauridae"],"description":"Tsagantegia longicranialis was an ankylosaurid from the Bayan Shireh Formation, based almost entirely on one complete skull.","facts":["The skull is distinctive but no associated body skeleton is known.","Armour, tail club, size and locomotor details are inferred from relatives."]},"ageReviewNote":"Bayan Shireh dating gives a broad Cenomanian-Santonian range.","residualUncertainty":["Only one skull anchors the genus."],"sourceUrls":["https://palaeontologia.pan.pl/Archive/1993-42_119-141_10-17.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2015.1059985"]},"tsintaosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Hadrosauridae","Lambeosaurinae"],"description":"Tsintaosaurus spinorhinus was a lambeosaurine hadrosaurid from the Wangshi Group of Shandong, known from multiple skull and skeletal specimens.","facts":["Modern reconstruction shows a complex crest involving several bones, not a single unicorn-like spike.","The crest was hollow and connected to nasal passages, but exact sound and soft tissue are unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type-bearing Wangshi units are late Campanian, not a single exact 75 Ma date.","residualUncertainty":["Crest soft tissue and species-level referrals remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0082268","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2009.00617.x"]},"tuojiangosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, about 160-157 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae"],"description":"Tuojiangosaurus multispinus was a stegosaur from the Upper Shaximiao Formation, known from a partial skeleton including skull fragments, plates and tail spikes.","facts":["The famous full plate sequence is a reconstruction from incomplete and disarticulated armour.","Quadrupedal herbivory is secure, while exact plate count and arrangement remain uncertain."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Upper Shaximiao type is Oxfordian; a range to 155 Ma should not be treated as directly dated.","residualUncertainty":["Armour order and skull completeness are limited."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Dong_et_al._77.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201908002459"]},"tylocephale":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"late Campanian, about 75-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Pachycephalosauria","Pachycephalosauridae"],"diet":null,"length":null,"description":"Tylocephale gilmorei was a pachycephalosaurid from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia, known from a partial skull and jaw.","facts":["Its tall dome is directly preserved but nearly all postcranial anatomy is unknown.","The catalogue length and categorical diet are removed because the type does not support them directly."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is late Campanian Baruungoyot Formation.","residualUncertainty":["Body size, diet and dome function remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://palaeontologia.pan.pl/Archive/1974-30_45-102_8-22.pdf","https://www.nmnaturalhistory.org/sites/default/files/publications/18_sullivan_pachy.pdf","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/tylocephale.html"]},"tyrannosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"latest Maastrichtian, about 68–66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea","Tyrannosauridae","Tyrannosaurinae","Tyrannosaurus"],"diet":"Carnivore","locomotion":"Bipedal","description":"Tyrannosaurus rex was a giant tyrannosaurine from latest Maastrichtian western North America, known from dozens of skulls and partial to substantial skeletons. The growth series must now be separated from the active dispute over which small-bodied specimens belong to juvenile T. rex versus the distinct contemporary genus Nanotyrannus.","facts":["The holotype CM 9380 is a partial skull and skeleton collected by Barnum Brown in 1902 from the Hell Creek Formation of Montana and described by Osborn in 1905.","FMNH PR 2081 (\"Sue\") is the most complete and among the largest specimens, preserving about 90 per cent of the skeleton by bone volume.","Growth, bite force, injuries and skin impressions are directly studied from a large sample, but Carr's 2020 assignment of controversial small specimens to the T. rex growth series is no longer treated as settled.","Two independent late-2025 studies supported a mature, distinct Nanotyrannus using a new associated specimen and histology of the holotype; further testing may refine which specimens belong to each genus.","Adult T. rex scale impressions exist, while the amount of feathering remains unknown."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure T. rex records occupy the final roughly two million years of the Cretaceous, contemporaneous with specimens newly reassigned to Nanotyrannus.","residualUncertainty":["Assignment of small-bodied Hell Creek tyrannosaurs between Tyrannosaurus and Nanotyrannus is unresolved.","Proposals to split Tyrannosaurus into multiple species have not gained consensus.","Maximum body mass depends on the estimation method.","The distribution of feathers, if any, on adults or juveniles is not directly known."],"sourceUrls":["https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/a02fc8e6-2c29-45cd-a95a-271d17d1786f","https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2003.10010947","https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.9192","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09801-6","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adx8706","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2017.0092"]},"tyrannotitan":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 121-101 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Carcharodontosauridae","Giganotosaurini"],"massKg":null,"description":"Tyrannotitan chubutensis was a giant carcharodontosaurid from the Cerro Barcino Formation of Patagonia, known from two incomplete individuals with cranial and postcranial material.","facts":["The holotype and paratype together document much of the axial and appendicular skeleton but no complete individual.","The catalogue seven-tonne mass is cleared because published estimates depend on scaling incomplete specimens."]},"ageReviewNote":"Published descriptions have called the Cerro Castaño Member Aptian or Albian; the single 101 Ma catalogue date is therefore overprecise.","residualUncertainty":["Formation age, maximum size and some specimen associations remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00114-005-0623-3","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2013.861830","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/tyrannotitan.html"]},"udanoceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 84-72 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Leptoceratopsidae"],"description":"Udanoceratops tschizhovi was a large leptoceratopsid from the Djadokhta-equivalent deposits of Mongolia, founded on a large, incomplete skull with limited postcranial referrals.","facts":["The skull supports a large-bodied leptoceratopsid but not a precise whole-body size.","Quadrupedal versus mixed locomotion and species ecology remain uncertain."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type is Campanian; formation correlation does not support a narrow point date.","residualUncertainty":["Postcranial referrals and formation identity need renewed study."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Kurzanov_92.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0013835","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/udanoceratops.html"]},"unenlagia":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Turonian-Coniacian, about 90-86 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Dromaeosauridae","Unenlagiinae"],"description":"Unenlagia was a Gondwanan dromaeosaurid from the Portezuelo Formation of Argentina, represented by two named species based on incomplete postcranial skeletons.","facts":["Shoulder anatomy once prompted a bird-like flapping interpretation, but powered flight is not supported for the large animal.","No secure skull is known for U. comahuensis, limiting diet and head reconstruction."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Portezuelo Formation is Turonian-Coniacian, approximately 90-86 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Species distinction and unenlagiine relationships remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/387390a0","https://doi.org/10.1590/S0001-37652011000100009","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/unenlagia.html"]},"urbacodon":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Turonian, about 94-90 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Troodontidae"],"description":"Urbacodon itemirensis was a troodontid from the Bissekty Formation of Uzbekistan, based primarily on a partial lower jaw with unserrated teeth.","facts":["Almost all full-body anatomy is unknown.","The catalogue one-metre length is cleared because a jaw alone does not support that precision."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Bissekty type is Turonian, close to 94-90 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Validity and troodontid placement rely on limited jaw anatomy.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1671/0272-4634(2007)27%5B87:ANTDTF%5D2.0.CO;2","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/urbacodon.html"]},"utahraptor":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-20","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Valanginian, about 135.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Dromaeosauridae","Dromaeosaurinae"],"massKg":null,"description":"Utahraptor ostrommaysorum was a giant dromaeosaurid from the Yellow Cat Member of the Cedar Mountain Formation, known from several incomplete individuals and a difficult multi-individual block.","facts":["Large sickle claws and robust limb elements are directly known.","New dating places the Stikes Quarry near 135.1 Ma, in the Valanginian rather than the Barremian; the 280 kg point mass is cleared as too precise."]},"ageReviewNote":"CA-ID-TIMS U-Pb dating and carbon-isotope stratigraphy place the Utahraptor Ridge horizon at 135.10 +/- 0.30 Ma, in the Valanginian.","residualUncertainty":["The block is still being prepared; maximum size and specimen association remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284308656_A_large_dromaeosaurid_Theropoda_from_the_Lower_Cretaceous_of_eastern_Utah","https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences13020032","https://doi.org/10.1206/748.1"]},"valdosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 125.8-121.4 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Dryosauridae"],"description":"Valdosaurus canaliculatus was a small dryosaurid from the Wessex Formation of the Isle of Wight, founded on paired femora with additional British material.","facts":["African and other historical referrals have largely been excluded.","The limited type makes precise size and anatomy dependent on referred specimens."]},"ageReviewNote":"Secure Isle of Wight material is Barremian; the numerical bounds use the current International Chronostratigraphic Chart rather than the obsolete 130-125 Ma calibration.","residualUncertainty":["Referral of non-type bones and diagnostic strength remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-4983.2011.01076.x","https://stratigraphy.org/chart/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/valdosaurus.html"]},"velociraptor":{"status":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-09-19","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Campanian, about 75–71 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Dromaeosauridae","Velociraptorinae","Velociraptor"],"diet":"Carnivore","locomotion":"Bipedal","description":"Velociraptor mongoliensis was a small dromaeosaurid from the Djadokhta Formation of Mongolia, known from several skulls and partial skeletons including the Fighting Dinosaurs specimen.","facts":["The holotype AMNH 6515 is a crushed but complete skull with a sickle claw, collected at the Flaming Cliffs in 1923 and described by Osborn in 1924.","The Fighting Dinosaurs specimen MPC-D 100/25 preserves a Velociraptor locked with a Protoceratops, apparently buried together mid-struggle.","Quill knobs on an ulna (IGM 100/981) provide direct evidence for large arm feathers.","The Fighting Dinosaurs preserve contact with Protoceratops but do not reveal the entire encounter or habitual hunting style.","V. osmolskae from Bayan Mandahu, China, is a second species known from limited cranial material."]},"ageReviewNote":"V. mongoliensis is Campanian Djadokhta Formation; V. osmolskae is a separate Chinese species with limited material.","residualUncertainty":["Species limits between V. mongoliensis and V. osmolskae rest on limited material for the latter.","Body feathering is inferred from relatives rather than preserved directly.","Social hunting is not supported by direct evidence.","The exact function of the sickle claw remains interpretive."],"sourceUrls":["https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/25d800cd-76d7-4ced-9dae-39e46f746def","https://digitallibrary.amnh.org/items/7c9b0154-7a3c-4618-8226-2b1e5523c334","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1145076"]},"vulcanodon":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"probably Sinemurian-Pliensbachian, about 199-184 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Gravisauria"],"description":"Vulcanodon karibaensis was an early sauropod from the uppermost Forest Sandstone Formation of Zimbabwe, known from one partial skeleton lacking skull and most of the neck.","facts":["Limbs, pelvis and tail establish an obligate quadrupedal sauropod.","Reassessment of the type locality makes it substantially older than the once-assumed Toarcian age, but does not yield a narrow radiometric date."]},"ageReviewNote":"Stratigraphic reassessment places the type in the uppermost Forest Sandstone Formation, probably Sinemurian-Pliensbachian; the exact numerical age remains unresolved.","residualUncertainty":["Formation age and exact position among early sauropods remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/40681962","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jafrearsci.2017.10.015","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/vulcanodon.html"]},"wuerhosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"exact stage and numerical age unresolved","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae"],"massKg":null,"description":"Wuerhosaurus homheni was an Early Cretaceous stegosaur from the Tugulu Group of Xinjiang, known from incomplete postcranial remains and distinctive low plate fragments.","facts":["No complete skeleton or skull is known for the type species.","The four-tonne point mass is cleared; proposed synonymy with Stegosaurus is not generally accepted."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Tugulu Group is Early Cretaceous, but correlations of the relevant unit remain too uncertain to support a narrow stage or numerical age.","residualUncertainty":["Species composition, formation age and plate shape remain incompletely known."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Dong_73.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201908002459"]},"yandusaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"exact stage and numerical age unresolved","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Neornithischia"],"description":"Yandusaurus hongheensis was a small neornithischian from the Shaximiao Formation near Honghe Dam, known from a partial skeleton with skull.","facts":["Yandusaurus multidens was transferred to Hexinlusaurus and should not expand Yandusaurus anatomy.","Published accounts disagree over whether the type belongs to the lower or upper member of the Shaximiao Formation.","Its exact position among early neornithischians varies."]},"ageReviewNote":"The type locality is near Honghe Dam in the Shaximiao Formation, but lower-versus-upper member assignment is disputed; no narrow stage or numerical age is defensible.","residualUncertainty":["The type needs modern comprehensive redescription and its member assignment remains unresolved.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/He_79.pdf","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4690359/","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yandusaurus.html"]},"yangchuanosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, about 160-157 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Tetanurae","Allosauroidea","Metriacanthosauridae"],"length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Yangchuanosaurus was a metriacanthosaurid from the Upper Shaximiao Formation, represented by substantial skulls and partial skeletons assigned to Y. shangyouensis and the possibly synonymous Y. magnus.","facts":["The type species is well represented but the catalogue 11 m and 3.4-tonne figures reflect the largest referred material, not every species.","Size fields are cleared pending species-specific treatment."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Upper Shaximiao occurrences are Oxfordian; the range should not extend through all 163-154 Ma.","residualUncertainty":["Y. magnus synonymy and species-specific size remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Dong_et_al._78.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1080/14772019.2011.630927"]},"yimenosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"exact stage and numerical age unresolved","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massopoda"],"description":"Yimenosaurus youngi was a basal sauropodomorph from the Fengjiahe Formation of Yunnan, reported from more than ten partial skeletons including substantial cranial material.","facts":["The reported sample is much larger than two skeletons and includes informative skull and jaw material.","The original account is brief and inadequately illustrated relative to the amount of material.","A modern first-hand redescription is needed before specimen-level completeness, diagnosis and adult size can be assessed securely."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Fengjiahe Formation is Early Jurassic, but the Yimenosaurus horizon is not dated well enough to justify a narrow stage or numerical range.","residualUncertainty":["Abundant material is reported, but the genus still lacks a modern comprehensive first-hand redescription and phylogenetic test.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.naturalhistory.si.edu/sites/default/files/media/translated_publications/Bai%26amp%3B_90_0.pdf","https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.252219","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yimenosaurus.html"]},"yingshanosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, approximately 162-157 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Thyreophora","Stegosauria","Stegosauridae"],"taxonomicStatus":"accepted","length":null,"massKg":null,"description":"Yingshanosaurus jichuanensis is a stegosaur from the Upper Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan, China, based on a partial skeleton with an incomplete skull and armour. It is not a nomen nudum: Zhu Songlin published a formal description in 1994, contrary to older English-language summaries. Its validity and exact relationships still need modern revision.","facts":["The holotype CV 00722 is a partial skeleton including incomplete cranial and postcranial material and dermal armour.","The name circulated informally before 1994, which explains the outdated nomen nudum label in some catalogues.","Zhu's 1994 paper supplied the formal genus and species description.","The striking shoulder-spine reconstruction and precise body dimensions require rechecking against the original specimen rather than copying exhibition mounts."]},"ageReviewNote":"The holotype is from the Upper Shaximiao Formation. Numerical ages for that terrestrial unit remain under revision, so the displayed Oxfordian range is approximate.","residualUncertainty":["The original description is difficult to access outside China and the specimen lacks a modern full redescription.","Its diagnostic status and precise position within Stegosauria require renewed study.","The age of the Upper Shaximiao Formation is still being refined."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.mindat.org/taxon-9735898.html","https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201908002459","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yingshanosaurus.html"]},"yinlong":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Jurassic","mya":"Oxfordian, about 160 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia"],"diet":null,"description":"Yinlong downsi was an early ceratopsian from the Shishugou Formation of Xinjiang, known from multiple skeletons including skulls.","facts":["Its skull combines early ornithischian features with a ceratopsian rostral bone.","Gastrolith-like stones occur with specimens, but exact diet remains broadly herbivorous or omnivorous rather than categorically established."]},"ageReviewNote":"The upper Shishugou type horizon is Oxfordian near 160 Ma, making the old Mid Jurassic label incorrect.","residualUncertainty":["Diet breadth and exact basal ceratopsian branching remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2006.3566","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yinlong.html"]},"yuanmousaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Mid Jurassic","mya":"exact stage and numerical age unresolved","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Sauropoda","Eusauropoda"],"description":"Yuanmousaurus jiangyiensis was a Chinese eusauropod from the Zhanghe Formation, based on one incomplete postcranial skeleton. Later comparison questioned whether its proposed diagnostic characters are unique.","facts":["The skull is absent and the neck is very incomplete.","The catalogue 17 m length is cleared because the incomplete specimen and uncertain validity do not support that precision."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Zhanghe Formation is Middle Jurassic but lacks a narrow, universally agreed stage or numerical date.","residualUncertainty":["Diagnostic validity, phylogeny, age and size need specialist reassessment.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.researchgate.net/publication/279556348_A_new_eusauropod_dinosaur_from_Yuanmou_of_Yunnan_Province_China","https://doi.org/10.4202/app.2012.0151","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yuanmousaurus.html"]},"yunnanosaurus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Jurassic","mya":"species-dependent Early Jurassic range; Y. youngi is probably Pliensbachian","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Sauropodomorpha","Massopoda"],"diet":"Herbivore","description":"Yunnanosaurus was a basal sauropodomorph from Early Jurassic Yunnan, known from multiple partial skeletons and skulls. The type species Y. huangi and the larger Y. youngi come from different units and must be treated separately.","facts":["Extensive tooth wear supports plant processing, so the catalogue omnivore label is replaced with herbivore.","Y. robustus is generally treated as a junior synonym of Y. huangi.","Sedimentological reassessment places Y. youngi in the Lower Jurassic Fengjiahe Formation, probably Pliensbachian, rather than the Middle Jurassic."]},"ageReviewNote":"Y. huangi is from the Lower Jurassic Lower Lufeng Formation, whereas Y. youngi is now placed in the Lower Jurassic Fengjiahe Formation and is probably Pliensbachian; one merged numerical genus range would conceal that species-level evidence.","residualUncertainty":["Species content and the precise ages of individual Lufeng horizons remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1096-3642.2007.00290.x","https://doi.org/10.1080/08912963.2021.1984445","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yunnanosaurus.html"]},"yutyrannus":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Barremian, about 125.8-124.1 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Saurischia","Theropoda","Coelurosauria","Tyrannosauroidea"],"description":"Yutyrannus huali was a large feathered tyrannosauroid from the Yixian Formation, known from three articulated individuals of different sizes.","facts":["Long filamentous feathers are directly preserved on multiple body regions.","The three associated individuals do not by themselves prove pack hunting or stable social groups.","Its position within Tyrannosauroidea varies among analyses, so a narrower family assignment is not presented as settled."]},"ageReviewNote":"High-precision dating brackets the Yixian Formation at 125.755-124.122 Ma, within the Barremian on the current time scale.","residualUncertainty":["Death-assemblage behaviour, exact feather coverage and finer tyrannosauroid placement remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10906","https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwab063","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/yutyrannus.html"]},"zalmoxes":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Maastrichtian, about 71-66 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Rhabdodontidae"],"description":"Zalmoxes was a rhabdodontid from Romania represented by two named species and many disarticulated skull and postcranial bones.","facts":["Z. robustus and Z. shqiperorum differ in size and anatomy and should not be merged.","Island dwarfism has been proposed, but size and growth differ between species and samples."]},"ageReviewNote":"Both species are Maastrichtian Hateg Basin; the single 69 Ma date is overprecise.","residualUncertainty":["Species-specific referrals and dwarfism magnitude remain debated."],"sourceUrls":["https://doi.org/10.1017/S1477201903001032","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0044318","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/zalmoxes.html"]},"zephyrosaurus":{"status":"audited","scientificDisposition":"needs-specialist-review","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Early Cretaceous","mya":"Aptian-Albian, about 115-105 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ornithopoda","Thescelosauridae"],"description":"Zephyrosaurus schaffi was a small ornithopod from the Cloverly Formation of Montana, based on a partial skull and sparse postcranial material.","facts":["Burrowing has been proposed from anatomy and trace fossils assigned to similar ornithopods, but no Zephyrosaurus skeleton is preserved inside a burrow.","Most body proportions and size are inferred."],"length":null},"ageReviewNote":"The Cloverly type is Aptian-Albian; the old 120-110 Ma interval is approximate.","residualUncertainty":["Generic validity, referrals and burrowing behaviour need renewed study.","Specialist confirmation is still required before this profile is marked primary-literature reviewed."],"sourceUrls":["https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/52032093","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/zephyrosaurus.html"]},"zuniceratops":{"status":"reviewed","scientificDisposition":"reviewed","reviewedOn":"2026-07-16","record":{"period":"Late Cretaceous","mya":"Turonian, about 92-90 million years ago","taxonomy":["Dinosauria","Ornithischia","Ceratopsia","Ceratopsoidea"],"description":"Zuniceratops christopheri was an early horned ceratopsoid from the Moreno Hill Formation of New Mexico, represented by several partial skulls and limited postcranial material.","facts":["It had well-developed brow horns but no large nasal horn.","The frill shows an early stage toward ceratopsid anatomy; it is close to, but outside, Ceratopsidae in most analyses."]},"ageReviewNote":"The Moreno Hill Formation is Turonian, near 92-90 Ma, not the full 94-89 Ma interval.","residualUncertainty":["Postcranial anatomy and exact position close to Ceratopsidae remain uncertain."],"sourceUrls":["https://nmdigital.unm.edu/digital/collection/bulletins/id/1139/","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0112055","https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/dino-directory/zuniceratops.html"]}};
